window.NOTES=[{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 1:1","word":"The book of the generation","title":"Family records kept on file","text":"Jewish families kept careful records of descent, and priestly families most of all. Josephus, writing about AD 95, traced his own line and said he copied it as he found it \"in the public records.\" He also claimed the priests had the names of their high priests \"from father to son\" for two thousand years.","src":["Josephus, Life 1 (sec. 1-6) | https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Life_of_Flavius_Josephus","Josephus, Against Apion 1.7 (1.36) | https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Against_Apion/Book_I"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 1:18","word":"espoused","title":"Betrothal, the first half of marriage","text":"Betrothal was the first, binding step of a Jewish marriage. The couple were legally husband and wife, and only a divorce could end it, which is why Joseph thinks of putting Mary \"away\" in the next verse. The bride usually stayed in her father's house. The Mishnah gives a virgin twelve months after betrothal to prepare for the wedding.","src":["Mishnah Kiddushin 1:1 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Kiddushin.1.1","Mishnah Ketubot 4:4 (a father receives the bill of divorce for a betrothed daughter) | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Ketubot.4.4","Mishnah Ketubot 5:2 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Ketubot.5.2"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 2:1","word":"Bethlehem","title":"Bethlehem, the house of bread","text":"Bethlehem is Hebrew for \"house of bread,\" a fitting name for a town in good grain country. Ruth gleaned barley in its fields, and David grew up there keeping his father's sheep. Jews of Jesus' day knew it as the city of David.","src":["Strong's H1035, Brown-Driver-Briggs | https://www.blueletterbible.org/lexicon/h1035/kjv/wlc/0-1/","Ruth 2:2-4, 23; 1 Samuel 16:1, 11; 17:15; Luke 2:4"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Luke 1:5","word":"of the course of Abia","title":"Twenty-four shifts of priests","text":"David divided the priests into twenty-four \"courses,\" or shifts, and Abia (Abijah) was the eighth. Josephus says each course served in the temple eight days, \"from sabbath to sabbath,\" then went home until its turn came again. Josephus came from the first course himself. When his week ended, Zacharias went back to his house in the hill country.","src":["1 Chronicles 24:1-19 (Abijah is the eighth lot, 24:10)","Josephus, Antiquities 7.14.7 (7.365-366) | https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Antiquities_of_the_Jews/Book_VII","Josephus, Life 1 (sec. 2) | https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Life_of_Flavius_Josephus"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Luke 1:59","word":"on the eighth day","title":"Circumcision, even on the sabbath","text":"The law set circumcision on the eighth day after birth (Leviticus 12:3), and the Mishnah says that when the eighth day fell on a sabbath, the circumcision went ahead anyway, with everything it needed. Jesus points to this in John 7:22-23: if a boy may be circumcised on the sabbath, a man may be healed on it.","src":["Leviticus 12:3; Genesis 17:12","Mishnah Shabbat 19:2 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Shabbat.19.2"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Luke 1:63","word":"His name is John","title":"John means \"Jehovah has been gracious\"","text":"John is Yohanan in Hebrew, and the lexicons give its meaning as \"Jehovah has graced\" or \"has been gracious.\" Families usually reused the names of relatives, which is why the neighbors object that \"there is none of thy kindred that is called by this name.\" The angel had chosen the name, and Zacharias wrote it down.","src":["Strong's H3110, Brown-Driver-Briggs | https://www.blueletterbible.org/lexicon/h3110/kjv/wlc/0-1/","Luke 1:13, 59-61"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Luke 2:7","word":"swaddling clothes","title":"Wrapped in swaddling bands","text":"Wrapping a newborn tightly in strips of cloth was ordinary care for a baby. Ezekiel describes an abandoned child as one who \"wast not swaddled at all.\" In the Wisdom of Solomon, the writer, speaking as King Solomon, says even he was \"nursed with care in swaddling cloths.\" Kings and poor children began life the same way.","src":["Ezekiel 16:4","Wisdom of Solomon 7:4-5 (RSV) | https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Wisdom+7%3A4&version=RSV"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Luke 2:8","word":"keeping watch over their flock by night","title":"Flocks near the tower of the flock","text":"The Mishnah says that any animal found straying between Jerusalem and Migdal Eder was presumed to belong to Jerusalem's offerings, and males were treated as burnt offerings. Migdal Eder means \"tower of the flock,\" and Genesis puts it near Bethlehem. The Mishnah is later than Luke, so it describes the region, not these shepherds.","src":["Mishnah Shekalim 7:4 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Shekalim.7.4","Genesis 35:19-21; Micah 4:8"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Luke 2:23","word":"Every male that openeth the womb","title":"Buying back the firstborn son","text":"Every firstborn son belonged to the Lord and was bought back from a priest for five shekels of silver (Numbers 18:15-16). The Mishnah says the five shekels were counted by the Tyrian standard, the purest silver coin in use. Luke cites the law but does not mention the payment.","src":["Exodus 13:2, 12-13; Numbers 18:15-16","Mishnah Bekhorot 8:7 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Bekhorot.8.7"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Luke 2:24","word":"A pair of turtledoves, or two young pigeons","title":"The offering of a poor family","text":"Forty days after a son was born, the mother brought a lamb and a bird to the temple. A family that could not afford a lamb could bring two birds instead (Leviticus 12:8), so Mary's offering was the one the law set for the poor. The temple had a collection chest marked for the price of these birds.","src":["Leviticus 12:2-8","Mishnah Shekalim 6:5 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Shekalim.6.5"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Luke 2:41","word":"every year at the feast of the passover","title":"Pilgrims to Jerusalem","text":"The law called every Israelite man to the temple at three feasts a year: Passover, Pentecost, and Tabernacles (Deuteronomy 16:16). The Mishnah lists who was excused, including women and young children. Mary went every year anyway, and the family brought Jesus with them.","src":["Deuteronomy 16:16","Mishnah Chagigah 1:1 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Chagigah.1.1"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Luke 2:42","word":"twelve years old","title":"Nearly of age at thirteen","text":"Jewish custom treated thirteen as the age when a boy became responsible for keeping the commandments himself. A saying in the Mishnah runs through the stages of life: \"at five years of age the study of Scripture... at thirteen subject to the commandments.\" At twelve, Jesus was one year short of that line.","src":["Mishnah Avot 5:21 | https://www.sefaria.org/Pirkei_Avot.5.21"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 3:4","word":"locusts","title":"Locusts, a lawful food","text":"Locusts were among the few insects the law allowed as food (Leviticus 11:22). The Damascus Document, a rulebook found in Cairo and among the Dead Sea Scrolls, even gives directions: locusts \"shall come into fire or into water whilst they are still living.\" John's food was plain wilderness fare, and it was clean.","src":["Leviticus 11:21-22","Damascus Document (CD) 12:14-15, trans. Charles (1913) | https://ccat.sas.upenn.edu/rak/courses/225/CD1910.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"John 1:27","word":"whose shoe’s latchet I am not worthy to unloose","title":"Untying sandals, a slave's job","text":"Untying a master's sandals was work for a slave. A rabbi quoted in the Talmud says a student should do for his teacher every task a slave does for his master, \"except for untying his shoe.\" John names the one task even a disciple was spared and says he is not worthy of it.","src":["Babylonian Talmud, Ketubot 96a (saying of Rabbi Joshua ben Levi) | https://www.sefaria.org/Ketubot.96a"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"John 1:39","word":"about the tenth hour","title":"Counting the hours from sunrise","text":"Jews counted twelve hours of daylight, starting about sunrise (John 11:9), so the tenth hour was around four in the afternoon. Josephus mentions the sixth hour, about noon, as the hour \"our laws require us to go to dinner on sabbath days.\" The hours in the Gospels are counted this way.","src":["John 11:9","Josephus, Life 54 (sec. 279) | https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Life_of_Flavius_Josephus"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"John 2:1","word":"there was a marriage in Cana of Galilee","title":"A wedding lasted a week","text":"A wedding was a feast that could last seven days. Samson's ran seven days (Judges 14:12), and in the book of Tobit, Tobias's marriage \"was celebrated for seven days with great festivity.\" Guests came and went, and the bridegroom answered for the food and wine, which is why the steward goes to him in verses 9-10.","src":["Judges 14:12, 17","Tobit 11:19 (RSV) | https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Tobit+11%3A18-19&version=RSV","John 2:9-10"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"John 2:6","word":"six waterpots of stone","title":"Why the jars were stone","text":"Under the purity laws, clay pots could become unclean and had to be broken, but stone vessels stayed clean. The Mishnah lists \"vessels of stone\" with the things that do not take on impurity. Archaeologists have found workshops near Jerusalem that made stone cups and large stone jars in Jesus' day.","src":["Leviticus 11:33","Mishnah Oholot 5:5 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Oholot.5.5","Mishnah Yadayim 1:2 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Yadayim.1.2","Yitzhak Magen, The Stone Vessel Industry in the Second Temple Period (Israel Exploration Society, 2002)"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 21:12","word":"the tables of the moneychangers","title":"Changing money for the temple tax","text":"Every Jewish man paid half a shekel a year for the temple (Exodus 30:13). The Mishnah says money changers set up tables around the country on the 15th of Adar, a month before Passover, and inside the temple from the 25th. Pilgrims exchanged their coins for the silver the temple accepted.","src":["Exodus 30:13-16","Mishnah Shekalim 1:3 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Shekalim.1.3"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Mark 11:15","word":"the seats of them that sold doves","title":"The price of doves","text":"Doves were the offering of the poor and of mothers after childbirth. The Mishnah tells of a time when a pair of birds in Jerusalem cost a gold denar. Rabban Simeon ben Gamaliel swore he would not sleep until the price fell, and he gave a ruling that cut how many birds women had to bring. That day the price dropped to a quarter of a silver denar.","src":["Leviticus 12:8; 15:29","Mishnah Keritot 1:7 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Keritot.1.7"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Mark 11:16","word":"carry any vessel through the temple","title":"No shortcuts through the temple","text":"The Mishnah forbids using the Temple Mount as a shortcut. It also forbids going up with a staff, shoes, or a money belt, or even with dust on one's feet. Jesus' rule against carrying goods through the temple courts has a close match in that law.","src":["Mishnah Berakhot 9:5 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Berakhot.9.5"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Mark 12:41","word":"cast money into the treasury","title":"Thirteen horn-shaped chests","text":"The temple had thirteen collection chests called \"horns,\" narrow at the top and wide at the bottom. Each was labeled for its use: new shekels, old shekels, birds, wood for the altar, frankincense, gold for the mercy seat, and six for freewill gifts.","src":["Mishnah Shekalim 6:1, 6:5 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Shekalim.6.5"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Mark 12:42","word":"two mites, which make a farthing","title":"The smallest coin","text":"The mite (Greek lepton) was a tiny bronze coin. Mark explains it for Roman readers: two made a quadrans, a quarter of the Roman as. That makes one mite an eighth of an as, the same as the perutah, which the Mishnah defines as \"one-eighth of an Italian issar\" and treats as the smallest sum the law counts.","src":["Mishnah Eduyot 4:7 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Eduyot.4.7","Mishnah Kiddushin 1:1 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Kiddushin.1.1"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Luke 4:16","word":"stood up for to read","title":"The sabbath reading","text":"On the sabbath, the Mishnah says, seven men read from the law of Moses in turn, and the reading closed with a passage from the Prophets. Each reading was put into Aramaic, a verse or a few verses at a time, so everyone could follow. Jesus is handed the scroll of Isaiah for that closing reading.","src":["Mishnah Megillah 4:2, 4:4 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Megillah.4.2","Luke 4:17"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Luke 4:20","word":"gave it again to the minister, and sat down","title":"The attendant, and sitting to teach","text":"The \"minister\" was the synagogue attendant, the hazzan, who kept the scrolls. The Mishnah describes the attendant taking a Torah scroll and handing it on to the head of the synagogue. Readers stood to read, and teachers sat to teach (Matthew 5:1; 23:2), so when Jesus sat down, the room waited for him to speak.","src":["Mishnah Sotah 7:7 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Sotah.7.7","Matthew 5:1-2; 23:2"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Luke 4:29","word":"cast him down headlong","title":"Thrown from a height","text":"In the Mishnah's procedure for stoning, the condemned man was first pushed off a height \"twice the height of an ordinary person.\" Stones followed only if the fall did not kill him. The crowd at Nazareth tries to throw Jesus from the brow of the hill, though they had held no trial.","src":["Mishnah Sanhedrin 6:4 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Sanhedrin.6.4"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Mark 1:32","word":"when the sun did set","title":"Waiting for the sabbath to end","text":"A Jewish day ran from sunset to sunset (Leviticus 23:32). This was a sabbath (Mark 1:21), and carrying anything from one place to another was one of the labors forbidden that day. When the sun went down, the sabbath was over, and the town could carry its sick to the door.","src":["Leviticus 23:32; Mark 1:21","Mishnah Shabbat 7:2 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Shabbat.7.2"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 12:1","word":"began to pluck the ears of corn","title":"Plucking grain on the sabbath","text":"The law let a hungry traveler pick ears of grain by hand in a neighbor's field (Deuteronomy 23:25). The complaint was the day. The Mishnah's list of thirty-nine kinds of work forbidden on the sabbath opens with sowing, plowing, reaping, and threshing, and rubbing grain out of the husk (Luke 6:1) could count as two of them.","src":["Deuteronomy 23:25","Mishnah Shabbat 7:2 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Shabbat.7.2"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 12:11","word":"if it fall into a pit on the sabbath day","title":"An animal in a pit","text":"Jewish groups disagreed about this case. The Damascus Document, a rulebook of the community behind the Dead Sea Scrolls, says that if an animal \"falls into a pit or ditch, he shall not raise it on the Sabbath.\" Jesus expects his hearers to pull their sheep out, so their practice was less strict.","src":["Damascus Document (CD) 11:13-14, trans. Charles (1913) | https://ccat.sas.upenn.edu/rak/courses/225/CD1910.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Mark 3:4","word":"to save life, or to kill?","title":"Saving life on the sabbath","text":"Jewish teachers agreed that danger to life set the sabbath aside. The Mishnah goes further: \"a case of uncertainty concerning a life-threatening situation overrides Shabbat.\" A withered hand was no danger to life, so the question was whether healing it could wait a day.","src":["Mishnah Yoma 8:6 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Yoma.8.6"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"John 5:10","word":"it is not lawful for thee to carry thy bed","title":"Carrying on the sabbath","text":"The last of the Mishnah's thirty-nine forbidden labors is carrying an object from one domain to another, such as from a house into the street. Jeremiah had said, \"bear no burden on the sabbath day\" (Jeremiah 17:21). A man walking through Jerusalem with his bedroll was breaking that rule where everyone could see.","src":["Jeremiah 17:21-22","Mishnah Shabbat 7:2 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Shabbat.7.2"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"John 9:6","word":"made clay of the spittle","title":"Making clay on the sabbath","text":"Kneading is one of the thirty-nine labors the Mishnah forbids on the sabbath, and mixing spittle and dust into clay could be counted as kneading. John adds that \"it was the sabbath day when Jesus made the clay\" (9:14). That is the charge the Pharisees bring against him.","src":["Mishnah Shabbat 7:2 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Shabbat.7.2","John 9:14-16"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 24:20","word":"neither on the sabbath day","title":"Travel limits on the sabbath","text":"Jewish practice limited how far a person could walk on the sabbath. The Mishnah sets the limit at two thousand cubits, a little over half a mile, from where one was; Acts 1:12 calls this \"a sabbath day's journey.\" Jesus' warning assumes his followers would still be keeping the sabbath when the time came.","src":["Mishnah Eruvin 4:3 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Eruvin.4.3","Acts 1:12"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Mark 7:3","word":"except they wash their hands oft","title":"Washing hands before meals","text":"Washing the hands before eating came from \"the tradition of the elders,\" not from the law of Moses. The Mishnah sets the amount: at least a quarter of a log of water, poured from a vessel. The Greek word behind \"oft\" can mean \"with the fist,\" which may describe how the hands were held while the water was poured.","src":["Mishnah Yadayim 1:1-2 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Yadayim.1.1","Strong's G4435 (pygme, \"the fist\") | https://www.blueletterbible.org/lexicon/g4435/kjv/tr/0-1/"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Mark 7:11","word":"It is Corban","title":"Corban, \"an offering\"","text":"Corban is the Hebrew word for an offering to God. Saying \"Corban\" over something put it off-limits to others, as if it belonged to the temple. A Jerusalem ossuary carries the formula: \"Every man that enjoys it, offering.\" The Mishnah later lets judges undo a vow when keeping it would dishonor a man's father and mother.","src":["Josephus, Against Apion 1.22 (1.167) | https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Against_Apion/Book_I","Mishnah Nedarim 1:2; 9:1 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Nedarim.9.1","Y. Billig, \"An Ossuary from Jerusalem Bearing 'Korban' Inscriptions,\" Cathedra 98 (2000) 49-60 | https://search.inscriptionsisraelpalestine.org/inscriptions/jeru0530"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 23:23","word":"mint and anise and cummin","title":"Tithing garden herbs","text":"The Mishnah's rule is that whatever is food, is looked after, and grows from the land owes a tithe. The KJV's \"anise\" is dill, and Rabbi Eliezer says dill must be tithed in seed, leaf, and pod. Kitchen herbs were worth little, and the Pharisees tithed them to the leaf.","src":["Mishnah Maasrot 1:1; 4:5 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Maasrot.4.5","Strong's G432 (anethon, \"anise, dill\") | https://www.blueletterbible.org/lexicon/g432/kjv/tr/0-1/"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Luke 11:42","word":"rue","title":"Rue, a plant exempt from tithes","text":"Luke's list has rue where Matthew has anise. The Mishnah names rue first among the plants that are \"exempt from tithes,\" along with goosefoot, purslane, and celery. A Pharisee who tithed his rue was paying more than the rabbis' own rule asked.","src":["Mishnah Sheviit 9:1 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Sheviit.9.1"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 23:5","word":"they make broad their phylacteries","title":"Phylacteries","text":"Phylacteries (tefillin) are small leather boxes holding passages of the law, bound on the forehead and arm to obey Deuteronomy 6:8. Josephus says the law has Jews \"bear on their forehead and their arm\" the wonders of God. The Mishnah says they held four passages, and one missing letter made them invalid.","src":["Deuteronomy 6:8; 11:18","Josephus, Antiquities 4.8.13 (4.213) | https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Antiquities_of_the_Jews/Book_IV","Mishnah Menachot 3:7 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Menachot.3.7"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 9:20","word":"touched the hem of his garment","title":"The fringe on his garment","text":"The Greek word for \"hem\" here is the same word translated \"borders\" in Matthew 23:5. It can mean the tassels, called tsitsit, that the law told Israelite men to wear on the corners of their garments, each with a blue cord (Numbers 15:38-39). Jesus wore them like any observant Jew.","src":["Numbers 15:38-39; Deuteronomy 22:12","Strong's G2899 (kraspedon) | https://www.blueletterbible.org/lexicon/g2899/kjv/tr/0-1/"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 23:27","word":"whited sepulchres","title":"Whitewashed graves","text":"Touching a grave made a person unclean for seven days (Numbers 19:16), so graves were marked with lime where travelers could see them. The Mishnah says graves were marked again every year on the 15th of Adar, a month before Passover, when pilgrims were about to fill the roads. Jesus' picture is a freshly whitened tomb.","src":["Numbers 19:16","Mishnah Maaser Sheni 5:1 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Maaser_Sheni.5.1","Mishnah Shekalim 1:1 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Shekalim.1.1"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 23:24","word":"strain at a gnat","title":"The gnat and the camel","text":"Both the gnat and the camel were unclean under the law (Leviticus 11:4, 20-23). The KJV's \"strain at\" means \"strain out,\" as wine was poured through a cloth to catch an insect. Jesus sets the smallest unclean creature beside the largest one in the land.","src":["Leviticus 11:4, 20-23, 41-42","Strong's G1368 (diulizo, \"strain out\") | https://www.blueletterbible.org/lexicon/g1368/kjv/tr/0-1/"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 5:18","word":"one jot or one tittle","title":"Jot and tittle","text":"The jot is yod, the smallest letter in the Hebrew alphabet, hardly more than a stroke. The tittle is the Greek keraia, \"little horn,\" the small hook or point that tells one Hebrew letter from another of nearly the same shape. Jesus names the smallest marks a scribe could make.","src":["Strong's G2503 (iota) and G2762 (keraia) | https://www.blueletterbible.org/lexicon/g2762/kjv/tr/0-1/"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 5:22","word":"Raca","title":"Raca, \"empty head\"","text":"Raca comes from the Aramaic reqa, \"empty,\" and was said of a person thought to be empty-headed and worthless. The Jewish Encyclopedia notes the word several times in the Talmud as a term of contempt. It was an everyday insult, the kind thrown in an argument.","src":["Jewish Encyclopedia (1906), \"Raca (Reka),\" by C. H. Toy and J. Z. Lauterbach | https://www.jewishencyclopedia.com/articles/12518-raca-reka","Strong's G4469 | https://www.blueletterbible.org/lexicon/g4469/kjv/tr/0-1/"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 5:24","word":"first be reconciled to thy brother","title":"Make peace before the offering","text":"The Mishnah teaches the same order for the Day of Atonement. Sins between a person and God are atoned for by the day, it says, but sins against another person are not \"until he appeases the other person.\" Both put peace with one's neighbor ahead of what is offered at the temple.","src":["Mishnah Yoma 8:9 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Yoma.8.9"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 5:31","word":"a writing of divorcement","title":"The bill of divorce","text":"The bill of divorce, called a get, was the woman's proof that she was free. The Mishnah gives its essential words: \"You are hereby permitted to marry any man.\" With that paper in hand, she could marry again (Deuteronomy 24:1-2).","src":["Deuteronomy 24:1-2","Mishnah Gittin 9:3 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Gittin.9.3"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 19:3","word":"for every cause","title":"Shammai, Hillel, and divorce","text":"\"For every cause\" was a live argument. The schools of Shammai and Hillel read Deuteronomy 24:1 differently. Shammai's school allowed divorce only for sexual sin. Hillel's allowed it for a small fault, even if the wife burned his food. The Pharisees were asking Jesus which side he was on.","src":["Mishnah Gittin 9:10 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Gittin.9.10"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 6:5","word":"pray standing","title":"The standing prayer","text":"The main Jewish prayer, said at set times each day, was prayed standing; it later took the name Amidah, \"standing.\" The Mishnah says a man in the middle of it should not stop even \"if the king greets him.\" Someone who began it at a busy corner would stand there in full view until he finished.","src":["Mishnah Berakhot 4:1; 5:1 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Berakhot.5.1"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Luke 18:12","word":"I fast twice in the week","title":"Two fast days a week","text":"The law of Moses set one yearly fast, the Day of Atonement (Leviticus 16:29). Pious Jews added more. The Didache, a Christian manual from about AD 100, tells believers not to fast with \"the hypocrites,\" who fast on Monday and Thursday, but on Wednesday and Friday instead.","src":["Leviticus 16:29-31","Didache 8:1 | https://cts.perseids.org/read/greekLit/tlg1311/tlg001/perseus-eng1/7.4-8.1"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 6:17","word":"anoint thine head, and wash thy face","title":"No washing on a fast day","text":"On fast days Jews went without washing and without oil on the skin. The Mishnah lists bathing and \"smearing oil on one's body\" among the things forbidden on the Day of Atonement, along with food and drink. Jesus tells his hearers to wash and anoint as on any ordinary day, so no one can tell they are fasting.","src":["Mishnah Yoma 8:1 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Yoma.8.1"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 7:3","word":"the beam that is in thine own eye","title":"The splinter and the beam","text":"The rabbis knew this picture too. In the Talmud, Rabbi Tarfon says he doubts anyone in his day can take a rebuke: tell a man \"Remove the splinter from between your eyes,\" and he answers, \"Remove the beam from between your eyes.\" Tarfon taught a generation or two after Jesus.","src":["Babylonian Talmud, Arakhin 16b | https://www.sefaria.org/Arakhin.16b"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 13:31","word":"a grain of mustard seed","title":"The mustard seed as \"tiny\"","text":"The mustard seed was a common Jewish way of saying \"the smallest amount.\" The Mishnah uses \"the size of a mustard seed\" for the tiniest quantity in its purity laws. Jesus uses the seed the same way when he speaks of faith \"as a grain of mustard seed\" (Matthew 17:20).","src":["Mishnah Niddah 5:2 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Niddah.5.2","Matthew 17:20"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 14:19","word":"he blessed, and brake","title":"The blessing over bread","text":"A Jewish meal began when the head of the table said a blessing over the bread and broke it. The Mishnah gives the words for bread: \"Blessed are You, Lord... who brings forth bread from the earth.\" Here Jesus takes the place of the head of the table for a crowd of thousands.","src":["Mishnah Berakhot 6:1 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Berakhot.6.1"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"John 6:9","word":"five barley loaves","title":"Barley, the poor man's bread","text":"Barley was the cheaper grain. When Elisha promised that a famine would end, the price he named was \"a measure of fine flour\" for a shekel, but \"two measures of barley\" for the same shekel (2 Kings 7:1). Five barley loaves were ordinary food for a boy from a family of modest means.","src":["2 Kings 7:1, 16; Revelation 6:6"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 17:24","word":"Doth not your master pay tribute?","title":"The temple tax","text":"The \"tribute\" here is the temple tax, the didrachma or two-drachma coin, equal to the half-shekel every Jewish man paid each year for the temple (Exodus 30:13). The coin Peter finds in the fish, a stater, was worth four drachmas, enough for two men: \"for me and thee.\"","src":["Exodus 30:13-16","Mishnah Shekalim 1:1, 1:3 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Shekalim.1.3"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 18:21","word":"till seven times?","title":"How many times to forgive","text":"Peter's seven was generous by one rabbinic standard. In the Talmud, Rabbi Yose bar Yehuda teaches that a person who repeats a sin is forgiven the first, second, and third time, \"but the fourth time, he is not forgiven,\" citing Amos 2:6. Seven more than doubles that.","src":["Babylonian Talmud, Yoma 86b | https://www.sefaria.org/Yoma.86b"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 18:24","word":"ten thousand talents","title":"A debt of ten thousand talents","text":"A talent was a weight of silver worth thousands of denarii, and one denarius was a laborer's pay for a day (Matthew 20:2). Josephus says Galilee and Perea together paid Herod Antipas two hundred talents a year in tribute. The servant owes fifty times a whole region's yearly tax.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 17.11.4 (17.318) | https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Antiquities_of_the_Jews/Book_XVII","Matthew 20:2"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"John 12:13","word":"Took branches of palm trees","title":"Palm branches for a victory","text":"Jews carried palm branches in celebration. When Simon Maccabeus took the citadel of Jerusalem in 141 BC, the people went in \"with praise and palm branches.\" When Judas Maccabeus cleansed the temple, the people carried \"fronds of palm\" and sang hymns, keeping the feast \"in the manner of the feast of booths.\"","src":["1 Maccabees 13:51 (RSV) | https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=1+Maccabees+13%3A51&version=RSV","2 Maccabees 10:6-7 (RSV) | https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=2+Maccabees+10%3A6-7&version=RSV"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 22:3","word":"sent forth his servants to call them that were bidden","title":"Invited twice","text":"A great feast came with two invitations: one ahead of time, and a second, by servants, when the meal was ready. Haman was invited to Esther's banquet, then fetched when it was time (Esther 5:8; 6:14). A later midrash praises the people of Jerusalem because none of them \"would attend a feast until he was invited twice.\"","src":["Esther 5:8; 6:14","Lamentations Rabbah 4:2 | https://www.sefaria.org/Eichah_Rabbah.4.2"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 25:6","word":"go ye out to meet him","title":"The wedding procession","text":"A wedding included a public procession. In 1 Maccabees, a bride is brought with \"a large escort,\" and \"the bridegroom came out with his friends and his brothers to meet them with tambourines and musicians.\" The ten young women in the parable are waiting to join a procession like that.","src":["1 Maccabees 9:37-39 (RSV) | https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=1+Maccabees+9%3A37-39&version=RSV"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 9:15","word":"the children of the bridechamber","title":"The bridegroom's friends","text":"\"Children of the bridechamber\" means the groom's friends and attendants. Jewish law excused them from some duties during the wedding week. The Talmud says the groom and his groomsmen are exempt from living in the booth at Tabernacles \"for all seven days of the wedding celebration,\" because they are there to rejoice.","src":["Babylonian Talmud, Sukkah 25b | https://www.sefaria.org/Sukkah.25b","Mishnah Berakhot 2:5 (the groom excused from the Shema on his wedding night) | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Berakhot.2.5"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"John 3:29","word":"the friend of the bridegroom","title":"The friend of the bridegroom","text":"The friend of the bridegroom, the shoshbin in later Hebrew, had a real role in the wedding. The Mishnah mentions \"gifts of groomsmen,\" given to help pay for the feast and repaid later in turn. It also bars a groomsman from acting as a judge in his friend's case, since he loves him too well.","src":["Mishnah Bava Batra 9:4 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Bava_Batra.9.4","Mishnah Sanhedrin 3:5 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Sanhedrin.3.5"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Luke 7:32","word":"We have piped unto you","title":"Playing wedding and funeral","text":"Children in the market copied the two loudest events in village life: weddings, with flutes and dancing, and funerals, with wailing. Hired flute players came to both (see Matthew 9:23). Jesus' generation is like children who will play neither game, and he applies it to how they treated John and himself.","src":["Mishnah Ketubot 4:4 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Ketubot.4.4","1 Maccabees 9:39; Matthew 9:23"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 9:23","word":"the minstrels and the people making a noise","title":"Hired mourners and flutes","text":"Mourning was loud and public, and families hired help for it. The Mishnah says even \"the poorest man\" in Israel must provide \"no fewer than two flutes and a lamenting woman\" for his wife's funeral. Jairus was a synagogue ruler, so the flute players and mourners were already at his house when Jesus arrived.","src":["Mishnah Ketubot 4:4 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Ketubot.4.4"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Luke 7:12","word":"there was a dead man carried out","title":"Burial on the day of death","text":"Jews buried their dead quickly, usually the same day. The Mishnah says a man who leaves his dead relative unburied overnight breaks a commandment, unless he is waiting for a coffin or shroud. The body was carried out of town on a bier, with family and neighbors following, and the bearers were excused from saying the Shema.","src":["Mishnah Sanhedrin 6:5 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Sanhedrin.6.5","Mishnah Berakhot 3:1 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Berakhot.3.1"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"John 11:39","word":"he hath been dead four days","title":"Past the third day","text":"Jewish law treated three days as the limit for knowing a body by sight. The Mishnah lets a witness testify that a man has died only if he saw the body within three days, \"since the appearance may change due to decomposition.\" By the fourth day, no one could doubt that Lazarus was dead.","src":["Mishnah Yevamot 16:3 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Yevamot.16.3"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"John 11:19","word":"to comfort them concerning their brother","title":"Seven days of mourning","text":"Mourning lasted a week. Joseph mourned his father Jacob seven days (Genesis 50:10), and Ben Sira writes, \"Mourning for the dead lasts seven days.\" Friends came to the house during that week to sit with the family, which is why visitors from Jerusalem were still with Martha and Mary when Jesus came.","src":["Genesis 50:10","Sirach 22:12 (RSV) | https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Sirach+22%3A12&version=RSV"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"John 11:55","word":"to purify themselves","title":"Getting clean before Passover","text":"A person made unclean by contact with the dead needed seven days and two sprinklings of purification water before he could eat the Passover (Numbers 19:11-19; 9:6-10). So pilgrims came up early. A month before, the graves along the roads had been freshly whitewashed so travelers would not touch one by accident.","src":["Numbers 9:6-10; 19:11-19","Mishnah Shekalim 1:1 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Shekalim.1.1"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"John 13:5","word":"began to wash the disciples’ feet","title":"Washing a guest's feet","text":"Guests came in from dusty roads in sandals, and someone brought water for their feet. Abraham did it for his three visitors (Genesis 18:4). Abigail, offered marriage to King David, said she was ready to be a servant \"to wash the feet of the servants of my lord\" (1 Samuel 25:41). It was a servant's work.","src":["Genesis 18:4; 19:2; 1 Samuel 25:41"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"John 13:23","word":"leaning on Jesus’ bosom","title":"Reclining at the table","text":"At formal meals and the Passover, diners lay on couches, leaning on the left elbow with their feet behind them. The Mishnah says even the poorest Jew must not eat the Passover meal \"until he reclines... as free and wealthy people recline.\" Lying this way, the disciple beside Jesus had his head near Jesus' chest.","src":["Mishnah Pesachim 10:1 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Pesachim.10.1"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Luke 22:17","word":"he took the cup","title":"Cups at the Passover table","text":"The Mishnah requires four cups of wine at the Passover meal, and says the charity fund must give even a poor man no fewer than four. Luke mentions one cup before the bread and another \"after supper\" (22:20). The Mishnah's order of the meal comes from about AD 200, so it shows the meal's later shape.","src":["Mishnah Pesachim 10:1, 10:7 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Pesachim.10.1"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 26:30","word":"when they had sung an hymn","title":"Singing the Hallel","text":"The Passover meal closed with singing. The Mishnah says the leader \"completes hallel\" over the fourth cup, having sung its first part before the meal. The Hallel is Psalms 113-118. The hymn sung before the walk to the Mount of Olives was most likely from these psalms.","src":["Mishnah Pesachim 10:7 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Pesachim.10.7","Psalms 113-118"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 26:17","word":"the first day of the feast of unleavened bread","title":"Searching out the leaven","text":"Before Passover, every bit of leaven had to be out of the house (Exodus 12:15, 19). The Mishnah says that on the night of the 14th of Nisan, each household searched the house for leavened bread \"by candlelight.\" The next afternoon the Passover lambs were killed at the temple.","src":["Exodus 12:15-20","Mishnah Pesachim 1:1 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Pesachim.1.1"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Mark 14:12","word":"when they killed the passover","title":"A quarter million lambs","text":"Josephus says the Passover lambs were killed between the ninth and the eleventh hour, about 3 to 5 in the afternoon, with at least ten people to a lamb. One year the priests counted 256,500 lambs. The Mishnah says worshipers entered the temple court in three groups, and priests stood in rows passing the blood to the altar in silver and gold bowls.","src":["Josephus, Jewish War 6.9.3 (6.423-424) | https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_War_of_the_Jews/Book_VI","Mishnah Pesachim 5:5 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Pesachim.5.5"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"John 18:28","word":"lest they should be defiled","title":"A Gentile house was unclean","text":"The Mishnah states that \"the dwelling-places of non-Jews are unclean.\" Going into the Roman governor's headquarters would have made the priests unclean, and an unclean person could not eat the Passover (Numbers 9:6). Pilate had to come out to them.","src":["Mishnah Oholot 18:7 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Oholot.18.7","Numbers 9:6; John 18:29"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Mark 5:41","word":"Talitha cumi","title":"Words kept in Aramaic","text":"Mark keeps a few of Jesus' words in Aramaic, the everyday language of Galilee: Talitha cumi here, and Ephphatha, Abba, and Eloi elsewhere. Talitha means \"little girl\" or \"maiden,\" and cumi means \"arise.\" Mark translates each for readers who did not know Aramaic.","src":["Strong's G5008 (talitha) | https://www.blueletterbible.org/lexicon/g5008/kjv/tr/0-1/","Strong's G2188 (ephphatha) | https://www.blueletterbible.org/lexicon/g2188/kjv/tr/0-1/"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 16:17","word":"Simon Bar-jona","title":"Bar, \"son of\"","text":"Bar is Aramaic for \"son,\" so Bar-jona means \"son of Jona.\" Jews were often known by their father's name. The same word gives Bartimaeus, Bartholomew, and Barabbas. In John 1:42 Jesus calls Peter \"Simon the son of Jona,\" the same name in Greek.","src":["Strong's G920 (Barjona) | https://www.blueletterbible.org/lexicon/g920/kjv/tr/0-1/","John 1:42; Mark 10:46"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Luke 9:59","word":"suffer me first to go and bury my father","title":"The duty to bury a father","text":"Burying a parent was one of the most binding duties in Jewish life. The Mishnah excuses a person whose dead relative lies unburied before him from the Shema, from prayer, and from putting on phylacteries until the burial is done. No one in the crowd would have thought this man's request strange.","src":["Mishnah Berakhot 3:1 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Berakhot.3.1","Tobit 4:3-4 (RSV) | https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Tobit+4%3A3-4&version=RSV"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Luke 14:26","word":"hate not his father","title":"\"Hate\" as \"love less\"","text":"In Hebrew speech, \"hate\" can mean \"love less.\" Genesis says Jacob \"loved also Rachel more than Leah,\" and the next verse says Leah \"was hated\" (Genesis 29:30-31). Matthew gives the same saying as \"He that loveth father or mother more than me is not worthy of me\" (Matthew 10:37).","src":["Genesis 29:30-31; Deuteronomy 21:15-17; Matthew 10:37"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Luke 15:12","word":"give me the portion of goods that falleth to me","title":"The younger son's share","text":"Under the law, the firstborn son received a double share (Deuteronomy 21:17), so with two sons the younger would get one third. Property normally passed when the father died. Ben Sira warns a father not to hand over his property while he lives, but \"in the hour of death, distribute your inheritance.\"","src":["Deuteronomy 21:17","Sirach 33:19-23 (RSV) | https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Sirach+33%3A19-23&version=RSV"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Luke 15:15","word":"to feed swine","title":"Feeding pigs","text":"Pigs were unclean under the law (Leviticus 11:7), and the Mishnah says flatly that \"one may not raise pigs anywhere.\" A Jewish listener would hear that the young man had gone to a Gentile country and taken work from a Gentile, tending animals he could not eat or even raise at home.","src":["Leviticus 11:7","Mishnah Bava Kamma 7:7 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Bava_Kamma.7.7"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Luke 10:31","word":"there came down a certain priest","title":"Priests and the dead","text":"Priests were forbidden to make themselves unclean by touching the dead, except for close family (Leviticus 21:1-3), and the man lay \"half dead.\" Yet the Mishnah teaches that even a high priest must bury an abandoned body if no one else is there to do it.","src":["Leviticus 21:1-3, 11","Mishnah Nazir 7:1 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Nazir.7.1"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 27:6","word":"to put them into the treasury","title":"The Corban treasury","text":"The word for treasury here is korbanas, built from Corban, the temple's sacred fund. Josephus says Pilate later spent \"that sacred treasure which is called Corban\" on an aqueduct, and the people of Jerusalem rose up against him. Money paid for a man's death could not go into it.","src":["Josephus, Jewish War 2.9.4 (2.175) | https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_War_of_the_Jews/Book_II","Deuteronomy 23:18"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 26:65","word":"the high priest rent his clothes","title":"Tearing garments at blasphemy","text":"Tearing one's clothes was a sign of grief or horror. The Mishnah says that when a witness in a blasphemy trial repeated what he had heard, the judges \"stand on their feet and make a tear in their garments,\" and never fully sewed it closed. The law forbade the high priest to tear his clothes in mourning (Leviticus 21:10).","src":["Mishnah Sanhedrin 7:5 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Sanhedrin.7.5","Leviticus 21:10"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 26:59","word":"all the council","title":"Rules for a capital trial","text":"The Mishnah's rules for trials that could end in death say the court judges only by day. It may acquit on the same day but must wait until the next day to condemn, and it does not hear such cases on the eve of a sabbath or festival. Scholars disagree whether these rules were in force in Jesus' day.","src":["Mishnah Sanhedrin 4:1 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Sanhedrin.4.1"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Mark 15:23","word":"wine mingled with myrrh","title":"A drink for the condemned","text":"There was a Jewish custom of giving a drink to a man led out to die. The Talmud says the court gave him frankincense in a cup of wine \"to confuse his mind,\" citing Proverbs 31:6, and that \"the prominent women of Jerusalem\" donated it. The Talmud names frankincense; Mark names myrrh.","src":["Babylonian Talmud, Sanhedrin 43a | https://www.sefaria.org/Sanhedrin.43a","Proverbs 31:6"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"John 19:40","word":"as the manner of the Jews is to bury","title":"Jewish burial customs","text":"Jews washed the body, anointed it, and wrapped it in linen with spices; the Mishnah allows the washing and anointing even on the sabbath. Nicodemus brought about a hundred Roman pounds of myrrh and aloes (19:39), about seventy-five of our pounds. Second Chronicles describes King Asa laid in a bed \"filled with sweet odours and divers kinds of spices.\"","src":["Mishnah Shabbat 23:5 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Shabbat.23.5","Strong's G3046 (litra, 12 ounces) | https://www.blueletterbible.org/lexicon/g3046/kjv/tr/0-1/","2 Chronicles 16:14"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Mark 16:3","word":"Who shall roll us away the stone","title":"The stone at the door","text":"Most Jewish tombs near Jerusalem were closed with a square block pushed into the doorway like a cork. In a survey of more than 900 tombs from this period, the archaeologist Amos Kloner found only four closed by round, rolling stones, and those belonged to the wealthiest families. Joseph of Arimathea was \"a rich man\" (Matthew 27:57).","src":["Amos Kloner, \"Did a Rolling Stone Close Jesus' Tomb?\" Biblical Archaeology Review 25:5 (1999) | https://www.biblicalarchaeology.org/daily/archaeology-today/biblical-archaeology-topics/how-was-jesus-tomb-sealed/"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 27:51","word":"the veil of the temple was rent in twain","title":"The veil of the temple","text":"The Mishnah says two curtains, a cubit apart, separated the holy place from the holy of holies, though one rabbi held there was only one. Josephus describes the temple's great curtain as \"a Babylonian curtain, embroidered with blue, and fine linen, and scarlet, and purple.\"","src":["Mishnah Yoma 5:1 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Yoma.5.1","Josephus, Jewish War 5.5.4 (5.212) | https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_War_of_the_Jews/Book_V"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"John 15:1","word":"I am the true vine","title":"The golden vine on the temple","text":"Over the entrance to the temple sanctuary hung a golden vine, \"with its branches hanging down from a great height,\" Josephus says. The Mishnah adds that people gave golden leaves, grapes, and clusters to hang on it, and once three hundred priests were needed to clear it.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 15.11.3 (15.395) | https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Antiquities_of_the_Jews/Book_XV","Mishnah Middot 3:8 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Middot.3.8"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"John 7:37","word":"that great day of the feast","title":"Water poured on the altar","text":"Each morning of Tabernacles, the Mishnah says, a priest filled a golden jug with water from the pool of Siloam and poured it out at the altar. At night the water-drawing was celebrated with music and dancing; \"one who did not see\" it \"never saw celebration in his days.\" On the last day of this feast, Jesus offers water to anyone thirsty.","src":["Mishnah Sukkah 4:9; 5:1 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Sukkah.4.9"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"John 8:12","word":"I am the light of the world","title":"The great lamps of Tabernacles","text":"At the same feast, the Mishnah says, tall golden lampstands stood in the Women's Court, each with four bowls of oil filled by young priests on ladders. Wicks were cut from the priests' worn-out clothes, and \"there was not a courtyard in Jerusalem\" their light did not reach. John places this saying \"in the treasury, as he taught in the temple\" (8:20).","src":["Mishnah Sukkah 5:2-3 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Sukkah.5.2"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"John 10:22","word":"the feast of the dedication","title":"The feast of dedication, Hanukkah","text":"The feast of the dedication is Hanukkah. It remembers Judas Maccabeus cleansing the temple in 164 BC after a Greek king had defiled it. 1 Maccabees says the feast was kept for eight days \"beginning with the twenty-fifth day of the month of Chislev,\" in early winter. Josephus says it was called \"Lights.\"","src":["1 Maccabees 4:52-59 (RSV) | https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=1+Maccabees+4%3A56-59&version=RSV","Josephus, Antiquities 12.7.7 (12.325) | https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Antiquities_of_the_Jews/Book_XII"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Luke 5:14","word":"shew thyself to the priest","title":"Only a priest could say \"clean\"","text":"The law put the leper's case in a priest's hands (Leviticus 13-14). The Mishnah says anyone could look at the skin, but \"only a priest may declare them unclean or clean.\" A leper had to live apart from others (Leviticus 13:46), so the healed man needed the priest's word to go home.","src":["Leviticus 13:45-46; 14:1-32","Mishnah Negaim 3:1 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Negaim.3.1"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 23:7","word":"Rabbi, Rabbi","title":"Rabbi, \"my master\"","text":"Rabbi means \"my great one\" or \"my master,\" a title of honor used in speaking to a teacher. John translates it for his readers as \"Master\" (John 1:38), and Mary Magdalene uses a stronger form, Rabboni, at the tomb (John 20:16).","src":["Strong's G4461 (rhabbi) | https://www.blueletterbible.org/lexicon/g4461/kjv/tr/0-1/","John 1:38; 20:16"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Mark 15:42","word":"the day before the sabbath","title":"Buried before sundown","text":"The law said a hanged man's body must not stay up overnight (Deuteronomy 21:23). Josephus writes that Jews took such care over burial \"that they took down those that were condemned and crucified, and buried them before the going down of the sun.\" With the sabbath starting at sundown, there were only a few hours.","src":["Deuteronomy 21:22-23","Josephus, Jewish War 4.5.2 (4.317) | https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_War_of_the_Jews/Book_IV"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"hebrew_culture","ref":"Matt 22:24","word":"his brother shall marry his wife","title":"The brother-in-law's duty","text":"The law of Deuteronomy 25:5-10 told a man to marry his brother's childless widow so the dead brother's name would go on. A brother who refused went before the elders, and the widow pulled off his shoe and spat before him. The Mishnah describes this ceremony, called halitzah, step by step.","src":["Deuteronomy 25:5-10; Ruth 4:7-10","Mishnah Yevamot 12:6 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Yevamot.12.6"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Luke 1:5","word":"Herod, the king of Judæa","title":"A king made in Rome","text":"Herod the Great was from Idumea, the land south of Judea. He got his crown in Rome, where the Roman senate declared him king of the Jews. A rival called him \"a half Jew\" to his face. He reigned 37 years from the day Rome named him king, and he spent much of it rebuilding the country.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 14.14.4–5 (made king by the senate) | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/ant-14.htm","Josephus, Antiquities 14.15.2 (\"an Idumean, i.e. a half Jew\") and 17.8.1 (length of reign)"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Matt 2:1","word":"Bethlehem of Judaea","title":"Bethlehem and Herod's fortress","text":"Bethlehem sits about five miles south of Jerusalem in the Judean hills. A few miles away, on a hilltop where he had once beaten his enemies in battle, Herod built Herodium, a palace and fortress named for himself. Later he chose it as the place he would be buried.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 14.13.9 (Herodium built where he won the battle); Jewish War 1.13.8 (\"called it, from his own name, Herodium\") | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/ant-14.htm","Josephus, Antiquities 17.8.3 (\"there by his own command he was to be buried\")","Encyclopaedia Britannica, \"Bethlehem\" (5 miles south of Jerusalem) | https://www.britannica.com/place/Bethlehem"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Matt 2:16","word":"slew all the children","title":"A king who killed his own sons","text":"Herod had his wife Mariamne executed. He had two of their sons strangled, and he ordered his eldest son killed five days before his own death. A Roman writer around AD 400 tells of a joke made by Emperor Augustus about him: it was better to be Herod's pig than Herod's son.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 15.7.4–5 (Mariamne); Jewish War 1.27.6 (sons strangled at Sebaste); Antiquities 17.8.1 (Antipater, five days) | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/war-1.htm","Macrobius, Saturnalia 2.4.11 (\"Melius est Herodis porcum esse quam filium\") | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/L/Roman/Texts/Macrobius/Saturnalia/2*.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Matt 2:19","word":"when Herod was dead","title":"Herod's funeral and tomb","text":"Herod died at his winter palace in Jericho. His body was carried in a long procession, with soldiers and servants bearing spices, to Herodium near Bethlehem, where he had asked to be buried. In 2007 the archaeologist Ehud Netzer found what he identified as Herod's tomb on the hill's slope.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 17.8.3 (procession to Herodium) | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/ant-17.htm","Israel Ministry of Foreign Affairs, \"Tomb of King Herod discovered at Herodium,\" 8 May 2007 | https://embassies.gov.il/MFA/IsraelExperience/history/Pages/Tomb%20of%20King%20Herod%20discovered%2008-May-2007.aspx"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Matt 2:22","word":"Archelaus did reign in Judaea","title":"Why Joseph feared Archelaus","text":"When Herod died, his son Archelaus took Judea. At his first Passover a crowd protested, and his cavalry killed about three thousand people around the temple. His rule stayed harsh, and after about ten years the Jews and Samaritans complained to Augustus, who banished him to Vienne in Gaul.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 17.9.3 (three thousand killed) | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/ant-17.htm","Josephus, Antiquities 17.13.2; Jewish War 2.7.3 (banished to Vienna in Gaul, \"the ninth year of his government\") | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/war-2.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"John 1:46","word":"Can there any good thing come out of Nazareth?","title":"A village nobody had written about","text":"Nazareth never appears in the Old Testament, in Josephus, or in the Mishnah. The oldest Jewish mention found so far is a stone inscription from Caesarea, dug up in 1962, that lists where the priestly families lived. It dates to about AD 300 to 500 and names Nazareth as one of those towns.","src":["M. Avi-Yonah, \"A List of Priestly Courses from Caesarea,\" Israel Exploration Journal 12 (1962), 137–139","Inscriptions of Israel/Palestine, Caes0684 | https://search.inscriptionsisraelpalestine.org/inscriptions/caes0684"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Luke 2:7","word":"no room for them in the inn","title":"An inn, or a guest room?","text":"Luke's word for \"inn\" is kataluma. He uses it again for the \"guestchamber\" where the Last Supper was eaten. Kenneth Bailey argued it means a family's guest room. Village houses often kept animals at night in a lower area at one end of the main room, with mangers built into the floor.","src":["Kenneth E. Bailey, \"The Manger and the Inn: The Cultural Background of Luke 2:7,\" Theological Review (Near East School of Theology), Nov. 1979; repr. Evangelical Review of Theology 4:2 (1980) | https://gospelstudies.org.uk/biblicalstudies/pdf/evangelical-review-of-theology/ert_04-2_bailey.pdf"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Luke 2:44","word":"a day’s journey","title":"Three days from Galilee","text":"Galilean families walked to Jerusalem for the feasts in large groups of relatives and neighbors, which is how a boy could be missing for a day. The quick road ran through Samaria. Josephus says that by that road a traveler could go from Galilee to Jerusalem in three days.","src":["Josephus, Life 52 (§269), \"in three days' time, go from Galilee to Jerusalem\" | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/autobiog.htm","Josephus, Antiquities 20.6.1 (Galileans travel through Samaria to the festivals) | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/ant-20.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Mark 6:3","word":"the carpenter","title":"A builder near a building boom","text":"The Greek word here, tekton, means a craftsman who builds, in wood or stone. About four miles from Nazareth stood Sepphoris, which Herod Antipas walled and made the capital of Galilee during Jesus' youth. Some scholars think Joseph and Jesus found work there. The Gospels never mention the city.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 18.2.1 (Antipas walls Sepphoris, \"the metropolis of the country\") | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/ant-18.htm","Richard A. Batey, Jesus and the Forgotten City (Baker, 1991); Jonathan Reed interview, Christian Century | https://www.christiancentury.org/node/12183"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Luke 3:1","word":"Herod being tetrarch of Galilee","title":"Herod's kingdom cut in pieces","text":"After Herod the Great died, Augustus split his kingdom among three sons. Archelaus got Judea, Antipas got Galilee and Perea, and Philip got the lands north and east of the Sea of Galilee. A tetrarch was a ruler of part of a kingdom. The Herod in the Gospel ministry stories is Antipas.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 17.11.4 (the division; Perea and Galilee to Antipas) | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/ant-17.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Luke 3:2","word":"Annas and Caiaphas being the high priests","title":"Two high priests at once?","text":"Only one man held the office at a time. Annas was appointed in AD 6 and removed by the Roman governor about ten years later, but he stayed the head of the family. Caiaphas, his son-in-law, held the office for about eighteen years, longer than anyone else in that century.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 18.2.1–2 (Annas appointed, removed by Gratus; Caiaphas appointed) and 18.4.3 (Caiaphas removed by Vitellius) | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/ant-18.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Matt 3:7","word":"Pharisees","title":"Who the Pharisees were","text":"The Pharisees were a lay movement devoted to keeping the law along with the traditions of their fathers. Josephus counted more than six thousand of them. They believed the soul lives after death and in the resurrection, and ordinary townspeople followed their lead in worship and prayer.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 17.2.4 (\"above six thousand\") and 18.1.3 (beliefs; influence with the people) | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/ant-18.htm","Josephus, Jewish War 2.8.14 | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/war-2.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Matt 4:5","word":"a pinnacle of the temple","title":"A dizzying drop","text":"Herod's temple courts were built out over steep valleys. Josephus says that anyone who looked down from the top of the Royal Porch, on the south side, would grow dizzy, because his eyes could not reach the bottom of the drop.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 15.11.5 (\"he would be giddy, while his sight could not reach to such an immense depth\") | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/ant-15.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"John 2:20","word":"Forty and six years was this temple in building","title":"Herod's unfinished temple","text":"Herod began rebuilding the temple in the eighteenth year of his reign. Some of its stones were about 25 cubits long, near 37 feet. Work on the courts went on long after Jesus; the eastern porches were still being repaired in Nero's day, a few years before the Romans burned it all.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 15.11.1 (eighteenth year) and 15.11.3 (stones twenty-five cubits) | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/ant-15.htm","Josephus, Antiquities 20.9.7 (the people ask Agrippa to rebuild the eastern cloisters) | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/ant-20.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Luke 5:1","word":"the lake of Gennesaret","title":"The lake of Galilee","text":"The Sea of Galilee is a freshwater lake about 13 miles long and 7 wide, lying about 700 feet below sea level. Josephus praised its water as sweet and good to drink. Pliny the Elder named the towns around it: Julias and Hippos on the east, Tarichea on the south, Tiberias on the west.","src":["Josephus, Jewish War 3.10.7 | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/war-3.htm","Pliny, Natural History 5.71 | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/L/Roman/Texts/Pliny_the_Elder/5*.html","Encyclopaedia Britannica, \"Sea of Galilee\" | https://www.britannica.com/summary/Sea-of-Galilee"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Luke 6:15","word":"Simon called Zelotes","title":"Simon the Zealot","text":"Zelotes means \"zealous one.\" Josephus describes a movement started by Judas the Galilean in AD 6 that would call no one Lord but God. The party that went by the name \"Zealots\" appears in his history later, during the war with Rome. Simon's name may mean a political past, or zeal for the law.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 18.1.6 (Judas the Galilean; \"God is to be their only Ruler and Lord\") | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/ant-18.htm","Josephus, Jewish War 4.3.9 (\"the zealots; for that was the name they went by\") | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/war-4.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Mark 4:36","word":"other little ships","title":"A boat from Jesus' time","text":"In 1986 a drought shrank the Sea of Galilee, and two brothers found an old boat in the mud near Ginosar. It is about 27 feet long and 7½ feet wide, dated to around the time of Jesus. It needed a crew of five and had room for over a dozen more people.","src":["Shelley Wachsmann, \"The Galilee Boat: 2,000-Year-Old Hull Recovered Intact,\" Biblical Archaeology Review 14:5 (1988) | https://cojs.org/galilee-boat-2000-year-old-hull-recovered-intact-shelley-wachsmann-bar-1405-sepoct-1988/","Israel Ministry of Foreign Affairs, \"The Roman Boat from the Sea of Galilee\""],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Mark 5:1","word":"the country of the Gadarenes","title":"Across the lake, a Greek city","text":"Gadara was one of the Decapolis, a league of Greek-speaking cities mostly east of the Jordan, and Pliny lists it among them. Most people there were not Jews, which is why a herd of two thousand pigs could be grazing near the shore.","src":["Pliny, Natural History 5.74 (the Decapolis, including Gadara) | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/L/Roman/Texts/Pliny_the_Elder/5*.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Mark 5:22","word":"one of the rulers of the synagogue","title":"A ruler of the synagogue, in stone","text":"In 1913 an inscription was found in Jerusalem from before AD 70. In it a priest named Theodotus, \"ruler of the synagogue,\" son and grandson of rulers of the synagogue, says he built it \"for the reading of the law and the teaching of the commandments,\" with rooms for travelers.","src":["\"The Theodotus Inscription,\" Biblical Archaeology Society Library | https://library.biblicalarchaeology.org/sidebar/the-theodotus-inscription/","John S. Kloppenborg, \"Dating Theodotus (CIJ II 1404),\" Journal of Jewish Studies 51 (2000)"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"John 1:44","word":"Bethsaida","title":"Bethsaida, renamed for Caesar's daughter","text":"Herod's son Philip enlarged the fishing village of Bethsaida into a city and renamed it Julias, after the emperor's daughter. Two ruins on the north shore compete to be it: et-Tell, dug since 1991, and el-Araj, where digs since 2014 found Roman-era remains and a later church.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 18.2.1 (\"advanced the village Bethsaida ... called it by the name of Julias\") | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/ant-18.htm","Samuel DeWitt Pfister, \"Where Is Biblical Bethsaida?\" Bible History Daily | https://www.biblicalarchaeology.org/daily/biblical-sites-places/biblical-archaeology-sites/where-is-biblical-bethsaida/"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Mark 6:14","word":"king Herod","title":"The tetrarch who wanted a crown","text":"Antipas was a tetrarch, and Luke always calls him that. Years later Herodias pushed him to ask the emperor Caligula for the title of king. A rival accused him instead, and Caligula took his land and banished him and Herodias to Lyons in Gaul.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 18.7.1–2 (\"awarded him a perpetual banishment, and appointed Lyons\") | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/ant-18.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Mark 6:17","word":"laid hold upon John","title":"Josephus remembers John","text":"The Jewish historian Josephus wrote about John too. He calls John a good man who urged the Jews to live righteously and come to baptism. Herod feared John's hold on the crowds, sent him in chains to the fortress of Machaerus, and had him killed there.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 18.5.2 (\"he was sent a prisoner ... to Macherus ... and was there put to death\") | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/ant-18.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Mark 6:21","word":"Herod on his birthday made a supper","title":"The fortress where John died","text":"Machaerus stands on a mountain east of the Dead Sea, in Perea, which Antipas also ruled. Hungarian archaeologists digging there since 2009 have uncovered the palace courtyard. The excavator, Győző Vörös, believes a niche at one end held the throne where Herod sat at the feast.","src":["Győző Vörös, \"Machaerus: Where Salome Danced and John the Baptist Was Beheaded,\" Biblical Archaeology Review, Sept/Oct 2012; \"Restoring Herod's Throne Niche at Machaerus,\" BAR, Winter 2020 | https://library.biblicalarchaeology.org/article/restoring-herods-throne-niche-at-machaerus/","Pliny, Natural History 5.72 (Machaerus, second fortress of Judea after Jerusalem)"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Mark 6:22","word":"the daughter of the said Herodias","title":"Her name was Salome","text":"The Gospels do not name the girl who danced. Josephus does. Herodias had a daughter, Salome, by her first husband. Salome later married Philip the tetrarch, her great-uncle, and after his death married another of Herod's grandsons.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 18.5.4 (\"her daughter Salome was married to Philip, the son of Herod, and tetrarch of Trachonitis\") | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/ant-18.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Luke 9:10","word":"the city called Bethsaida","title":"Crossing out of Antipas's land","text":"Matthew says Jesus withdrew by boat after hearing of John's death. Bethsaida belonged to Philip the tetrarch, across the border from Antipas, the ruler who had killed John and who now wondered who Jesus was.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 18.2.1 (Bethsaida/Julias in Philip's territory) | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/ant-18.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Matt 14:34","word":"the land of Gennesaret","title":"The garden plain of Gennesaret","text":"Gennesaret was a small plain on the lake's northwest shore. Josephus called its soil so rich that walnuts, which need cool air, grew beside palms, which need heat. He says grapes and figs could be picked there ten months of the year.","src":["Josephus, Jewish War 3.10.8 | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/war-3.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"John 21:1","word":"the sea of Tiberias","title":"A city built on graves","text":"Herod Antipas founded Tiberias on the lake shore and named it for the emperor Tiberius. He built it over old graves, which made the ground unclean under Jewish law, so he had to force or pay people to move there. John alone calls the lake by the new city's name.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 18.2.3 (\"many sepulchers were to be here taken away, in order to make room for the city Tiberias\") | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/ant-18.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Matt 13:8","word":"some an hundredfold","title":"A hundredfold near Gadara","text":"A hundred times the seed sown was a famous harvest. The Roman farm writer Varro, writing a few decades before Jesus, reports that near Gadara in Syria, across the Sea of Galilee, one measure of seed could yield a hundred.","src":["Varro, On Agriculture 1.44.2 (\"in Syria ad Gadara ... ex modio nasci centum\") | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Varro/de_Re_Rustica/1*.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Matt 13:25","word":"sowed tares among the wheat","title":"Sabotage in the wheat field","text":"The tares were likely darnel, a weed that looks like wheat until it heads out. Sowing it in a neighbor's field was a real crime. Roman law books record a case of someone throwing darnel into another man's crop to spoil it, and the lawsuit the owner could bring.","src":["Justinian, Digest 9.2.27.14 (Ulpian citing Celsus: \"si lolium aut avenam in segetem alienam inieceris\") | https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/justinian/digest9.shtml"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Matt 13:31","word":"a grain of mustard seed","title":"A weed that takes over","text":"Pliny the Elder, writing about the time of the Gospels, says mustard grows with no care at all, and once it is sown you can hardly get rid of it, because the seed sprouts as soon as it falls.","src":["Pliny, Natural History 19.54.170 (\"vix est sato semel eo liberare locum, quoniam semen cadens protinus viret\") | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/L/Roman/Texts/Pliny_the_Elder/19*.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Luke 8:2","word":"Mary called Magdalene","title":"Magdala's synagogue","text":"Magdalene means from Magdala, a town on the lake's west shore. In 2009 diggers there found a synagogue from Jesus' time. In its main hall sat a carved stone showing a seven-branched lampstand, one of the oldest carvings of the temple menorah ever found.","src":["Israel Ministry of Foreign Affairs, \"Unique ancient synagogue exposed at Sea of Galilee,\" 14 Sept 2009 | https://embassies.gov.il/MFA/IsraelExperience/history/Pages/Unique-ancient-synagogue-exposed-at-Sea-of-Galilee-14-SEP-2009.aspx","Bible History Daily, \"New First-Century Synagogue\" | https://www.biblicalarchaeology.org/daily/new-first-century-synagogue/"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Matt 16:13","word":"Caesarea Philippi","title":"Where the Jordan begins","text":"This Caesarea sat at the springs that feed the Jordan River, beside a cave sacred to the Greek god Pan. Herod the Great built a white marble temple there to honor Augustus. His son Philip rebuilt the town and named it Caesarea.","src":["Josephus, Jewish War 1.21.3 (marble temple at Panium) | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/war-1.htm","Josephus, Antiquities 18.2.1 (Philip builds Paneas and names it Caesarea); Pliny, Natural History 5.71"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"John 4:4","word":"he must needs go through Samaria","title":"The short road north","text":"Samaria lay between Judea and Galilee. Josephus says Galileans going to the feasts in Jerusalem regularly traveled through Samaria, and anyone in a hurry had to, since that road took three days. The longer way went down the Jordan valley.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 20.6.1 (\"the custom of Galileans ... to take their journeys through the country of the Samaritans\") | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/ant-20.htm","Josephus, Life 52 (§269) | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/autobiog.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"John 4:11","word":"the well is deep","title":"How deep Jacob's well is","text":"The well near Nablus that has been shown as Jacob's well for centuries is cut into solid rock. An English traveler, Henry Maundrell, measured it in 1697 at about three yards across and thirty-five yards deep, more than a hundred feet. A traveler without a rope and bucket could get nothing from it.","src":["Henry Maundrell, A Journey from Aleppo to Jerusalem (1697), in Thomas Wright, ed., Early Travels in Palestine (1848) | https://archive.org/download/earlytravelsinpa00wrig/earlytravelsinpa00wrig_djvu.txt"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"John 4:20","word":"Our fathers worshipped in this mountain","title":"A temple on Mount Gerizim","text":"The Samaritans once had their own temple on Mount Gerizim, above Jacob's well. The Jewish ruler John Hyrcanus destroyed it more than a century before Jesus. The Samaritans still called Gerizim the holiest of all mountains and kept worshipping on it.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 13.9.1 (Hyrcanus takes Gerizim and the temple) | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/ant-13.htm","Josephus, Antiquities 18.4.1 (Gerizim \"the most holy of all mountains\") | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/ant-18.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Luke 9:53","word":"they did not receive him","title":"Trouble on the Samaria road","text":"Samaritan villages did not always welcome Jews headed for Jerusalem. About twenty years after this, Josephus reports, men of a Samaritan village called Ginea attacked a band of Galileans traveling to a feast and killed many of them.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 20.6.1 (Ginea) | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/ant-20.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Luke 10:30","word":"went down from Jerusalem to Jericho","title":"Down the Jericho road","text":"Jerusalem sits about 2,500 feet above sea level, and Jericho about 800 feet below it. The road between them runs about 15 miles and drops some 3,300 feet. Josephus describes the country along it as desert and stony, good cover for robbers.","src":["Josephus, Jewish War 4.8.3 (\"one hundred and fifty furlongs from Jerusalem ... desert and stony\") | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/war-4.htm","Todd Bolen, BiblePlaces.com, \"Jericho road\" | https://www.bibleplaces.com/?p=12199","Encyclopaedia Britannica, \"Jerusalem\" (2,575 ft) | https://www.britannica.com/place/Jerusalem/Landscape"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Luke 10:33","word":"a certain Samaritan","title":"Bad blood with Samaria","text":"Feelings between Jews and Samaritans were raw. When Jesus was a boy, some Samaritans slipped into the temple at Passover and scattered human remains in the porches, making the holy place unclean. After that the priests shut Samaritans out of the temple.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 18.2.2 (\"threw about dead men's bodies, in the cloisters\") | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/ant-18.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Luke 19:2","word":"chief among the publicans, and he was rich","title":"Rich Jericho","text":"Jericho was one of the richest spots in the land. Its spring watered groves of date palms and the balsam bush, whose oil was the most precious product of the region. It was warm enough that people wore linen there while snow fell on the rest of Judea.","src":["Josephus, Jewish War 4.8.3 (palms, balsam, linen in winter) | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/war-4.htm","Josephus, Antiquities 15.4.2 (Jericho's balsam and palms) | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/ant-15.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Luke 15:1","word":"publicans and sinners","title":"What people thought of tax men","text":"Later Jewish law books show how tax collectors were seen. The Mishnah says that if tax collectors entered a house, the house became unclean. It also forbids making change from a tax collector's purse or taking charity from one, and it puts them in a list beside murderers and robbers.","src":["Mishnah Tohorot 7:6 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Tahorot.7.6","Mishnah Bava Kamma 10:1 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Bava_Kamma.10.1","Mishnah Nedarim 3:4 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Nedarim.3.4"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Matt 22:23","word":"the Sadducees, which say that there is no resurrection","title":"The Sadducees","text":"The Sadducees were a small party of the powerful, drawn from the leading priestly families. Josephus says they taught that the soul dies with the body, and they kept only what the written law commanded. Few people followed them, but those few held the highest rank.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 18.1.4 (\"souls die with the bodies ... received but by a few, yet by those still of the greatest dignity\") | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/ant-18.htm","Josephus, Jewish War 2.8.14 | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/war-2.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Mark 12:38","word":"Beware of the scribes","title":"Scribes had time to study","text":"Scribes were experts in the law, trained to read, write, and teach it. A Jewish book from about 180 BC says the wisdom of a learned man comes from having leisure. It names the ploughman, carpenter, smith, and potter, who keep the world running but are not sought for in public counsel.","src":["Sirach (Ecclesiasticus) 38:24–34, KJV Apocrypha"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Matt 23:2","word":"Moses’ seat","title":"A Moses' seat in stone","text":"In 1926 workers clearing the synagogue at Chorazin, a town above the lake, found a stone chair carved from a single block of black basalt, with an Aramaic inscription. Many scholars think a chair like it was the \"Moses' seat\" where a teacher sat. The original is in the Israel Museum.","src":["Encyclopaedia Judaica, \"Chorazin\" | https://jewishvirtuallibrary.org/jsource/judaica/ejud_0002_0004_0_04274.html","Center for Online Judaic Studies, \"Moses Seat\" | https://cojs.org/moses_seat-_2nd_century_ce/"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"John 10:23","word":"Solomon’s porch","title":"The long eastern porch","text":"Solomon's porch was the covered colonnade along the east side of the temple courts, above the Kidron valley. People said it was the work of King Solomon. Josephus gives its wall a length of 400 cubits, about 600 feet, built of large white stones.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 20.9.7 (\"walls that reached four hundred cubits ... This was the work of king Solomon\") | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/ant-20.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"John 5:2","word":"Bethesda, having five porches","title":"The pool with five porches","text":"Excavations beside St. Anne's Church in Jerusalem found a large pool split in two by a wall down the middle. With covered walkways on the four outer sides and one along the middle wall, it had five porches. The southern pool had wide steps leading down into the water.","src":["Urban C. von Wahlde, \"The Puzzling Pool of Bethesda,\" Biblical Archaeology Review, Sept/Oct 2011; summary at Bible History Daily | https://www.biblicalarchaeology.org/daily/biblical-sites-places/jerusalem/the-bethesda-pool-site-of-one-of-jesus-miracles/"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"John 9:7","word":"the pool of Siloam","title":"The pool found by a broken pipe","text":"In 2004 city workers repairing a pipe south of the Old City uncovered stone steps. Archaeologists Eli Shukron and Ronny Reich found they belonged to a large stepped pool. Coins in the plaster show it was built about a century before Jesus and used until the Romans took Jerusalem.","src":["Hershel Shanks, \"The Siloam Pool: Where Jesus Cured the Blind Man,\" Biblical Archaeology Review, Sept/Oct 2005 | https://library.biblicalarchaeology.org/?p=71189","Associated Press, \"Archaeologists identify traces of 'miracle' pool,\" Dec. 2004 | https://www.nbcnews.com/id/wbna6750670"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"John 11:38","word":"It was a cave, and a stone lay upon it","title":"How tombs were closed","text":"Families around Jerusalem buried their dead in rooms cut into the rock. The doorway was low and small. In a survey of more than 900 such tombs, almost all were shut with a square stone pushed into the opening like a cork.","src":["Amos Kloner, \"Did a Rolling Stone Close Jesus' Tomb?\" Biblical Archaeology Review 25:5 (1999) | https://library.biblicalarchaeology.org/article/did-a-rolling-stone-close-jesus-tomb/"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Mark 15:46","word":"rolled a stone unto the door of the sepulchre","title":"A rich man's rolling stone","text":"Round stones that rolled in a groove were rare. Amos Kloner found only four among more than 900 tombs around Jerusalem from this period, all belonging to the richest families. Matthew says Joseph of Arimathea was a rich man.","src":["Amos Kloner, \"Did a Rolling Stone Close Jesus' Tomb?\" Biblical Archaeology Review 25:5 (1999) | https://library.biblicalarchaeology.org/article/did-a-rolling-stone-close-jesus-tomb/","Matt 27:57"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Matt 24:3","word":"as he sat upon the mount of Olives","title":"The view from Olivet","text":"The Mount of Olives rises east of Jerusalem, across the deep Kidron valley, a little higher than the city. From its slope you look straight across at the temple. Forty years later the Romans camped a legion on this same hill to besiege the city.","src":["Josephus, Jewish War 5.2.3 (Mount of Olives, \"parted from it by a deep valley ... Cedron\"; tenth legion's camp) and 5.3.5 | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/war-5.htm","Encyclopaedia Britannica, Mount of Olives (2,652 ft) | https://britannica.com/print/article/427745"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Luke 22:39","word":"the mount of Olives","title":"Are these the same olive trees?","text":"When the Romans besieged Jerusalem in AD 70, they cut down all the trees around the city. In 2012 scientists dated three of the oldest olive trees in Gethsemane to the 1000s and 1100s AD. Olive trees grow back from their roots, so the roots may be older still.","src":["Josephus, Jewish War 5.12.4 (\"all the trees that were about the city had been already cut down\") | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/war-5.htm","Mauro Bernabei et al., \"The age of the olive trees in the Garden of Gethsemane,\" Journal of Archaeological Science (2015) | https://agris.fao.org/search/en/records/65de2b6c4c5aef494fda8a7d"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Matt 26:36","word":"a place called Gethsemane","title":"Gethsemane means oil press","text":"The name comes from Aramaic words for \"press\" and \"oil.\" Olives from the groves on the hillside were crushed there for their oil, used for lamps, cooking, and anointing.","src":["Strong's Concordance G1068, \"oil-press\"; Thayer's Lexicon, \"from גִּת press, and שָׁמְנָא oil\" | https://biblehub.com/greek/1068.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"John 18:13","word":"led him away to Annas first","title":"The house of Annas","text":"Five of Annas's sons became high priest after him, and so did his son-in-law Caiaphas. Josephus says nothing like it had happened to any other high priest. The Talmud later preserved a lament against the priestly families, with \"Woe is me because of the house of Hanin\" (Annas) for their whispering.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 20.9.1 (\"he had five sons who had all performed the office of a high priest\") | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/ant-20.htm","Babylonian Talmud, Pesachim 57a | https://www.sefaria.org/Pesachim.57a"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Matt 26:57","word":"Caiaphas the high priest","title":"The Caiaphas bone box","text":"In 1990 workers building a park in Jerusalem broke into a family tomb. Inside was an ornate ossuary, a stone box for bones, inscribed \"Yehosef bar Qayafa,\" Joseph son of Caiaphas. Josephus gives the high priest's full name as Joseph Caiaphas. The box is now in the Israel Museum.","src":["\"The Tomb of Caiaphas,\" Biblical Archaeology Society Library (Zvi Greenhut's excavation) | https://library.biblicalarchaeology.org/sidebar/the-tomb-of-caiaphas/","Josephus, Antiquities 18.2.2 (\"Joseph Caiaphas\") | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/ant-18.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Mark 14:54","word":"the palace of the high priest","title":"How the priestly rich lived","text":"In Jerusalem's Upper City archaeologists uncovered large stone mansions from this time, with courtyards and private ritual baths. One burned house held a stone weight inscribed \"of Bar Kathros,\" a high-priestly family. Caiaphas's own house has not been identified.","src":["Hillel Geva, Jewish Quarter Excavations Conducted by Nahman Avigad, vol. IV: The Burnt House (Israel Exploration Society, 2010)","Babylonian Talmud, Pesachim 57a (house of Kathros among the high-priestly families) | https://www.sefaria.org/Pesachim.57a"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Mark 14:55","word":"all the council","title":"The Sanhedrin and Herod","text":"The council, the Sanhedrin, was Judea's highest court. The Mishnah says the great Sanhedrin had 71 members. Josephus tells how young Herod was once put on trial before it, and how, when he became king, he killed nearly all its members.","src":["Mishnah Sanhedrin 1:6 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Sanhedrin.1.6","Josephus, Antiquities 14.9.4 (\"he slew all the members of this Sanhedrim ... excepting Sameas\") | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/ant-14.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Matt 26:73","word":"thy speech bewrayeth thee","title":"A Galilean accent","text":"Galileans spoke Aramaic with an accent that Judeans laughed at. The Talmud tells of a Galilean in the market asking for \"amar.\" His listeners mocked him, since he blurred his sounds so badly that he could have meant a donkey, wine, wool, or a lamb.","src":["Babylonian Talmud, Eruvin 53a–53b | https://www.sefaria.org/Eruvin.53b"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Luke 23:7","word":"Herod’s jurisdiction","title":"Pilate sends him to Herod","text":"Jesus was from Nazareth, so he was Antipas's subject; Antipas ruled Galilee and Perea, while Judea was under a Roman governor. Antipas had come up to Jerusalem for Passover. Pilate passed the prisoner to the man who ruled his home district.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 17.11.4 (Galilee and Perea to Antipas) | https://sacred-texts.com/jud/josephus/ant-17.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"judea","ref":"Luke 24:13","word":"threescore furlongs","title":"Where was Emmaus?","text":"Sixty furlongs is about seven miles. No one knows for sure which village was Emmaus. Josephus mentions an Emmaus about thirty furlongs from Jerusalem, where the emperor Vespasian later settled eight hundred retired soldiers; other candidates lie farther out.","src":["Josephus, Jewish War 7.6.6 (§217), Greek text \"σταδίους τριάκοντα\" | https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0147:book=7:section=217"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Matt 2:1","word":"Herod the king","title":"A king appointed by Rome","text":"Herod was king because the Roman Senate voted him the title in 40 BC, after Mark Antony and Octavian (the future Augustus) backed him. He ruled Judea as a \"client king,\" free at home but loyal to Rome abroad. When the wise men arrived, his throne still depended on keeping the emperor pleased.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 14.14.4-5 | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/josephus/ant-14.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Matt 2:22","word":"Archelaus","title":"Archelaus and the Roman takeover","text":"Augustus never made Archelaus a king. He gave him half of Herod's land with the lesser title \"ethnarch.\" Archelaus ruled so badly that in AD 6 Augustus banished him to Vienne in Gaul. Judea then came under a Roman governor, the arrangement Pilate later inherited.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 17.11.4; 17.13.2; 17.13.5 | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/josephus/ant-17.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Luke 2:1","word":"Cæsar Augustus","title":"Augustus and his census","text":"Augustus ruled Rome from 31 BC to AD 14. In his own record of his reign, carved on bronze, he lists three counts of Roman citizens: in 28 BC, 8 BC, and AD 14. Most people in the provinces were not citizens, so they were counted by separate provincial censuses. No ancient writer besides Luke mentions one decree for the whole empire.","src":["Augustus, Res Gestae 8 | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Augustus/Res_Gestae/2*.html","Suetonius, Augustus 100.1 | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Suetonius/12Caesars/Augustus*.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Luke 2:2","word":"Cyrenius","title":"Quirinius and a dating puzzle","text":"Cyrenius is the Greek form of Quirinius. Josephus says Quirinius ran a census of Judea in AD 6, after Archelaus was removed. But Matthew and Luke both place Jesus' birth under Herod, who died about ten years earlier. Historians have long treated this as a known puzzle. Tertullian, writing around AD 207, said a census was taken in Judea under Sentius Saturninus, governor of Syria before Herod died.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 18.1.1; 17.13.5 | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/josephus/ant-18.html","Tertullian, Against Marcion 4.19 | https://www.tertullian.org/anf/anf03/anf03-31.htm","Luke 1:5; Matt 2:1"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Luke 2:3","word":"every one into his own city","title":"Going home to be counted","text":"A Roman order from Egypt, dated AD 104, shows the same thing Luke describes. The governor Gaius Vibius Maximus announced a house-by-house census and ordered everyone living away from home to return to their own district to be registered. The papyrus is in the British Library.","src":["Edict of G. Vibius Maximus, P.Lond. 3.904 (Select Papyri 2.220) | https://www.attalus.org/docs/select2/p220.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Luke 2:10","word":"good tidings","title":"Good news about an emperor","text":"In 9 BC the cities of the Roman province of Asia changed their calendar to start the year on Augustus's birthday. Their decree, found at Priene in modern Turkey, says his birthday was the beginning of \"good tidings\" for the world. The Greek word is euangelia, the same family of words Luke uses here and the root of our word \"evangel.\"","src":["Priene calendar inscription, OGIS 458, line 40 | https://heurist.huma-num.fr/heurist/judaism_and_rome/hml/263"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Luke 2:14","word":"on earth peace","title":"The peace of Augustus","text":"Rome had a temple of Janus whose doors were shut only when the whole empire was at peace. Augustus boasted that the Senate closed them three times during his rule, after only twice in all of Rome's earlier history. Jesus was born during this long stretch of Roman peace, the Pax Romana.","src":["Augustus, Res Gestae 13 | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Augustus/Res_Gestae/2*.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Luke 3:1","word":"Tiberius Cæsar","title":"Dating by the emperor","text":"Luke dates John's preaching the Roman way, by the emperor's year. Augustus died on August 19, AD 14, and Tiberius took his place. His fifteenth year falls about AD 28 or 29, depending on how Luke counted. This is one of the firmest dates in the Gospels.","src":["Suetonius, Augustus 100.1 | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Suetonius/12Caesars/Augustus*.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Luke 3:1","word":"Lysanias","title":"A stone that names Lysanias","text":"Critics once said Luke made a mistake here, because the best-known Lysanias died in 36 BC. Then an inscription turned up at Abila, the capital of Abilene. A freedman of \"Lysanias the tetrarch\" built a temple there for the safety of \"the lords Augusti,\" a phrase that fits Tiberius and his mother Livia after AD 14. That points to a later Lysanias, the one Luke names.","src":["Abila inscription, CIG 4521; R. Savignac, Revue Biblique 9 (1912): 533-540 | https://wholestones.org/the-complete-text-of-the-abila-inscription-concerning-lysanias/"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Matt 27:2","word":"Pontius Pilate","title":"The Pilate stone","text":"In 1961 archaeologists digging the Roman theater at Caesarea found a limestone block reused in a stairway. Its Latin inscription says Pontius Pilate, \"prefect of Judea,\" dedicated a building honoring Tiberius. It is the only inscription from his lifetime that names him, and it is now in the Israel Museum in Jerusalem.","src":["Caesarea Pilate inscription, AE 1963, 104 | https://cojs.org/pontius_pilate-s_tiberium_inscription-_26-36_ce/"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Luke 3:12","word":"publicans","title":"Who the publicans were","text":"A publicanus was a Roman contractor who bid for the right to collect taxes, then hired local men to gather them. Whatever they collected above the amount owed was their profit. People across the empire despised them, and ancient writers listed them alongside robbers.","src":["Thayer's Greek Lexicon, telones (G5057) | https://biblehub.com/greek/5057.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Luke 3:14","word":"be content with your wages","title":"Soldiers and their pay","text":"Pay was a sore point in the Roman army. When Augustus died in AD 14, the legions on the Danube mutinied. Tacitus records their complaint that a soldier's body and life were valued at ten asses a day, and they demanded a full denarius instead.","src":["Tacitus, Annals 1.17 | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Tacitus/Annals/1B*.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Matt 5:41","word":"compel thee to go a mile","title":"Forced to carry a load","text":"The Greek word for \"compel\" came from the Persian royal post, whose riders could seize men and animals to keep messages moving. Under Rome, soldiers and officials used the same right to force a civilian to carry their gear. The \"mile\" here is a Roman mile, a thousand paces, a little shorter than an English mile.","src":["Herodotus, Histories 8.98 | https://lexundria.com/hdt/8.98/mcly","Thayer's Greek Lexicon, angareuo (G29) and milion (G3400) | https://biblehub.com/greek/3400.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Mark 12:42","word":"a farthing","title":"The widow's coins in Roman money","text":"The widow gave two lepta, the smallest Jewish coins. Mark explains their value in Roman terms: together they made one quadrans, a Latin word for a quarter of an as. Mark often explains things for readers who knew Roman money better than Jewish money.","src":["Thayer's Greek Lexicon, kodrantes (G2835) | https://biblehub.com/greek/2835.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Mark 5:9","word":"Legion","title":"The name Legion","text":"\"Legion\" is a Latin army word. A legion was a unit of several thousand heavy infantry, the backbone of Rome's army. Tacitus counts only twenty-five legions guarding the whole empire in AD 23, four of them in Syria, the nearest province. A man who called himself Legion was claiming to be thousands strong.","src":["Tacitus, Annals 4.5 | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Tacitus/Annals/4A*.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Mark 6:14","word":"king Herod","title":"The tetrarch who wanted a crown","text":"This Herod is Antipas, a son of Herod the Great. Rome let him rule Galilee only as a \"tetrarch,\" the title Matthew and Luke use. Mark calls him king, the title he wanted. Years later his wife Herodias pushed him to ask Rome for it, and the emperor Caligula answered by exiling him to Gaul.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 18.7.1-2 | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/josephus/ant-18.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Mark 6:27","word":"an executioner","title":"A Roman word in Herod's court","text":"Mark's word here is spekoulator, borrowed from the Latin speculator. It first meant a scout, then an officer of a commander's bodyguard. It appears nowhere else in the New Testament. Herod Antipas's court used the Roman name for the guard he sent to the prison.","src":["Thayer's Greek Lexicon, spekoulator (G4688) | https://biblehub.com/greek/4688.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"John 6:1","word":"the sea of Tiberias","title":"A lake named for an emperor","text":"Herod Antipas built a new capital on the lake's western shore around AD 20 and named it Tiberias, after the emperor Tiberius. Josephus says the builders had to clear away tombs, so many Jews thought the city unclean. John is the only Gospel writer who calls the lake by the emperor's name.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 18.2.3 | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/josephus/ant-18.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Matt 16:13","word":"Caesarea Philippi","title":"A city named for Caesar","text":"Caesarea Philippi stood at the springs of the Jordan, a place once called Paneas. Herod the Great built a temple there to Augustus, and his son Philip enlarged the town and renamed it for Caesar. Jesus asked \"Whom do men say that I am?\" in a city that honored the emperor as a god.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 15.10.3; 18.2.1 | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/josephus/ant-18.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Matt 16:24","word":"take up his cross","title":"Crosses his hearers had seen","text":"Jesus spoke of the cross before his own death, and his hearers knew what he meant. About 4 BC, after Herod died, the Roman governor of Syria, Varus, put down a revolt in Judea and crucified two thousand men. Older Galileans could remember it.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 17.10.10 | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/josephus/ant-17.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Matt 17:25","word":"custom or tribute","title":"Two kinds of tax","text":"Jesus names the two taxes kings collected. \"Custom\" (telos) was a toll on goods moving through markets, ports, and roads. \"Tribute\" here is kensos, borrowed from the Latin census, a yearly tax on each person. The coin in the fish was for the temple tax, which Jews paid to God's house rather than to Caesar.","src":["Thayer's Greek Lexicon, kensos (G2778) | https://biblehub.com/greek/2778.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Matt 20:2","word":"a penny a day","title":"A day's pay","text":"The \"penny\" is the Roman denarius, a small silver coin. A denarius for a day's work was a fair wage. When the legions mutinied in AD 14, a denarius a day was the raise they demanded.","src":["Tacitus, Annals 1.17 | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Tacitus/Annals/1B*.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Matt 22:17","word":"tribute unto Caesar","title":"The tax that started a revolt","text":"The tribute was the Roman head tax, begun when Judea came under a Roman governor in AD 6 and Quirinius counted the people. A man named Judas the Galilean led a revolt against it, teaching that paying it meant accepting slavery. The question was meant to trap Jesus between Rome and the crowd.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 18.1.1 | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/josephus/ant-18.html","Josephus, Jewish War 2.8.1 | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/josephus/war-2.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Matt 22:20","word":"image and superscription","title":"Whose name was on the coin","text":"The usual silver denarius of Tiberius showed his head and the Latin words TI CAESAR DIVI AVG F AVGVSTVS: \"Tiberius Caesar Augustus, son of the divine Augustus.\" The back read PONTIF MAXIM, \"high priest.\" To a Jew the coin made religious claims for the emperor.","src":["Harvard Art Museums 1976.79.9, denarius of Tiberius, Lugdunum (RIC I² 30) | https://www.harvardartmuseums.org/collections/object/196252"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Luke 13:1","word":"Pilate","title":"Pilate's violent record","text":"Josephus does not mention these Galileans, but their story fits what he does tell about Pilate. When Jews protested his use of temple money for an aqueduct, Pilate sent soldiers dressed as civilians into the crowd with hidden daggers, and many were killed.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 18.3.2 | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/josephus/ant-18.html","Josephus, Jewish War 2.9.4 | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/josephus/war-2.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Luke 19:12","word":"to receive for himself a kingdom","title":"A real trip to get a kingdom","text":"Jesus' hearers knew this story from life. After Herod the Great died, his son Archelaus sailed to Rome to ask Augustus for the kingdom. The Jewish nation sent fifty men after him to argue against him, much like the citizens in verse 14. Augustus gave Archelaus part of the land.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 17.9.3; 17.11.1; 17.11.4 | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/josephus/ant-17.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"John 11:48","word":"the Romans shall come","title":"The fear that came true","text":"The council feared Rome would take \"our place,\" meaning the temple. In AD 70 the Roman army under Titus captured Jerusalem. Josephus, who was there, says the temple burned on the tenth of the month Ab, the same day of the year the Babylonians had burned the first temple.","src":["Josephus, Jewish War 6.4.5-7 | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/josephus/war-6.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Luke 21:20","word":"compassed with armies","title":"The church leaves Jerusalem","text":"Roman armies surrounded Jerusalem in AD 66 and again in AD 70. Eusebius, a church historian of the 300s, says the Christians in Jerusalem were warned by revelation before the war and moved across the Jordan to the town of Pella.","src":["Eusebius, Church History 3.5.3 | https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf201.iii.viii.v.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Matt 26:5","word":"lest there be an uproar","title":"Soldiers on the temple porches","text":"Roman troops watched the Passover crowds from the Antonia fortress beside the temple. Josephus says soldiers stood on the temple porches at feasts to stop riots. About twenty years after Jesus, one soldier's rude gesture at Passover set off a stampede that Josephus says killed thousands.","src":["Josephus, Jewish War 5.5.8 | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/josephus/war-5.html","Josephus, Antiquities 20.5.3; Jewish War 2.12.1 | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/josephus/ant-20.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Matt 26:53","word":"twelve legions","title":"Twelve legions","text":"Twelve legions would have been an enormous army. Tacitus says that in AD 23 the whole Roman Empire was guarded by only twenty-five legions, with four in Syria. Jesus named an army larger than all of Rome's forces in the east combined.","src":["Tacitus, Annals 4.5 | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Tacitus/Annals/4A*.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"John 18:3","word":"a band of men","title":"A Roman cohort at the arrest","text":"The Greek word for \"band\" is speira, the usual word for a Roman cohort, a unit of several hundred soldiers. In verse 12 its commander appears, the chiliarch or tribune, \"captain\" in the King James. John is the Gospel that places Roman troops at the arrest.","src":["Thayer's Greek Lexicon, speira (G4686) and chiliarchos (G5506) | https://biblehub.com/greek/4686.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"John 18:31","word":"It is not lawful for us to put any man to death","title":"Who could order an execution","text":"When Judea became a Roman province in AD 6, Josephus says the first governor came with power from Caesar over life and death. Under Roman rule, capital sentences belonged to the governor. The one exception Josephus records is the temple warning: Jews could kill any foreigner, even a Roman, who passed the barrier into the inner courts.","src":["Josephus, Jewish War 2.8.1; 6.2.4 | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/josephus/war-2.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Mark 15:16","word":"Prætorium","title":"The governor's headquarters","text":"Praetorium was the Latin name for a Roman commander's headquarters. In Jerusalem the governor likely stayed in Herod the Great's palace on the city's west side. Philo says Pilate hung gilded shields there. Older tradition placed the trial at the Antonia fortress by the temple.","src":["Philo, Embassy to Gaius 299 | https://www.earlychristianwritings.com/yonge/book40.html","Josephus, Jewish War 5.5.8 | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/josephus/war-5.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Matt 27:19","word":"his wife sent unto him","title":"Governors' wives in the provinces","text":"Older Roman custom kept wives at home when a governor left for his province. By Pilate's day many brought their wives along. In AD 21, the Senate debated a motion to forbid it, and the motion was set aside.","src":["Tacitus, Annals 3.33-34 | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Tacitus/Annals/3B*.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"John 19:12","word":"thou art not Cæsar’s friend","title":"Pilate's fear of Rome","text":"Philo, a Jewish writer of Pilate's own time, describes a quarrel over shields Pilate set up in Jerusalem. When Jewish leaders threatened to write to Tiberius, Pilate panicked. Philo says he feared they would report his bribes, his insults, and his executions of people never tried. A complaint to the emperor could end his career.","src":["Philo, Embassy to Gaius 299-305 | https://www.earlychristianwritings.com/yonge/book40.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Luke 23:12","word":"Pilate and Herod","title":"Enemies who became friends","text":"Luke does not say why the two men were enemies. Philo gives one possible clue. When Pilate hung shields in Jerusalem, the protest was led by the four sons of King Herod the Great, who wrote to Tiberius, and the emperor ordered Pilate to remove them. Philo does not name the sons.","src":["Philo, Embassy to Gaius 300-305 | https://www.earlychristianwritings.com/yonge/book40.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Mark 15:15","word":"when he had scourged him","title":"Scourging before the cross","text":"Romans often whipped a condemned man before crucifying him. Josephus saw it during the siege of Jerusalem: captured Jews \"were first whipped, and then tormented\" before being crucified. The governor Florus later had even men of high Roman rank whipped and nailed to crosses in Jerusalem, which Josephus calls something no one had dared before.","src":["Josephus, Jewish War 5.11.1; 2.14.9 | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/josephus/war-5.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Matt 27:26","word":"delivered him to be crucified","title":"A Roman historian names Pilate","text":"Tacitus, Rome's great historian, was no friend of Christians. Writing around AD 116 about the fire in Rome, he explained where their name came from: Christus \"had undergone the death penalty in the reign of Tiberius, by sentence of the procurator Pontius Pilatus.\"","src":["Tacitus, Annals 15.44 | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Tacitus/Annals/15B*.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Luke 23:24","word":"Pilate gave sentence","title":"How Pilate's rule ended","text":"Pilate governed Judea for ten years. Around AD 36 his soldiers attacked a crowd of Samaritans at Mount Gerizim. The Samaritans complained to Vitellius, governor of Syria, who sent Pilate to Rome to answer to Tiberius. Tiberius died before Pilate arrived, and Pilate disappears from the record.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 18.4.1-2 | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/josephus/ant-18.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Mark 15:21","word":"compel","title":"Simon pressed into service","text":"The word for \"compel\" is the same one Jesus used in the Sermon on the Mount about being forced to go a mile. Roman soldiers could make any passerby carry a load. Simon came from Cyrene, a city in North Africa with a large Jewish community. His son Rufus has a Latin name.","src":["Thayer's Greek Lexicon, angareuo (G29) | https://biblehub.com/greek/29.htm","Matt 5:41"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Matt 27:32","word":"to bear his cross","title":"What Simon carried","text":"Christian art usually shows a whole cross being carried. Several Latin writers mention a condemned man carrying a patibulum, the crossbeam, but a 2020 study found only four such sources and urged caution about how common the practice was. Scholars still know little about it.","src":["R. van Wingerden, \"Carrying a patibulum: A Reassessment of Non-Christian Latin Sources,\" New Testament Studies 66 (2020): 433-453 | https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/new-testament-studies/article/carrying-a-patibulum-a-reassessment-of-nonchristian-latin-sources/6CFB251146E39995D5A088540E4DCFDA"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Mark 15:24","word":"crucified","title":"What Romans thought of the cross","text":"Romans thought crucifixion the worst way to die and kept it away from their own citizens. Cicero called it \"the most cruel and disgusting punishment.\" In another speech he said even the word \"cross\" should be kept far from the eyes and ears of Roman citizens.","src":["Cicero, Against Verres 2.5.165 | https://www.thelatinlibrary.com/cicero/verres.2.5.shtml","Cicero, For Rabirius on a Charge of Treason 16 | https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.02.0019:text=Rab.%20Perd.:section=16"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"John 19:19","word":"Pilate wrote a title","title":"The sign of the crime","text":"Romans often made a condemned person's crime public on a sign. Suetonius describes a slave led through a dinner party \"preceded by a placard giving the reason for his punishment.\" A Christian martyr at Lyons in AD 177 was led around the arena under a sign reading \"This is Attalus the Christian.\"","src":["Suetonius, Caligula 32.2 | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Suetonius/12Caesars/Caligula*.html","Eusebius, Church History 5.1.44 | https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf201.iii.x.ii.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"John 19:20","word":"Hebrew, and Greek, and Latin","title":"Three languages","text":"Each language reached a different reader. Hebrew (or the related Aramaic) was spoken by local Jews. Greek was the common language of the eastern empire, used by traders and pilgrims. Latin was the language of Roman government. The temple itself posted warning stones in Greek and Latin, Josephus says, so foreigners would know to stay out.","src":["Josephus, Jewish War 5.5.2 | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/josephus/war-5.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Mark 15:39","word":"centurion","title":"Mark's Latin word","text":"Matthew and Luke call this officer by a Greek title meaning \"leader of a hundred.\" Mark uses kenturion, the Latin word centurio written in Greek letters. It appears only in this chapter of the whole New Testament. Mark also uses other Latin words, such as legion, speculator, and quadrans.","src":["Thayer's Greek Lexicon, kenturion (G2760) | https://biblehub.com/greek/2760.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Mark 15:44","word":"Pilate marvelled if he were already dead","title":"Death could take days","text":"Crucifixion was designed to be slow, and victims could hang for a long time. Josephus once found three friends among the crucified after the fall of Jerusalem. Titus let him take them down and give them to doctors. Two died anyway, and one lived.","src":["Josephus, Life 75 (420-421) | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/josephus/autobiog.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"John 19:31","word":"that their legs might be broken","title":"Taking the bodies down","text":"Roman custom often left the crucified on the cross for days, but Jerusalem was different. Josephus says Jews took such care of burial that they took down even the crucified and buried them before sunset, as the law of Moses required. Philo says some governors also released bodies to families at feast times.","src":["Josephus, Jewish War 4.5.2 | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/josephus/war-4.html","Philo, Flaccus 83 | https://www.earlychristianwritings.com/yonge/book36.html","Deut 21:22-23"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Matt 27:58","word":"begged the body","title":"Asking for the body","text":"Under Roman law the bodies of the executed could be given to relatives who asked for them. A later Roman jurist wrote that Augustus himself followed this practice, though permission could be refused, especially in treason cases. Pilate granted the body to Joseph of Arimathea, who was not a relative.","src":["Digest 48.24.1 (Ulpian, On the Duties of a Proconsul 9) | https://droitromain.univ-grenoble-alpes.fr/Corpus/d-48.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Matt 27:65","word":"Ye have a watch","title":"Whose guard?","text":"The word \"watch\" is koustodia, from the Latin custodia, \"guard.\" Pilate's reply can be read two ways: \"you have a guard of your own\" (the temple police) or \"take a guard\" (Roman soldiers). Matthew 28:14, where the guards fear the governor, suggests they answered to Pilate.","src":["Thayer's Greek Lexicon, koustodia (G2892) | https://biblehub.com/greek/2892.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"Matt 2:16","word":"slew all the children","title":"A Roman joke about Herod","text":"Matthew is the only early source for this killing. Around AD 400 the Roman writer Macrobius told a story about Augustus. Hearing that Herod had ordered boys under two killed, and that Herod's own son died among them, Augustus joked that it was better to be Herod's pig than his son. Jews did not eat pork, so the pig was safer.","src":["Macrobius, Saturnalia 2.4.11 | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/L/Roman/Texts/Macrobius/Saturnalia/2*.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"rome","ref":"John 12:21","word":"Bethsaida of Galilee","title":"A town named for Caesar's daughter","text":"Philip the tetrarch, Herod's son, rebuilt the fishing village of Bethsaida as a city and renamed it Julias, after Julia, the daughter of Augustus. Philip, Andrew, and Peter came from there. Two of them, Philip and Andrew, carry Greek names, which fits a town tied to the wider Roman world.","src":["Josephus, Antiquities 18.2.1 | https://penelope.uchicago.edu/josephus/ant-18.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Matt 2:6","word":"thou Bethlehem","title":"Micah's little town, and David's words","text":"The chief priests answer Herod from Micah 5:2. Micah calls Bethlehem \"little among the thousands of Judah\"; Matthew's wording turns it around to \"not the least.\" The last line borrows God's words to David in 2 Samuel 5:2, \"Thou shalt feed my people Israel.\" Matthew's Greek verb for \"rule\" means to shepherd. Bethlehem was David's hometown.","src":["Micah 5:2","2 Samuel 5:2","1 Samuel 17:12","Strong's Greek G4165 (poimaino), in gospel-harmony/dictionary/out/strongs_gospels.json"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Matt 2:15","word":"Out of Egypt have I called my son","title":"Israel's story, lived again","text":"Hosea 11:1 looks back to the Exodus: \"When Israel was a child, then I loved him, and called my son out of Egypt.\" In Hosea the son is the nation of Israel. Matthew reads Jesus' going down into Egypt and coming back as Israel's history repeated in one person. Here Matthew follows the Hebrew; the Greek Septuagint reads \"called his children.\"","src":["Hosea 11:1","Septuagint, Hosea 11:1 (Brenton ed.) | https://ebible.org/grcbrent/HOS11.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Matt 2:18","word":"Rachel weeping for her children","title":"Rachel's tomb on the Bethlehem road","text":"Jeremiah 31:15 pictures Rachel, the mother of Joseph and Benjamin, weeping at Ramah for her children carried into exile. Genesis 35:19 says Rachel was buried \"in the way to Ephrath, which is Bethlehem,\" so her weeping fits the town where Herod's soldiers killed the children. Jeremiah's next verse promises the children \"shall come again.\"","src":["Jeremiah 31:15-16","Genesis 35:19"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Matt 3:3","word":"The voice of one crying in the wilderness","title":"A highway through the desert","text":"All four Gospels apply Isaiah 40:3 to John the Baptist. In Isaiah, a voice calls for a road through the desert so God can bring his people home from exile: \"make straight in the desert a highway for our God.\" The Gospels shorten that last line to \"make his paths straight.\" Isaiah 40 opens with \"Comfort ye, comfort ye my people.\"","src":["Isaiah 40:1-5"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Mark 1:2","word":"Behold, I send my messenger before thy face","title":"Mark opens with two prophets","text":"Mark's first quotation joins two prophets. \"Behold, I send my messenger\" is Malachi 3:1, with a touch of Exodus 23:20, \"Behold, I send an Angel before thee.\" In Malachi God says the messenger \"shall prepare the way before me\"; the Gospel wording reads \"before thy face\" and \"before thee.\" Mark then goes straight on to Isaiah 40:3.","src":["Malachi 3:1","Exodus 23:20"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Matt 3:4","word":"a leathern girdle about his loins","title":"Dressed like Elijah","text":"John's clothing matches the description of Elijah in 2 Kings 1:8: \"He was an hairy man, and girt with a girdle of leather about his loins.\" The Hebrew can mean a man wearing a garment of hair. When King Ahaziah's messengers described a man dressed that way, the king knew at once: \"It is Elijah the Tishbite.\"","src":["2 Kings 1:1-8"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Mark 1:11","word":"Thou art my beloved Son","title":"Words from a psalm and a prophet","text":"The voice from heaven uses two Old Testament passages. \"Thou art my Son\" comes from Psalm 2:7, a psalm about the king God has anointed (Psalm 2:2). \"In whom I am well pleased\" is close to Isaiah 42:1, about God's chosen servant: \"mine elect, in whom my soul delighteth; I have put my spirit upon him.\" At the baptism the Spirit comes down too.","src":["Psalm 2:2, 7","Isaiah 42:1"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Matt 4:2","word":"forty days and forty nights","title":"Forty days, like Moses and Elijah","text":"Moses was with God on the mountain \"forty days and forty nights; he did neither eat bread, nor drink water\" (Exodus 34:28). Elijah walked \"forty days and forty nights unto Horeb the mount of God\" (1 Kings 19:8). Israel spent forty years in the wilderness, where God \"suffered thee to hunger\" (Deuteronomy 8:2-3). Matthew gives Jesus' fast the same number.","src":["Exodus 34:28","1 Kings 19:8","Deuteronomy 8:2-3"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Matt 4:4","word":"Man shall not live by bread alone","title":"An answer from the manna chapter","text":"Jesus answers from Deuteronomy 8:3, where Moses reminds Israel that God let them go hungry and then fed them with manna, \"that he might make thee know that man doth not live by bread only.\" The devil asked for bread made from stones. Jesus answered with a verse about forty years of hunger and manna in the wilderness.","src":["Deuteronomy 8:1-5"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Matt 4:6","word":"He shall give his angels charge concerning thee","title":"The devil leaves out a line","text":"The devil quotes Psalm 91:11-12, but he leaves out part of it. The psalm says the angels will \"keep thee in all thy ways.\" The devil drops those words and offers the promise as a reason to jump from the top of the temple. Psalm 91 is a prayer of trust in God's protection.","src":["Psalm 91:11-12"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Matt 4:7","word":"Thou shalt not tempt the Lord thy God","title":"Remember Massah","text":"The full verse is Deuteronomy 6:16: \"Ye shall not tempt the LORD your God, as ye tempted him in Massah.\" At Massah, thirsty Israel demanded water and asked, \"Is the LORD among us, or not?\" Exodus 17:7 says the place got its name \"because they tempted the LORD.\" Jumping from the temple to force a rescue would be the same kind of test.","src":["Deuteronomy 6:16","Exodus 17:1-7"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Matt 4:10","word":"him only shalt thou serve","title":"Three answers from Deuteronomy","text":"All three of Jesus' answers come from Deuteronomy 6 and 8, Moses' words to Israel at the end of their forty years. This one is Deuteronomy 6:13. The KJV Old Testament reads \"Thou shalt fear the LORD thy God, and serve him.\" The word \"only\" is not in the Hebrew. It is in the Greek Septuagint, and the Gospel wording follows the Greek.","src":["Deuteronomy 6:13","Septuagint, Deuteronomy 6:13 (Brenton ed.) | https://ebible.org/grcbrent/DEU06.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Matt 4:15","word":"Galilee of the Gentiles","title":"The first land to see the light","text":"Matthew quotes Isaiah 9:1-2. Naphtali, in Galilee, was among the first lands Assyria conquered and carried captive (2 Kings 15:29). Isaiah promised that this same region would see \"a great light.\" Jesus' ministry began at Capernaum, inside it. Isaiah's \"Galilee of the nations\" is Matthew's \"Galilee of the Gentiles.\"","src":["Isaiah 9:1-2","2 Kings 15:29"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Matt 5:5","word":"the meek","title":"The meek shall inherit","text":"This beatitude is nearly word for word from Psalm 37:11: \"But the meek shall inherit the earth; and shall delight themselves in the abundance of peace.\" Psalm 37 tells the reader not to envy evildoers. It says five times that the faithful will \"inherit the earth\" or \"the land\" (verses 9, 11, 22, 29, 34).","src":["Psalm 37:1, 9, 11, 22, 29, 34"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Matt 5:38","word":"An eye for an eye, and a tooth for a tooth","title":"A rule for judges","text":"\"Eye for eye, tooth for tooth\" appears three times in the law of Moses (Exodus 21:24; Leviticus 24:20; Deuteronomy 19:21). In each place it sets the penalty a court may give, so that the punishment matches the injury. Deuteronomy 19:18 says \"the judges shall make diligent inquisition.\" Jesus speaks to the person who was wronged.","src":["Exodus 21:23-25","Leviticus 24:20","Deuteronomy 19:18-21"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Matt 5:43","word":"hate thine enemy","title":"Not found in the law","text":"\"Thou shalt love thy neighbour\" is Leviticus 19:18. \"Hate thine enemy\" is not in the Old Testament; Leviticus 19:17 says \"Thou shalt not hate thy brother in thine heart.\" The idea was around in Jesus' time. The Dead Sea Scrolls' Community Rule tells its members to love \"all the sons of light\" and hate \"all the sons of darkness.\"","src":["Leviticus 19:17-18","Dead Sea Scrolls, Community Rule (1QS) 1.9-10 | https://ccat.sas.upenn.edu/gopher/other/courses/rels/225/Texts/1QS"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Matt 8:17","word":"Himself took our infirmities, and bare our sicknesses","title":"Isaiah 53, read as healing","text":"Matthew quotes Isaiah 53:4 after an evening of healing. The KJV Old Testament reads \"Surely he hath borne our griefs, and carried our sorrows,\" but the Hebrew words can mean sickness and pain, and Matthew translates them that way. The Greek Septuagint reads \"He bears our sins,\" so Matthew is working from the Hebrew here.","src":["Isaiah 53:4","Septuagint, Isaiah 53:4 (Brenton ed.) | https://ebible.org/grcbrent/ISA53.htm","Strong's Hebrew H2483 (choli) | https://www.blueletterbible.org/lexicon/h2483/kjv/wlc/0-1/"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Matt 9:15","word":"the bridegroom","title":"God as bridegroom in the prophets","text":"The prophets speak of God as Israel's husband. \"Thy Maker is thine husband\" (Isaiah 54:5). \"As the bridegroom rejoiceth over the bride, so shall thy God rejoice over thee\" (Isaiah 62:5). Hosea 2:19 promises, \"I will betroth thee unto me for ever.\" When Jesus calls himself the bridegroom, he takes up that language.","src":["Isaiah 54:5","Isaiah 62:5","Hosea 2:19-20"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Matt 11:5","word":"The blind receive their sight","title":"Jesus answers John from Isaiah","text":"John asked from prison whether Jesus was \"he that should come.\" Jesus answers with lines from Isaiah: the blind see, the deaf hear, and the lame leap (Isaiah 35:5-6), and good tidings are preached to the poor (Isaiah 61:1; \"the meek\" in the KJV, \"the poor\" in the Greek). Lepers cleansed and the dead raised are not in Isaiah's lists.","src":["Isaiah 35:5-6","Isaiah 61:1","Septuagint, Isaiah 61:1 (Brenton ed.) | https://ebible.org/grcbrent/ISA61.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Matt 12:3","word":"what David did, when he was an hungred","title":"David and the holy bread","text":"Jesus points to 1 Samuel 21:1-6. David, running from King Saul, asked the priest at Nob for food. The only bread there was the shewbread, the holy loaves set out before the LORD every Sabbath and afterward eaten by the priests (Leviticus 24:8-9). The priest gave it to David and his hungry men.","src":["1 Samuel 21:1-6","Leviticus 24:5-9"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Matt 12:21","word":"in his name shall the Gentiles trust","title":"Isaiah's servant, in Greek","text":"Matthew quotes four verses of Isaiah 42 about a servant who will not shout in the street or break \"a bruised reed.\" His last line, \"in his name shall the Gentiles trust,\" matches the Greek Septuagint. The Hebrew, as the KJV Old Testament gives it, reads \"the isles shall wait for his law.\"","src":["Isaiah 42:1-4","Septuagint, Isaiah 42:4 (Brenton ed.) | https://ebible.org/grcbrent/ISA42.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Matt 12:40","word":"as Jonas was three days and three nights","title":"The sign of Jonah","text":"Jonah 1:17 says the LORD \"prepared a great fish,\" and \"Jonah was in the belly of the fish three days and three nights.\" Matthew's Greek word is ketos, a huge sea creature, which the KJV translates \"whale.\" Jonah came out alive. Jesus says this is the only sign he will give.","src":["Jonah 1:17","Strong's Greek G2785 (ketos), in gospel-harmony/dictionary/out/strongs_gospels.json"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Matt 13:15","word":"their eyes they have closed","title":"Isaiah's call, from the Greek","text":"Jesus quotes Isaiah 6:9-10, the commission Isaiah received when he saw the Lord in the temple. In the Hebrew, as in the KJV Old Testament, God tells Isaiah to \"make the heart of this people fat... and shut their eyes.\" Matthew follows the Greek Septuagint, where the people have done it themselves: \"their eyes they have closed.\"","src":["Isaiah 6:9-10","Septuagint, Isaiah 6:10 (Brenton ed.) | https://ebible.org/grcbrent/ISA06.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Matt 13:35","word":"I will open my mouth in parables","title":"A psalm of Asaph","text":"Matthew quotes Psalm 78:2, \"I will open my mouth in a parable.\" The psalm is credited to Asaph, and Matthew calls its writer \"the prophet\"; 2 Chronicles 29:30 calls Asaph \"the seer.\" Psalm 78 then retells Israel's history, from Egypt to King David, so that children will learn it (78:4-6).","src":["Psalm 78:1-8","2 Chronicles 29:30"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Matt 15:9","word":"teaching for doctrines the commandments of men","title":"Isaiah 29, in the Greek wording","text":"Jesus quotes Isaiah 29:13. The first half matches the Hebrew: \"this people draw near me with their mouth, and with their lips do honour me.\" The second half follows the Greek Septuagint, \"in vain do they worship me, teaching the commandments and doctrines of men.\" The Hebrew, as the KJV gives it, reads \"their fear toward me is taught by the precept of men.\"","src":["Isaiah 29:13","Septuagint, Isaiah 29:13 (Brenton ed.) | https://ebible.org/grcbrent/ISA29.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Matt 17:5","word":"hear ye him","title":"The prophet like Moses","text":"\"Hear ye him\" points to Deuteronomy 18:15, where Moses promised, \"The LORD thy God will raise up unto thee a Prophet from the midst of thee... like unto me; unto him ye shall hearken.\" The Greek Septuagint ends that verse with the same verb the voice uses here. Moses himself was standing on the mountain when the words were spoken.","src":["Deuteronomy 18:15-19","Septuagint, Deuteronomy 18:15 (Brenton ed.) | https://ebible.org/grcbrent/DEU18.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Matt 21:5","word":"Tell ye the daughter of Sion","title":"Two prophets joined at the gate","text":"Matthew joins two prophets. \"Tell ye the daughter of Sion\" comes from Isaiah 62:11, \"Say ye to the daughter of Zion.\" The rest is Zechariah 9:9: \"thy King cometh unto thee... lowly, and riding upon an ass, and upon a colt the foal of an ass.\" Matthew leaves out Zechariah's words \"he is just, and having salvation.\"","src":["Isaiah 62:11","Zechariah 9:9"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Matt 21:9","word":"Hosanna","title":"Hosanna, from Psalm 118","text":"The crowd's words come from Psalm 118:25-26: \"Save now, I beseech thee, O LORD... Blessed be he that cometh in the name of the LORD.\" Hosanna is the Hebrew \"save now\" from verse 25, written in Greek letters. The Mishnah, written down about AD 200, says priests circled the altar at the feast of tabernacles saying these words.","src":["Psalm 118:25-26","Strong's Greek G5614 (hosanna), in gospel-harmony/dictionary/out/strongs_gospels.json","Mishnah Sukkah 4:5 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Sukkah.4.5"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Mark 11:17","word":"My house shall be called of all nations the house of prayer","title":"Isaiah and Jeremiah in the temple","text":"Jesus joins two prophets. \"Mine house shall be called an house of prayer for all people\" is Isaiah 56:7, in a chapter welcoming foreigners to the temple. Only Mark keeps \"of all nations.\" \"A den of thieves\" is Jeremiah 7:11, from Jeremiah's sermon at the temple gate against people who stole and killed, then came to worship (7:9-10).","src":["Isaiah 56:6-7","Jeremiah 7:2, 9-11"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Matt 21:16","word":"Out of the mouth of babes and sucklings","title":"Praise from children, in the Greek","text":"Jesus answers the priests with Psalm 8:2. The KJV Old Testament reads \"Out of the mouth of babes and sucklings hast thou ordained strength.\" Matthew's \"thou hast perfected praise\" follows the Greek Septuagint. The children in the temple were shouting \"Hosanna,\" so the Greek wording fits what was happening.","src":["Psalm 8:2","Septuagint, Psalm 8:3 (Brenton numbering) | https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA008.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Matt 21:33","word":"planted a vineyard","title":"Isaiah's song of the vineyard","text":"The parable opens with details from Isaiah 5:1-2: a vineyard on a fruitful hill, fenced, planted, with \"a tower in the midst of it, and also... a winepress.\" Isaiah says who the vineyard is: \"the vineyard of the LORD of hosts is the house of Israel\" (5:7). Matthew 21:45 says the chief priests and Pharisees knew the parable was about them.","src":["Isaiah 5:1-7"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Mark 12:26","word":"I am the God of Abraham","title":"The God of the living","text":"Jesus answers the Sadducees from the burning bush. In Exodus 3:6 God tells Moses, \"I am the God of thy father, the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob.\" Abraham had been dead for centuries, and God still called himself Abraham's God. Jesus draws the conclusion: \"He is not the God of the dead, but the God of the living\" (12:27).","src":["Exodus 3:6, 15"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Mark 12:29","word":"Hear, O Israel","title":"The Shema","text":"Only Mark includes the opening line of Deuteronomy 6:4-5: \"Hear, O Israel: The LORD our God is one LORD.\" Jews call these verses the Shema, from the Hebrew word for \"hear,\" and recited them morning and evening. Deuteronomy names three things to love God with: heart, soul, and might. Mark and Luke list four, adding \"mind.\"","src":["Deuteronomy 6:4-7","Mishnah Berakhot 1:1-2 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Berakhot.1.1"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Matt 22:39","word":"Thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself","title":"The second commandment, from Leviticus","text":"The second commandment is Leviticus 19:18: \"thou shalt not avenge, nor bear any grudge... but thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself.\" It sits in a chapter of everyday laws, such as leaving grain at the edge of the field for the poor and paying a hired worker the same day (19:9-13). Jesus pairs it with Deuteronomy 6:5.","src":["Leviticus 19:9-18"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Luke 10:29","word":"who is my neighbour","title":"Leviticus already widened the circle","text":"In Leviticus 19:18 the \"neighbour\" stands beside \"the children of thy people,\" fellow Israelites. Sixteen verses later the same chapter widens it: \"the stranger that dwelleth with you shall be unto you as one born among you, and thou shalt love him as thyself\" (19:34). The lawyer knew the law well enough to quote it.","src":["Leviticus 19:18, 33-34"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Matt 22:44","word":"The LORD said unto my Lord","title":"David's Lord","text":"Psalm 110 is a psalm of David. In Hebrew, \"The LORD said unto my Lord\" uses two different words: the first is the divine name the KJV prints in capitals, and the second is adoni, \"my lord,\" a title for a king or master. Jesus asks how David's descendant can be the one David calls \"my Lord.\" Acts 2:34-35 and Hebrews 1:13 quote the verse again.","src":["Psalm 110:1","Acts 2:34-35","Hebrews 1:13"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Matt 24:37","word":"as the days of Noe were","title":"Ordinary days before the flood","text":"Genesis says the earth before the flood \"was filled with violence\" (6:11). Jesus points to something more ordinary: people were \"eating and drinking, marrying and giving in marriage, until the day that Noe entered into the ark\" (Matthew 24:38). Noe is the Greek spelling of Noah.","src":["Genesis 6:11-13","Genesis 7:7"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Matt 25:32","word":"as a shepherd divideth his sheep from the goats","title":"Ezekiel's judgment of the flock","text":"The picture comes from Ezekiel 34, the chapter about God as Israel's shepherd. \"Behold, I judge between cattle and cattle, between the rams and the he goats\" (34:17). In Ezekiel the flock is judged by how the strong animals treated the weak ones: \"ye have... pushed all the diseased with your horns\" (34:21).","src":["Ezekiel 34:17-22"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Matt 26:15","word":"thirty pieces of silver","title":"The price of a slave","text":"The law of Moses set thirty shekels of silver as the payment when an ox killed a servant (Exodus 21:32). In Zechariah 11:12-13 the prophet is paid thirty pieces of silver for shepherding God's people. God calls it \"a goodly price that I was prised at of them\" and tells him to throw it to the potter. Matthew comes back to this in 27:9-10.","src":["Exodus 21:32","Zechariah 11:12-13"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Matt 27:9","word":"spoken by Jeremy the prophet","title":"Jeremiah's name, Zechariah's words","text":"Matthew credits this prophecy to Jeremiah, but the words are closest to Zechariah 11:12-13, where thirty pieces of silver are thrown \"to the potter in the house of the LORD.\" Jeremiah also went to a potter's house (Jeremiah 18:2) and bought a field with silver (32:9). Matthew seems to draw on both prophets.","src":["Zechariah 11:12-13","Jeremiah 18:1-4","Jeremiah 32:6-9"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Mark 14:24","word":"This is my blood of the new testament","title":"The blood of the covenant","text":"The words recall Exodus 24:8. At Sinai, after the people agreed to keep God's law, Moses sprinkled them with the blood of the offerings and said, \"Behold the blood of the covenant.\" The Greek word the KJV translates \"testament\" is diatheke, the same word the Greek Old Testament uses for \"covenant\" in that verse.","src":["Exodus 24:3-8","Septuagint, Exodus 24:8 (Brenton ed.) | https://ebible.org/grcbrent/EXO24.htm","Strong's Greek G1242 (diatheke), in gospel-harmony/dictionary/out/strongs_gospels.json"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Luke 22:20","word":"This cup is the new testament in my blood","title":"Jeremiah's new covenant","text":"Luke and Paul (1 Corinthians 11:25) both have Jesus say \"the new testament,\" which can also be translated \"the new covenant.\" That points to Jeremiah 31:31: \"I will make a new covenant with the house of Israel.\" Jeremiah says that under it God will \"put my law in their inward parts, and write it in their hearts\" (31:33).","src":["Jeremiah 31:31-34","1 Corinthians 11:25"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Mark 14:27","word":"I will smite the shepherd","title":"The struck shepherd","text":"On the way to the Mount of Olives, Jesus quotes Zechariah 13:7: \"smite the shepherd, and the sheep shall be scattered.\" In Zechariah, God calls for the sword against \"my shepherd... the man that is my fellow.\" The words came true that night. When Jesus was arrested, \"they all forsook him, and fled\" (Mark 14:50).","src":["Zechariah 13:7"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Matt 26:64","word":"coming in the clouds of heaven","title":"Daniel's Son of man","text":"Jesus' answer to the high priest joins two passages. \"Sitting on the right hand of power\" is Psalm 110:1. \"Coming in the clouds of heaven\" is Daniel 7:13, where \"one like the Son of man came with the clouds of heaven\" and was given \"an everlasting dominion\" (7:14). The high priest tore his clothes and called it blasphemy (26:65).","src":["Daniel 7:13-14","Psalm 110:1"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Matt 27:43","word":"He trusted in God; let him deliver him now","title":"The mockers quote Psalm 22","text":"The mockers' words follow Psalm 22:7-8 closely: \"they shake the head saying, He trusted on the LORD that he would deliver him: let him deliver him, seeing he delighted in him.\" Matthew has already said the people passing by were \"wagging their heads\" (27:39).","src":["Psalm 22:7-8"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Mark 15:34","word":"Eloi, Eloi, lama sabachthani","title":"The first line of Psalm 22","text":"Jesus' cry is the first line of Psalm 22: \"My God, my God, why hast thou forsaken me?\" Mark gives it in Aramaic, the everyday language of Judea; Matthew's \"Eli\" is the Hebrew form. The rest of the psalm describes mocking (22:7-8) and divided garments (22:18), and it ends in praise: \"in the midst of the congregation will I praise thee\" (22:22).","src":["Psalm 22:1, 7-8, 18, 22-31","Strong's Greek G1682 (Eloi) and G2241 (Eli), in gospel-harmony/dictionary/out/strongs_gospels.json"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"John 19:24","word":"They parted my raiment among them","title":"Garments and lots","text":"John quotes Psalm 22:18: \"They part my garments among them, and cast lots upon my vesture.\" Hebrew poetry often says one thing twice in two lines, and this verse does. John describes two separate acts: the soldiers divided the garments into four parts and cast lots for the seamless coat (19:23-24).","src":["Psalm 22:18"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"John 19:28","word":"I thirst","title":"Vinegar from Psalm 69","text":"John says Jesus said \"I thirst\" so \"that the scripture might be fulfilled.\" The scripture is Psalm 69:21: \"in my thirst they gave me vinegar to drink.\" The first half of that verse reads, \"They gave me also gall for my meat,\" and Matthew 27:34 says the soldiers offered Jesus \"vinegar to drink mingled with gall.\"","src":["Psalm 69:21"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"John 19:29","word":"put it upon hyssop","title":"Hyssop, the Passover plant","text":"Matthew and Mark say the sponge was put on \"a reed\"; John says hyssop. At the first Passover, Israel used \"a bunch of hyssop\" to brush the lamb's blood on the doorposts (Exodus 12:22). John also says Jesus was condemned on \"the preparation of the passover\" (19:14).","src":["Exodus 12:21-23","Matthew 27:48"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"John 19:36","word":"A bone of him shall not be broken","title":"Like the Passover lamb","text":"The words come from the Passover law: \"neither shall ye break a bone thereof\" (Exodus 12:46; Numbers 9:12). Psalm 34:20 says of the righteous man, \"He keepeth all his bones: not one of them is broken.\" The soldiers broke the legs of the two men crucified with Jesus, but he was already dead, so they did not break his (19:32-33).","src":["Exodus 12:46","Numbers 9:12","Psalm 34:20"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"John 19:37","word":"They shall look on him whom they pierced","title":"Pierced, from the Hebrew","text":"John quotes Zechariah 12:10: \"they shall look upon me whom they have pierced.\" Here John follows the Hebrew. The Greek Septuagint reads \"they shall look upon me, because they have mocked me.\" Zechariah goes on, \"they shall mourn for him, as one mourneth for his only son.\" Revelation 1:7 uses the same verse.","src":["Zechariah 12:10","Septuagint, Zechariah 12:10 (Brenton ed.) | https://ebible.org/grcbrent/ZEC12.htm","Revelation 1:7"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Matt 27:51","word":"the veil of the temple was rent","title":"The veil before the most holy place","text":"The veil hung between the holy place and the most holy place, where the ark of the covenant had been kept (Exodus 26:33). Only the high priest went behind it, once a year on the Day of Atonement, with the blood of the offering (Leviticus 16:2, 14-15, 34). Hebrews 10:19-20 calls Christ's flesh \"the veil\" and speaks of a \"new and living way\" in.","src":["Exodus 26:31-33","Leviticus 16:2, 14-15, 34","Hebrews 10:19-20"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Luke 22:37","word":"And he was reckoned among the transgressors","title":"Numbered with the transgressors","text":"At the Last Supper Jesus applies Isaiah 53:12 to himself: \"he was numbered with the transgressors.\" It is the only time in the Gospels he quotes Isaiah 53 in his own words. The same verse ends, \"he bare the sin of many, and made intercession for the transgressors.\" Hours later he was crucified between two thieves.","src":["Isaiah 53:12"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Luke 23:46","word":"Father, into thy hands I commend my spirit","title":"A prayer from Psalm 31","text":"Jesus' last words in Luke come from Psalm 31:5: \"Into thine hand I commit my spirit: thou hast redeemed me, O LORD God of truth.\" Jesus adds one word to the psalm: \"Father.\" When Stephen was stoned, he prayed a similar prayer: \"Lord Jesus, receive my spirit\" (Acts 7:59).","src":["Psalm 31:5","Acts 7:59"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Luke 1:17","word":"to turn the hearts of the fathers to the children","title":"The last words of Malachi","text":"Gabriel's words to Zacharias borrow the last verses of the Old Testament. Malachi 4:5-6 promises Elijah \"before the coming of the great and dreadful day of the LORD,\" and says, \"he shall turn the heart of the fathers to the children.\" Luke 1:17 repeats that line almost word for word. Malachi 3:1, the messenger sent ahead, is applied to John in Luke 7:27.","src":["Malachi 4:5-6","Malachi 3:1"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Luke 1:32","word":"the throne of his father David","title":"The promise to David","text":"Gabriel's promise echoes 2 Samuel 7:12-13, where the prophet Nathan tells King David that God will raise up his descendant and \"stablish the throne of his kingdom for ever.\" Isaiah 9:7 repeats it: \"upon the throne of David, and upon his kingdom... for ever.\" Luke 1:27 has already said Joseph was \"of the house of David.\"","src":["2 Samuel 7:12-16","Isaiah 9:6-7"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Luke 1:52","word":"He hath put down the mighty from their seats","title":"Mary's song and Hannah's","text":"Mary's song follows the song of Hannah in 1 Samuel 2:1-10. Hannah had been unable to have children until God gave her Samuel. Her song praises God for turning things over: \"He raiseth up the poor out of the dust... to set them among princes\" (2:8). Both songs open with joy in God's salvation (1 Samuel 2:1; Luke 1:47).","src":["1 Samuel 1:1-20","1 Samuel 2:1-10"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Luke 1:69","word":"an horn of salvation","title":"The horn of David","text":"A horn stood for an animal's strength, and the Old Testament uses it for a king's power. David sings, \"The LORD is... the horn of my salvation\" (Psalm 18:2). Psalm 132:17 says, \"There will I make the horn of David to bud.\" Hannah's song ends with God exalting \"the horn of his anointed\" (1 Samuel 2:10).","src":["Psalm 18:2","Psalm 132:17","1 Samuel 2:10"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Luke 1:79","word":"To give light to them that sit in darkness","title":"Sunrise for people in darkness","text":"Zacharias ends with Isaiah 9:2: \"they that dwell in the land of the shadow of death, upon them hath the light shined.\" The \"dayspring\" of verse 78 means sunrise. Malachi 4:2 uses the same picture: \"unto you that fear my name shall the Sun of righteousness arise with healing in his wings.\"","src":["Isaiah 9:2","Malachi 4:2","Strong's Greek G395 (anatole), in gospel-harmony/dictionary/out/strongs_gospels.json"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Luke 2:8","word":"keeping watch over their flock by night","title":"The tower of the flock","text":"Genesis 35:21 says that after Rachel's burial on the Bethlehem road, Jacob camped \"beyond the tower of Edar.\" Micah 4:8 speaks to the \"tower of the flock\" (migdal eder in Hebrew) and promises \"the first dominion\" will come to it. Targum Pseudo-Jonathan, an Aramaic paraphrase written down after the Gospels, calls it the place where the Messiah will be revealed. Luke does not name the tower.","src":["Genesis 35:19-21","Micah 4:8","Targum Pseudo-Jonathan on Genesis 35:21 | https://www.sefaria.org/Targum_Jonathan_on_Genesis.35.21"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Luke 2:32","word":"A light to lighten the Gentiles","title":"Simeon quotes Isaiah's servant","text":"Simeon's words come from Isaiah's servant passages. God says to his servant, \"I will also give thee for a light to the Gentiles, that thou mayest be my salvation unto the end of the earth\" (Isaiah 49:6; also 42:6). Simeon has already said, \"mine eyes have seen thy salvation\" (2:30). Paul and Barnabas quote Isaiah 49:6 in Acts 13:47.","src":["Isaiah 49:6","Isaiah 42:6","Acts 13:47"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Luke 3:6","word":"all flesh shall see the salvation of God","title":"Luke quotes further, from the Greek","text":"Luke quotes Isaiah 40 further than Matthew or Mark, through verse 5. His last line, \"all flesh shall see the salvation of God,\" follows the Greek Septuagint. The Hebrew, as the KJV gives it, reads \"the glory of the LORD shall be revealed, and all flesh shall see it together.\"","src":["Isaiah 40:3-5","Septuagint, Isaiah 40:5 (Brenton ed.) | https://ebible.org/grcbrent/ISA40.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Luke 4:18","word":"to set at liberty them that are bruised","title":"Isaiah 61, as Luke gives it","text":"Jesus reads Isaiah 61:1-2, but Luke's wording differs from the Hebrew. \"Recovering of sight to the blind\" follows the Greek Septuagint, where the Hebrew (as the KJV gives it) has \"the opening of the prison to them that are bound.\" \"To set at liberty them that are bruised\" comes from the Greek of Isaiah 58:6, a chapter about the fast God wants.","src":["Isaiah 61:1-2","Isaiah 58:6","Septuagint, Isaiah 61:1 and 58:6 (Brenton ed.) | https://ebible.org/grcbrent/ISA61.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Luke 4:19","word":"To preach the acceptable year of the Lord","title":"He stopped mid-sentence","text":"Jesus stops reading in the middle of Isaiah 61:2. The next words are \"and the day of vengeance of our God.\" He closed the book and said, \"This day is this scripture fulfilled in your ears\" (4:21). \"The acceptable year\" recalls the jubilee of Leviticus 25:10, when Israel was to \"proclaim liberty throughout all the land.\"","src":["Isaiah 61:2","Leviticus 25:8-10"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Luke 4:27","word":"saving Naaman the Syrian","title":"Two miracles for outsiders","text":"Jesus reminds Nazareth of two miracles done for foreigners. In a famine, Elijah was sent to a widow in Zarephath, a town of Sidon outside Israel (1 Kings 17:9). Elisha healed Naaman, commander of the Syrian army, when he \"dipped himself seven times in Jordan\" (2 Kings 5:14). The synagogue was \"filled with wrath\" (Luke 4:28).","src":["1 Kings 17:8-16","2 Kings 5:1-14"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Luke 7:15","word":"he delivered him to his mother","title":"Elijah's words at Nain","text":"Luke's words \"he delivered him to his mother\" match 1 Kings 17:23, where Elijah raised a widow's son and \"delivered him unto his mother.\" Both mothers were widows who had lost a son. After the miracle at Nain the crowd said, \"a great prophet is risen up among us\" (7:16).","src":["1 Kings 17:17-24","Septuagint, 1 Kings 17:23 (Brenton ed., 3 Kingdoms) | https://ebible.org/grcbrent/1KI17.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Luke 9:31","word":"decease","title":"His exodus","text":"Moses and Elias spoke with Jesus about his \"decease.\" Luke's Greek word is exodos, literally \"a way out,\" the same word that gives the book of Exodus its name. Moses had led Israel's exodus out of Egypt. Luke says Jesus would \"accomplish\" his own exodus at Jerusalem.","src":["Strong's Greek G1841 (exodos), in gospel-harmony/dictionary/out/strongs_gospels.json","Exodus 12:40-42"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Luke 9:62","word":"put his hand to the plough","title":"Elisha at the plough","text":"Jesus' answer recalls the call of Elisha. Elijah found him \"plowing with twelve yoke of oxen\" and threw his mantle on him. Elisha asked, \"Let me, I pray thee, kiss my father and my mother, and then I will follow thee\" (1 Kings 19:20). Then he killed his oxen, cooked them on the wood of the plough gear, and followed Elijah (19:21).","src":["1 Kings 19:19-21"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"Luke 19:10","word":"to seek and to save that which was lost","title":"Ezekiel's seeking shepherd","text":"The words echo Ezekiel 34. Israel's shepherds had failed their flock: \"neither have ye sought that which was lost\" (34:4). So God says he will do it himself: \"I will seek that which was lost, and bring again that which was driven away\" (34:16).","src":["Ezekiel 34:4, 11-16"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"John 1:1","word":"In the beginning","title":"The first words of Genesis","text":"John begins with the first words of the Bible: \"In the beginning God created the heaven and the earth\" (Genesis 1:1). In Genesis, God speaks and light appears and is divided from the darkness (1:3-4). John follows the same order and speaks next of light shining in darkness (John 1:4-5).","src":["Genesis 1:1-5"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"John 1:14","word":"dwelt among us","title":"He pitched his tent","text":"\"Dwelt\" translates the Greek verb skenoo, from the word for a tent. It recalls the tabernacle, the tent where God's presence stayed with Israel in the wilderness. Exodus 40:34 says \"the glory of the LORD filled the tabernacle.\" John's next words are \"and we beheld his glory.\"","src":["Exodus 40:34-38","Strong's Greek G4637 (skenoo), in gospel-harmony/dictionary/out/strongs_gospels.json"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"John 1:21","word":"Art thou that prophet?","title":"The prophet Moses promised","text":"\"That prophet\" means the prophet Moses promised in Deuteronomy 18:15: \"The LORD thy God will raise up unto thee a Prophet from the midst of thee, of thy brethren, like unto me.\" The priests and Levites asked John whether he was that prophet, and he said no. After the loaves and fishes, the crowd decided Jesus was (John 6:14).","src":["Deuteronomy 18:15-18","Acts 3:22"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"John 1:29","word":"Behold the Lamb of God","title":"Lambs in the Old Testament","text":"No Old Testament verse uses the words \"Lamb of God,\" but the title draws on several. Abraham told Isaac, \"God will provide himself a lamb\" (Genesis 22:8). Israel killed a lamb \"without blemish\" at the first Passover (Exodus 12:5). Isaiah's servant was \"brought as a lamb to the slaughter\" (Isaiah 53:7).","src":["Genesis 22:8","Exodus 12:3-6","Isaiah 53:7"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"John 1:47","word":"in whom is no guile","title":"An Israelite without Jacob's trick","text":"Jesus' words point back to Jacob, whom God renamed Israel. After Jacob tricked his father, Isaac told Esau, \"Thy brother came with subtilty\" (Genesis 27:35). The Greek Old Testament uses dolos there, the same word translated \"guile\" here. Nathanael is an Israelite without the trickery Jacob was known for.","src":["Genesis 27:35","Genesis 32:28","Septuagint, Genesis 27:35 (Brenton ed.) | https://ebible.org/grcbrent/GEN27.htm","Strong's Greek G1388 (dolos), in gospel-harmony/dictionary/out/strongs_gospels.json"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"John 1:51","word":"the angels of God ascending and descending","title":"Jacob's ladder","text":"Jesus describes Jacob's dream at Bethel: \"a ladder set up on the earth, and the top of it reached to heaven: and behold the angels of God ascending and descending on it\" (Genesis 28:12). In Jesus' version the angels go up and down \"upon the Son of man.\" Jacob woke and called the place \"the house of God\" and \"the gate of heaven\" (28:17).","src":["Genesis 28:10-17"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"John 3:14","word":"as Moses lifted up the serpent in the wilderness","title":"The serpent of brass","text":"Numbers 21:4-9 tells of Israel bitten by poisonous snakes in the wilderness. God had Moses make a serpent of brass and set it on a pole, and \"if a serpent had bitten any man, when he beheld the serpent of brass, he lived.\" A bitten Israelite had to look at it to live. Jesus says whoever believes in him will have eternal life (3:15).","src":["Numbers 21:4-9"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"John 4:5","word":"the parcel of ground that Jacob gave to his son Joseph","title":"Jacob's field at Shechem","text":"Genesis 33:19 says Jacob bought a field near Shechem, and in Genesis 48:22 he gives Joseph \"one portion above thy brethren.\" Joshua 24:32 says Joseph's bones, carried out of Egypt, were buried \"in a parcel of ground which Jacob bought.\" The Old Testament does not mention Jacob's well itself.","src":["Genesis 33:18-19","Genesis 48:22","Joshua 24:32"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"John 6:31","word":"He gave them bread from heaven to eat","title":"Which verse did the crowd quote?","text":"The crowd quotes scripture: \"He gave them bread from heaven to eat.\" The closest verses are Psalm 78:24, where the Greek Old Testament reads \"gave them bread of heaven,\" Nehemiah 9:15, \"gavest them bread from heaven,\" and Exodus 16:4, where God says, \"I will rain bread from heaven.\" Jesus answers that it was his Father who gives the true bread from heaven (6:32).","src":["Psalm 78:24","Septuagint, Psalm 78:24 (77:24 in its numbering) | https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA077.htm","Nehemiah 9:15","Exodus 16:4, 15"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"John 8:12","word":"I am the light of the world","title":"Isaiah's light for the nations","text":"Isaiah's servant was to be \"a light to the Gentiles\" (Isaiah 49:6), and Isaiah 60:1 says to Jerusalem, \"Arise, shine; for thy light is come.\" Isaiah 9:2 promises \"a great light\" for a people in darkness. Jesus said \"I am the light of the world\" in the temple during the feast of tabernacles (John 7:2; 8:20).","src":["Isaiah 9:2","Isaiah 49:6","Isaiah 60:1-3"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"John 10:11","word":"I am the good shepherd","title":"Ezekiel's bad shepherds","text":"Ezekiel 34 is a prophecy against Israel's leaders: \"Woe be to the shepherds of Israel that do feed themselves! should not the shepherds feed the flocks?\" (34:2). They had used the flock and left it scattered. God then says, \"I, even I, will both search my sheep, and seek them out\" (34:11). Jesus calls himself the good shepherd.","src":["Ezekiel 34:1-16"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"John 10:16","word":"one fold, and one shepherd","title":"One flock under one shepherd","text":"\"One fold, and one shepherd\" echoes Ezekiel. God promises, \"I will set up one shepherd over them... even my servant David\" (34:23). In 37:22-24 the divided kingdoms of Israel and Judah become \"one nation\" with \"one shepherd.\" David had been dead for centuries, so the prophecy looks to a king from David's family line.","src":["Ezekiel 34:23-24","Ezekiel 37:22-24"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"John 10:34","word":"Ye are gods","title":"Psalm 82","text":"Jesus quotes Psalm 82:6: \"I have said, Ye are gods; and all of you are children of the most High.\" The psalm opens, \"God standeth in the congregation of the mighty; he judgeth among the gods\" (82:1). Jesus argues that if scripture calls them gods, he is no blasphemer for saying \"I am the Son of God\" (10:36). John calls the Psalms \"your law.\"","src":["Psalm 82:1, 6"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"John 13:18","word":"He that eateth bread with me hath lifted up his heel against me","title":"Betrayed by a friend at table","text":"Jesus quotes Psalm 41:9: \"mine own familiar friend, in whom I trusted, which did eat of my bread, hath lifted up his heel against me.\" He leaves out the words \"in whom I trusted.\" In the psalm, David is sick and a close friend turns on him. Within the hour Judas left the supper (13:30).","src":["Psalm 41:1-9"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"old_testament","ref":"John 15:1","word":"I am the true vine","title":"Israel, God's vine","text":"The Old Testament often pictures Israel as God's vine. \"Thou hast brought a vine out of Egypt... and planted it\" (Psalm 80:8). \"I had planted thee a noble vine... how then art thou turned into the degenerate plant of a strange vine unto me?\" (Jeremiah 2:21). Isaiah 5 is a song about a vineyard that gave wild grapes. Jesus calls himself the true vine.","src":["Psalm 80:8-19","Jeremiah 2:21","Isaiah 5:1-7"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Luke 1:59","word":"on the eighth day","title":"Ordained at eight days old","text":"On the day John was circumcised, something happened that Luke does not record. A revelation to Joseph Smith says John \"was ordained by the angel of God at the time he was eight days old unto this power, to overthrow the kingdom of the Jews, and to make straight the way of the Lord.\"","src":["D&C 84:27-28"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Luke 1:17","word":"in the spirit and power of Elias","title":"The spirit of Elias","text":"Joseph Smith preached on this phrase in March 1844: \"The spirit of Elias is to prepare the way for a greater revelation of God.\" John held that preparing office. He preached and baptized, and he did not claim the authority to give the Holy Ghost. That belonged to the one coming after him.","src":["Joseph Smith, discourse, Nauvoo, March 10, 1844 (History of the Church 6:250)"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 1:21","word":"JESUS","title":"The name, centuries early","text":"Nephi wrote that an angel had told him the Messiah's name: \"his name shall be Jesus Christ, the Son of God.\" King Benjamin heard from an angel that \"his mother shall be called Mary.\" Both prophets lived in the Americas, centuries before Gabriel came to Nazareth.","src":["2 Nephi 25:19","Mosiah 3:8"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Luke 1:27","word":"the virgin’s name was Mary","title":"Nephi saw her in Nazareth","text":"Nephi saw this young woman in a vision about six hundred years before Luke wrote. He saw \"the city of Nazareth,\" and in it \"a virgin,\" whom he called \"most beautiful and fair above all other virgins.\" The angel told him she was \"the mother of the Son of God, after the manner of the flesh.\" Later he saw her again, \"bearing a child in her arms.\"","src":["1 Nephi 11:13-21"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Luke 1:35","word":"the Son of God","title":"The condescension of God","text":"In Nephi's vision the angel asked him, \"Knowest thou the condescension of God?\" To condescend is to come down. The first condescension Nephi was shown is the Father's: he came down from his glory to be the Father of a Son born of a mortal mother. Later in the same vision Nephi sees the second, the Son's own ministry and death.","src":["1 Nephi 11:16-21, 26-33"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 2:2","word":"his star","title":"The new star in the West","text":"Five years before the birth, Samuel the Lamanite told the Nephites that \"a new star\" would rise, \"such an one as ye never have beheld.\" When the night of the sign came, the record says, \"a new star did appear, according to the word.\"","src":["Helaman 14:2, 5","3 Nephi 1:21"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Luke 2:8","word":"by night","title":"The night that stayed light","text":"On the other side of the world that night, the sun went down and it did not get dark. \"There was no darkness in all that night, but it was as light as though it was mid-day.\" Samuel the Lamanite had foretold it. Unbelievers had set a day to put the believers to death if the sign did not come.","src":["Helaman 14:3-4","3 Nephi 1:9, 15, 19"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Luke 2:11","word":"this day","title":"\"On the morrow come I\"","text":"The night before the birth, Nephi spent the whole day praying for his people, who were about to be killed. The voice of the Lord came to him: \"On this night shall the sign be given, and on the morrow come I into the world.\"","src":["3 Nephi 1:11-13"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Luke 2:46","word":"asking them questions","title":"The doctors were asking him","text":"Joseph Smith's translation turns this scene around. In the Inspired Version the teachers in the temple \"were hearing him, and asking him questions.\" In this reading Jesus is being questioned, and he is the one giving answers. The next verse says all who heard him were astonished at his understanding and his answers.","src":["Inspired Version, Luke 2:46-47 (1867 text)"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Luke 2:52","word":"increased in wisdom","title":"Grace to grace","text":"Luke covers about eighteen years in one verse. A revelation to Joseph Smith adds how that growth happened: \"he received not of the fulness at first, but continued from grace to grace, until he received a fulness.\" The same revelation says this is why he was called the Son of God.","src":["D&C 93:12-14"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 2:23","word":"a Nazarene","title":"The silent years","text":"Joseph Smith's translation adds three verses here. Jesus \"grew up with his brethren, and waxed strong, and waited upon the Lord for the time of his ministry to come.\" He \"served under his father,\" and \"needed not that any man should teach him.\" Then, \"after many years, the hour of his ministry drew nigh.\"","src":["Inspired Version, Matthew 3:24-26 (1867 text)"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 3:1","word":"John the Baptist","title":"John came back","text":"On May 15, 1829, Joseph Smith and Oliver Cowdery went into the woods to pray about baptism. A messenger laid his hands on them and conferred the Aaronic Priesthood. He \"said that his name was John, the same that is called John the Baptist in the New Testament.\" John, who baptized Jesus, restored the authority to baptize.","src":["Joseph Smith—History 1:68-72","D&C 13:1"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Mark 1:4","word":"the baptism of repentance","title":"The preparatory gospel","text":"A revelation to Joseph Smith calls the Aaronic Priesthood the priesthood of \"the preparatory gospel,\" the \"gospel of repentance and of baptism, and the remission of sins.\" It says this gospel stayed with the house of Aaron \"until John.\" John held it by right, as a priest and a son of Aaron's line.","src":["D&C 84:26-27","Luke 1:5"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"John 1:28","word":"Bethabara beyond Jordan","title":"Lehi named the place","text":"Six hundred years before John, Lehi told his family about a prophet who would come before the Messiah. Nephi records that \"he should baptize in Bethabara, beyond Jordan,\" and that he would \"baptize the Messiah with water.\" John's Gospel names the same place.","src":["1 Nephi 10:7-10"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 3:15","word":"to fulfil all righteousness","title":"Why a holy man was baptized","text":"Nephi took up the question John raised. How did the Lamb of God fulfill all righteousness by being baptized? His answer: though \"he was holy,\" he showed that \"according to the flesh he humbleth himself before the Father,\" and promised to obey the Father's commandments. Nephi adds that if Jesus needed baptism, everyone else needs it more.","src":["2 Nephi 31:5-9"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 3:16","word":"like a dove","title":"The sign of the dove","text":"Joseph Smith taught that the Holy Ghost did not turn into a bird. \"The Holy Ghost cannot be transformed into a dove; but the sign of a dove was given to John to signify the truth of the deed.\" He said the sign was set before the creation, and that \"the devil cannot come in the sign of a dove.\"","src":["Joseph Smith, discourse, Nauvoo, January 29, 1843 (History of the Church 5:261)"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Mark 1:10","word":"like a dove descending upon him","title":"Nephi saw the baptism","text":"Nephi saw this baptism in vision six hundred years before it happened. He saw \"the prophet who should prepare the way,\" saw the Lamb of God baptized by him, and then saw \"the heavens open, and the Holy Ghost come down out of heaven and abide upon him in the form of a dove.\"","src":["1 Nephi 11:27"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 3:17","word":"This is my beloved Son","title":"The same words in 1820","text":"The voice at the Jordan introduced the Son. Early in the spring of 1820, in the woods near his home in New York, Joseph Smith saw two personages. One pointed to the other and said, \"This is My Beloved Son. Hear Him!\" The Nephites heard nearly the same words at Bountiful before Jesus came down.","src":["Joseph Smith—History 1:14-17","3 Nephi 11:7"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 4:1","word":"into the wilderness","title":"Led out to be with God","text":"Joseph Smith's translation changes why Jesus went into the wilderness. \"Jesus was led up of the Spirit, into the wilderness, to be with God.\" The next verse says he had fasted and \"communed with God,\" and only afterward \"was left to be tempted of the devil.\"","src":["Inspired Version, Matthew 4:1-2 (1867 text)"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 4:9","word":"worship me","title":"Moses heard it too","text":"The Book of Moses records Satan making the same demand of Moses: \"Moses, son of man, worship me.\" Moses answered, \"I am a son of God, in the similitude of his Only Begotten; and where is thy glory, that I should worship thee?\" Both temptations come right after the man has been with God.","src":["Moses 1:12-13"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 5:3","word":"poor in spirit","title":"\"Who come unto me\"","text":"Jesus gave this sermon again to the Nephites. There the first beatitude reads, \"Blessed are the poor in spirit who come unto me.\" Before it he added blessings on those who would believe the words of the twelve he had chosen that day, because the twelve had seen him.","src":["3 Nephi 12:1-3"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 5:48","word":"Be ye therefore perfect","title":"\"Even as I\"","text":"In Galilee Jesus told his disciples to be perfect as the Father is perfect. At Bountiful, after his resurrection, he said, \"I would that ye should be perfect even as I, or your Father who is in heaven is perfect.\" In Matthew he names only the Father.","src":["3 Nephi 12:48"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 5:48","word":"perfect","title":"Brigham Young on being perfect","text":"Brigham Young said this verse could be put another way: \"Be ye as perfect as ye can,\" for that is all we can do. \"To be as perfect as we possibly can, according to our knowledge, is to be just as perfect as our Father in heaven is.\"","src":["Brigham Young, December 18, 1853, Journal of Discourses 2:129-130 | https://jod.mrm.org/2/129/"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 5:16","word":"Let your light so shine","title":"\"I am the light\"","text":"At Bountiful Jesus told his disciples to hold up their light, and then said what that light was: \"Behold I am the light which ye shall hold up, that which ye have seen me do.\" He had knelt and prayed in front of them that day.","src":["3 Nephi 18:24"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 5:17","word":"to fulfil","title":"\"The law is fulfilled\"","text":"Some of the Nephites were puzzled when he said old things had passed away. So he told them plainly: \"Behold, I say unto you that the law is fulfilled that was given unto Moses.\" In Matthew he says he came to fulfill it.","src":["3 Nephi 15:2-5"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 6:13","word":"lead us not into temptation","title":"\"Suffer us not to be led\"","text":"Joseph Smith's translation changes this line so that it no longer sounds as if God leads anyone into temptation. It reads, \"And suffer us not to be led into temptation, but deliver us from evil.\"","src":["Inspired Version, Matthew 6:14 (1867 text)","James 1:13"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 6:25","word":"Take no thought for your life","title":"Said to the twelve","text":"At Bountiful this part of the sermon was not said to everyone. Jesus \"looked upon the twelve whom he had chosen\" and told them, \"ye are they whom I have chosen to minister unto this people. Therefore I say unto you, take no thought for your life.\"","src":["3 Nephi 13:25-34"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 7:1","word":"Judge not","title":"\"Judge not unrighteously\"","text":"Joseph Smith's translation reads, \"Judge not unrighteously, that ye be not judged; but judge righteous judgment.\" The verse no longer forbids all judging. It forbids unjust judging, which matches what Jesus says in John 7:24.","src":["Inspired Version, Matthew 7:2 (1867 text)","John 7:24"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"John 1:1","word":"In the beginning was the Word","title":"\"The gospel preached through the Son\"","text":"Joseph Smith's translation opens John differently: \"In the beginning was the gospel preached through the Son. And the gospel was the word, and the word was with the Son, and the Son was with God, and the Son was of God.\" The Word is the gospel, and the Son is the one who preached it.","src":["Inspired Version, John 1:1 (1867 text)"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"John 1:14","word":"full of grace and truth","title":"John's fuller record","text":"A revelation to Joseph Smith quotes a record by John that uses these same words: \"I beheld his glory, as the glory of the Only Begotten of the Father, full of grace and truth.\" The Lord promises that \"the fulness of the record of John\" will be given to the faithful.","src":["D&C 93:6-11, 18"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"John 1:18","word":"No man hath seen God at any time","title":"Except by the Son","text":"Joseph Smith's translation adds a condition: \"no man hath seen God at any time, except he hath borne record of the Son; for except it is through him no man can be saved.\" When Joseph Smith saw the Father in 1820, the Father's first act was to point to the Son.","src":["Inspired Version, John 1:19 (1867 text)","Joseph Smith—History 1:17"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"John 1:42","word":"Cephas","title":"Cephas means a seer","text":"Joseph Smith's translation adds to the meaning of Peter's new name: \"thou shalt be called Cephas, which is, by interpretation, a seer, or a stone.\" The same verse adds that Peter and Andrew were fishermen and \"straightway left all, and followed Jesus.\"","src":["Inspired Version, John 1:42 (1867 text)"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"John 3:5","word":"born of water and of the Spirit","title":"\"Half a baptism\"","text":"Joseph Smith taught on this verse in July 1843: \"Baptism by water is but half a baptism, and is good for nothing without the other half, that is, the baptism of the Holy Ghost.\" In the same sermon he said, \"You might as well baptize a bag of sand as a man\" without it.","src":["Joseph Smith, discourse, Nauvoo, July 9, 1843 (History of the Church 5:499)"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"John 5:19","word":"what he seeth the Father do","title":"As the Father had done","text":"Joseph Smith preached on this idea a few days before his death. He said that Jesus \"laid down His life, and took it up the same as His Father had done before.\" The Son does what he has seen the Father do.","src":["Joseph Smith, sermon in the grove, Nauvoo, June 16, 1844 (History of the Church 6:477)"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"John 5:29","word":"the resurrection of life","title":"The verse that opened a vision","text":"On February 16, 1832, Joseph Smith and Sidney Rigdon were translating this verse. They were given different wording: \"they who have done good, in the resurrection of the just; and they who have done evil, in the resurrection of the unjust.\" While they thought about it, they saw the vision of the three degrees of glory, now D&C 76.","src":["D&C 76:11, 15-19"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"John 9:2","word":"who did sin, this man, or his parents","title":"Sin before birth?","text":"The disciples' question assumes a person could have lived, and even sinned, before being born. The Restoration teaches that every person lived as a spirit before this life. Abraham saw \"the intelligences that were organized before the world was.\" Jesus' answer rules out sin as the cause of this man's blindness.","src":["Abraham 3:22-23","D&C 93:29"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"John 10:16","word":"other sheep","title":"The other sheep","text":"At Bountiful Jesus quoted this verse and told the Nephites, \"ye are they of whom I said: Other sheep I have.\" Then he told them there were still others, \"not of this land, neither of the land of Jerusalem,\" and that he was going to show himself to them too.","src":["3 Nephi 15:16-21","3 Nephi 16:1-3"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"John 10:18","word":"I have power to lay it down","title":"A power no one else has","text":"John Taylor, writing in 1882, named this as one thing that set the Son apart: \"He had power, as He said, to lay down His life and to take it up again, which power was also given Him by the Father. This is also a power which no other being associated with this earth possesses.\"","src":["John Taylor, The Mediation and Atonement (1882), chapter 18 | https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/36327/pg36327-images.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"John 11:25","word":"I am the resurrection, and the life","title":"Life in himself","text":"John Taylor tied this title to John 5:26, where the Father gives the Son \"to have life in himself.\" Because of that, he wrote, the Son \"had power, when all mankind had lost their life, to restore life to them again; and hence He is the Resurrection and the Life, which power no other man possesses.\"","src":["John Taylor, The Mediation and Atonement (1882), chapter 18 | https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/36327/pg36327-images.html","John 5:26"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"John 13:5","word":"began to wash the disciples’ feet","title":"An ordinance restored","text":"A revelation to Joseph Smith made the washing of feet an ordinance. Men were received into the School of the Prophets by it. The presiding elder was to pray, share bread and wine, and then \"gird himself according to the pattern given in the thirteenth chapter of John's testimony.\"","src":["D&C 88:127, 138-141"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"John 14:2","word":"many mansions","title":"\"Many kingdoms\"","text":"Joseph Smith preached on this verse in May 1844 and said it \"should be: In my Father's kingdom are many kingdoms.\" There are mansions for those who keep a celestial law, he said, and other mansions for those who come short of it. D&C 76 describes those kingdoms.","src":["Joseph Smith, discourse, Nauvoo, May 12, 1844 (History of the Church 6:365)","D&C 76:50-112"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"John 14:18","word":"I will come to you","title":"The other Comforter","text":"Joseph Smith taught that John 14 speaks of two Comforters. The first is the Holy Ghost. The other, he said, \"is no more nor less than the Lord Jesus Christ Himself.\" When a faithful person receives it, Christ will \"appear unto him from time to time,\" and the Lord \"will teach him face to face.\"","src":["Joseph Smith, instructions to the Twelve, Commerce, Illinois, summer 1839 (History of the Church 3:380-381)"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"John 14:23","word":"make our abode with him","title":"A personal appearance","text":"A revelation to Joseph Smith comments on this exact verse: \"The appearing of the Father and the Son, in that verse, is a personal appearance; and the idea that the Father and the Son dwell in a man's heart is an old sectarian notion, and is false.\"","src":["D&C 130:3"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"John 15:1","word":"I am the true vine","title":"Zenos and the olive tree","text":"The Book of Mormon preserves a much longer story of the same kind. The prophet Zenos compared the house of Israel to \"a tame olive tree\" in the Lord's vineyard. The Lord of the vineyard prunes, digs, grafts, and asks again and again what more he could have done. It fills all 77 verses of Jacob 5.","src":["Jacob 5:1-3, 41, 47, 77"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"John 19:30","word":"It is finished","title":"\"I partook and finished\"","text":"The Savior used the same word about his own suffering in a revelation to Joseph Smith. After describing how the suffering made him tremble and bleed at every pore, he said, \"Nevertheless, glory be to the Father, and I partook and finished my preparations unto the children of men.\"","src":["D&C 19:18-19"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"John 20:27","word":"reach hither thy hand","title":"Twenty-five hundred witnesses","text":"At Bountiful Jesus invited a whole crowd to do what he invited Thomas to do. The people \"thrust their hands into his side, and did feel the prints of the nails,\" going forward \"one by one until they had all gone forth.\" The record says there were about two thousand five hundred of them.","src":["3 Nephi 11:14-15","3 Nephi 17:25"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"John 21:22","word":"that he tarry till I come","title":"John is still here","text":"The saying went out that John would not die. A revelation to Joseph Smith settles it. John asked for \"power over death, that I may live and bring souls unto thee,\" and the Lord said, \"thou shalt tarry until I come in my glory.\" Three of the Nephite twelve later asked for the same thing.","src":["D&C 7:1-3","3 Nephi 28:6-7"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Luke 24:39","word":"a spirit hath not flesh and bones","title":"The test for a messenger","text":"Joseph Smith quoted this verse when he taught how to tell heavenly messengers apart. Angels who are \"resurrected personages\" have \"bodies of flesh and bones.\" The instructions add, \"Jesus said: Handle me and see, for a spirit hath not flesh and bones, as ye see me have.\" A resurrected messenger will offer his hand.","src":["D&C 129:1-2, 4-5"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Luke 24:40","word":"he shewed them his hands and his feet","title":"The wounds he will show again","text":"A revelation to Joseph Smith says he will show these wounds again when he comes. The Jews will ask, \"What are these wounds in thine hands and in thy feet?\" He will answer, \"These wounds are the wounds with which I was wounded in the house of my friends.\"","src":["D&C 45:51-52","Zechariah 13:6"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 27:45","word":"there was darkness","title":"Three hours here, three days there","text":"Matthew records three hours of darkness at Jerusalem. In the Americas there were about three hours of storms and earthquakes, and then darkness that lasted \"for the space of three days.\" It was so thick the people \"could feel the vapor of darkness,\" and no candle or fire would light.","src":["3 Nephi 8:19-23","Helaman 14:20, 27"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 27:46","word":"why hast thou forsaken me","title":"The Spirit withdrawn","text":"Brigham Young taught that at the hour of the crisis \"the Father withdrew Himself, withdrew His Spirit, and cast a veil over him.\" He said this is what made Jesus sweat blood in the garden. \"If he had had the power of God upon him, he would not have sweat blood.\"","src":["Brigham Young, February 17, 1856, Journal of Discourses 3:206 | https://jod.mrm.org/3/202/"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 27:52","word":"the graves were opened","title":"The prophecy Jesus asked about","text":"Samuel the Lamanite foretold that at Christ's death \"many graves shall be opened\" and \"many saints shall appear unto many.\" When the risen Lord read the Nephite records, he asked why this had not been written down. Nephi remembered it had happened but had not been written, and Jesus commanded that it be written.","src":["Helaman 14:25","3 Nephi 23:7-13"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 28:19","word":"baptizing them","title":"The words of the ordinance","text":"Matthew gives the command. The Book of Mormon gives the words. At Bountiful Jesus said the one baptizing should stand in the water, call the person by name, and say, \"Having authority given me of Jesus Christ, I baptize you in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Ghost. Amen.\" Then they were to immerse them.","src":["3 Nephi 11:23-26","D&C 20:73"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Mark 16:15","word":"Go ye into all the world","title":"The same charge in the Americas","text":"Moroni quotes Jesus giving this same commission to his Nephite disciples: \"Go ye into all the world, and preach the gospel to every creature.\" The signs that follow believers are listed in almost the same words as Mark 16:17-18.","src":["Mormon 9:22-25"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Luke 22:44","word":"as it were great drops of blood","title":"Bleeding at every pore","text":"Only Luke records the blood. King Benjamin's angel foretold it: \"blood cometh from every pore, so great shall be his anguish.\" In a revelation to Joseph Smith, the Savior described it himself. The suffering caused him \"to tremble because of pain, and to bleed at every pore, and to suffer both body and spirit.\"","src":["Mosiah 3:7","D&C 19:16-18"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Mark 14:33","word":"sore amazed","title":"John Taylor on the garden","text":"John Taylor wrote that Jesus bore \"the weight, the responsibility, and the burden of the sins of all men.\" It was \"groaning beneath this concentrated load\" and \"sweating great drops of blood\" that he prayed, \"Father, if it be possible, let this cup pass from me.\"","src":["John Taylor, The Mediation and Atonement (1882), chapter 21 | https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/36327/pg36327-images.html"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 26:39","word":"let this cup pass from me","title":"\"Would that I might not drink\"","text":"In a revelation to Joseph Smith, the Savior speaks of this prayer in the first person. The suffering was so great that he says, \"would that I might not drink the bitter cup, and shrink.\" To the Nephites he said simply, \"I have drunk out of that bitter cup which the Father hath given me.\"","src":["D&C 19:18","3 Nephi 11:11"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 26:26","word":"Take, eat; this is my body","title":"In remembrance of my body","text":"Joseph Smith's translation adds the reason: \"Take, eat; this is in remembrance of my body which I give a ransom for you.\" At Bountiful Jesus said the same: \"this shall ye do in remembrance of my body, which I have shown unto you.\" Earlier that same day those Nephites had touched that body.","src":["Inspired Version, Matthew 26:22 (1867 text)","3 Nephi 18:7"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 26:29","word":"drink it new with you","title":"Who will be at that table","text":"A revelation to Joseph Smith names some of the people Jesus will drink with when he comes: Moroni, Elias, John the Baptist, Elijah, Joseph, Jacob, Isaac and Abraham, \"Michael, or Adam, the father of all,\" and Peter, James, and John. The faithful who are with them will be there too.","src":["D&C 27:5-14"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 26:41","word":"Watch and pray","title":"\"Lest ye be tempted\"","text":"Jesus repeated this warning to the Nephites twice in one day, once to his disciples and once to the whole crowd. \"Ye must watch and pray always lest ye enter into temptation; for Satan desireth to have you, that he may sift you as wheat.\"","src":["3 Nephi 18:15, 18","Luke 22:31"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 17:2","word":"was transfigured before them","title":"The earth will be transfigured","text":"A revelation to Joseph Smith says the earth itself will one day be transfigured, \"according to the pattern which was shown unto mine apostles upon the mount.\" It adds, \"of which account the fulness ye have not yet received.\" The Gospels give only part of what happened there.","src":["D&C 63:20-21"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 17:3","word":"Moses and Elias","title":"Keys on the mount","text":"Joseph Smith taught that the transfiguration was a priesthood event: \"The Savior, Moses, and Elias, gave the keys to Peter, James, and John, on the mount, when they were transfigured before him.\"","src":["Joseph Smith, on the priesthood, Commerce, Illinois, about July 1839 (History of the Church 3:387)"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 16:18","word":"upon this rock","title":"\"What rock? Revelation.\"","text":"Joseph Smith quoted this verse in January 1843 and asked, \"What rock?\" His answer was one word: \"Revelation.\" Peter had confessed Jesus as the Christ, and Jesus said flesh and blood had not revealed it to him.","src":["Joseph Smith, discourse, Nauvoo, January 22, 1843 (History of the Church 5:258)","Matthew 16:17"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 16:19","word":"whatsoever thou shalt bind on earth","title":"The sealing power","text":"In a letter to the Saints, Joseph Smith quoted this verse as the \"precedent\" for baptism for the dead. He wrote that the power to bind on earth and in heaven is \"the power of the priesthood, by the revelation of Jesus Christ,\" and that it reaches the ordinances done for the dead.","src":["D&C 128:8-10"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 11:11","word":"he that is least in the kingdom","title":"Who is \"least\"?","text":"Joseph Smith gave three reasons John was the greatest prophet born of a woman. He prepared the way, he baptized the Son of God, and he was the only legal administrator on earth. As for \"the least,\" Joseph Smith read it as Jesus speaking of himself, the one people counted least: \"that is I myself.\"","src":["Joseph Smith, discourse, Nauvoo, January 29, 1843 (History of the Church 5:260-261)"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 23:35","word":"between the temple and the altar","title":"Zacharias, John's father","text":"Joseph Smith wrote in 1842 that when Herod's order went out to kill the children, Zacharias had Elisabeth take John into the mountains. Zacharias, \"the officiating high priest at the Temple that year,\" refused to tell where the boy was hidden, and \"was slain by Herod's order, between the porch and the altar, as Jesus said.\"","src":["Times and Seasons 3 (September 1, 1842): 902"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Luke 15:2","word":"This man receiveth sinners","title":"The key to the parable","text":"Joseph Smith said the way to understand a parable is to find the question that drew it out. For the prodigal son, that is this verse. The parables that follow answer the Pharisees who murmured that Jesus ate with sinners. \"It is not national,\" he said. It was about men \"in an individual capacity.\"","src":["Joseph Smith, discourse, Nauvoo, January 29, 1843 (History of the Church 5:261-262)"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 13:31","word":"a grain of mustard seed","title":"Mustard seed and the Book of Mormon","text":"In 1835 Joseph Smith wrote that this parable describes \"the Church as it shall come forth in the last days.\" He compared the seed to the Book of Mormon, hidden in a field and coming out of the ground \"accounted the least of all seeds,\" then growing into the greatest of herbs.","src":["Joseph Smith, letter to the elders, Messenger and Advocate, December 1835 (History of the Church 2:268)"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 13:30","word":"Let both grow together until the harvest","title":"Wheat and tares, explained again","text":"A revelation to Joseph Smith explains this parable. \"The field was the world, and the apostles were the sowers.\" After they died, the enemy sowed tares. In the last days the wheat is to be gathered out first. Then \"the tares are bound in bundles, and the field remaineth to be burned.\"","src":["D&C 86:1-7"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 13:47","word":"gathered of every kind","title":"The gospel net","text":"Brigham Young used this parable to explain why the Church had bad members as well as good ones. \"The kingdom of heaven is like unto a net that gathers fish both good and bad,\" he said, and \"the Gospel net must gather them of necessity.\" In the parable the sorting happens at the end of the world.","src":["Brigham Young, November 9, 1856, Journal of Discourses 4:76 | https://jod.mrm.org/4/75/"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 22:30","word":"they neither marry, nor are given in marriage","title":"Marriage after death","text":"A revelation to Joseph Smith explains this saying. A marriage made only \"so long as he is in the world\" is \"not of force when they are dead.\" Those people \"neither marry nor are given in marriage\" after this life. A marriage made by the Lord's word and authority does not end at death.","src":["D&C 132:15-19"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 24:3","word":"the end of the world","title":"The end of the wicked","text":"Joseph Smith's translation of this chapter is printed in the Pearl of Great Price. In it the disciples ask about \"the end of the world, or the destruction of the wicked, which is the end of the world.\" The end of the world means the end of the wicked.","src":["Joseph Smith—Matthew 1:4"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 24:34","word":"This generation shall not pass","title":"Which generation?","text":"Joseph Smith's translation makes this promise clearer: \"this generation, in which these things shall be shown forth, shall not pass away until all I have told you shall be fulfilled.\" The promise is tied to the generation that sees the signs.","src":["Joseph Smith—Matthew 1:34"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Matt 25:1","word":"ten virgins","title":"Who the wise virgins are","text":"A revelation to Joseph Smith says this parable will be fulfilled at the Second Coming, and says who the wise virgins are: \"they that are wise and have received the truth, and have taken the Holy Spirit for their guide, and have not been deceived.\" They \"shall abide the day.\"","src":["D&C 45:56-57"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Luke 23:43","word":"in paradise","title":"The world of spirits","text":"Joseph Smith said the Greek behind \"paradise\" here meant the world of spirits, so the sense is \"This day thou shalt be with me in the world of spirits.\" Alma taught that the righteous rest there in \"a state of happiness, which is called paradise,\" until the resurrection.","src":["Joseph Smith, discourse, Nauvoo, June 11, 1843 (History of the Church 5:424-425)","Alma 40:11-14"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Mark 3:14","word":"he ordained twelve","title":"Special witnesses","text":"A revelation to Joseph Smith calls the Twelve Apostles \"special witnesses of the name of Christ in all the world.\" The Twelve of this dispensation were chosen in 1835 by the Three Witnesses of the Book of Mormon. At Bountiful, Jesus also chose twelve.","src":["D&C 107:23","D&C 18:26-37","3 Nephi 12:1"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Luke 10:1","word":"other seventy also","title":"The Seventy restored","text":"Luke is the only Gospel that tells of the seventy. A revelation to Joseph Smith restores the office: \"The Seventy are also called to preach the gospel, and to be especial witnesses unto the Gentiles and in all the world.\" They work under the direction of the Twelve.","src":["D&C 107:25, 34"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Mark 10:14","word":"Suffer the little children","title":"Little children are whole","text":"Mormon wrote that Christ told him \"little children are whole, for they are not capable of committing sin.\" A revelation to Joseph Smith sets eight years old as the age when children are to be taught to repent and be baptized.","src":["Moroni 8:8-12","D&C 68:25-27"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Mark 10:21","word":"sell whatsoever thou hast","title":"The sacrifice of all things","text":"The Lectures on Faith, given to the elders at Kirtland, say \"a religion that does not require the sacrifice of all things never has power sufficient to produce the faith necessary unto life and salvation.\" Jesus asked the young man to sell everything he had.","src":["Lectures on Faith 6:7 (1835)"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"John 6:35","word":"shall never hunger","title":"Bread for the soul","text":"At Bountiful Jesus used this same promise for the sacrament. \"He that eateth this bread eateth of my body to his soul; and he that drinketh of this wine drinketh of my blood to his soul; and his soul shall never hunger nor thirst, but shall be filled.\"","src":["3 Nephi 20:8"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Mark 14:22","word":"Take, eat","title":"\"Remember this hour\"","text":"Joseph Smith's translation of Mark gives the Savior's words at the table this way: \"Behold, this is for you to do in remembrance of my body; for as oft as ye do this ye will remember this hour that I was with you.\"","src":["Inspired Version, Mark 14:20-21 (1867 text)"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"restoration","ref":"Mark 9:43","word":"if thy hand offend thee","title":"The hand is a brother","text":"Joseph Smith's translation reads this saying as a warning about people. \"If thy hand offend thee, cut it off; or if thy brother offend thee and confess not and forsake not, he shall be cut off.\" The foot becomes \"he that is thy standard, by whom thou walkest.\" It is better to enter life without a brother than to be lost with him.","src":["Inspired Version, Mark 9:40-43 (1867 text)"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Mark 1:1","word":"The beginning of the gospel","title":"Four ways to begin","text":"Each Gospel starts in a different place. Matthew opens with a family tree reaching back to Abraham. Mark opens with John the Baptist in the wilderness. Luke opens with a short letter to a man named Theophilus. John opens \"in the beginning,\" before the world was made, and only then comes to John the Baptist.","src":["Matthew 1:1; Mark 1:1-4; Luke 1:1-4; John 1:1"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Luke 1:2","word":"eyewitnesses","title":"Who saw it, and who asked","text":"Luke does not claim to have been there. He says he gathered what the \"eyewitnesses\" had handed down and then wrote it out in order. John claims the opposite about his own book: \"he that saw it bare record\" (John 19:35). Papias, a bishop writing about AD 120, said Mark wrote down what he heard Peter preach.","src":["Luke 1:1-4; John 19:35; John 21:24","Papias, quoted in Eusebius, Church History 3.39.15 | https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/250103.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Matt 1:16","word":"Jacob begat Joseph","title":"Two family trees","text":"Matthew says Joseph's father was Jacob. Luke says Heli (Luke 3:23). Matthew runs the line through King Solomon; Luke runs it through Nathan, another son of David. Julius Africanus, writing about AD 220, explained it this way: Jacob and Heli were half-brothers, Heli died childless, and Jacob married his widow under the law of Deuteronomy 25:5-6. Joseph was then Jacob's son by birth and Heli's son by law.","src":["Julius Africanus, Letter to Aristides, in Eusebius, Church History 1.7 | https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/250101.htm","Deuteronomy 25:5-6"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Matt 1:20","word":"in a dream","title":"Joseph's side and Mary's side","text":"Matthew tells the birth from Joseph's side. The angel comes to Joseph in dreams, and Joseph makes the choices: to marry Mary, to flee to Egypt, to settle in Nazareth (Matthew 1:20; 2:13; 2:22). Luke tells it from Mary's side: Gabriel's visit, her trip to Elisabeth, her song, and what she \"kept\" in her heart (Luke 2:19). The two accounts hardly overlap at all.","src":["Matthew 1:18-2:23; Luke 1:26-2:52"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Luke 2:39","word":"they returned into Galilee","title":"Where does Egypt fit?","text":"Luke goes straight from the temple at forty days to Nazareth. Matthew has the wise men, the flight into Egypt, and the death of Herod before the family settles in Nazareth (Matthew 2:13-23). Neither Gospel mentions the other's events. The usual way to fit them is that Luke passes over the months Matthew describes, and the move to Nazareth comes after Egypt.","src":["Matthew 2:13-23; Luke 2:22-39","Augustine, Harmony of the Gospels 2.5 | https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1602205.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Matt 2:11","word":"into the house","title":"Shepherds first, wise men later","text":"Luke's shepherds come the night of the birth and find \"the babe lying in a manger\" (Luke 2:16). Matthew's wise men come \"into the house\" and find \"the young child.\" Herod then orders the killing of boys \"from two years old and under,\" by the time the wise men had given him (Matthew 2:16). The wise men likely came months after the shepherds, though no Gospel gives the date.","src":["Matthew 2:11, 16; Luke 2:8-16"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"John 1:21","word":"Art thou Elias?","title":"\"I am not that Elias\"","text":"In the King James Version, John the Baptist answers \"I am not\" when asked if he is Elias, yet Jesus says of him, \"this is Elias\" (Matthew 11:14). Joseph Smith's translation settles it. John \"denied not that he was Elias,\" and when pressed he said, \"I am not that Elias who was to restore all things\" (John 1:21-22 in that translation). He was an Elias, a forerunner, and another was still to come.","src":["Inspired Version (1867), John 1:21-22; Matthew 17:14","Matthew 11:14"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Matt 3:16","word":"he saw the Spirit of God descending","title":"Who saw the dove?","text":"Matthew's \"he saw\" could mean Jesus or John. Mark says Jesus saw it (Mark 1:10). In John's Gospel the Baptist says, \"I saw the Spirit descending\" (John 1:32). Joseph Smith's translation of Matthew reads, \"and John saw, and lo, the heavens were opened unto him\" (3:45), and his Mark adds, \"And John bare record of it\" (1:9). The sign came to Jesus, and John saw it and told it.","src":["Inspired Version (1867), Matthew 3:45; Mark 1:9","Mark 1:10; John 1:32-34"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Matt 3:17","word":"This is my beloved Son","title":"\"This is\" or \"Thou art\"","text":"In Matthew the voice speaks about Jesus: \"This is my beloved Son.\" In Mark and Luke it speaks to him: \"Thou art my beloved Son.\" The meaning is the same. Joseph Smith's translation of Matthew adds \"Hear ye him\" (3:46), the same words the Father speaks at the Transfiguration (Matthew 17:5), so the two times the Father's voice is heard in Matthew end alike.","src":["Inspired Version (1867), Matthew 3:46","Matthew 17:5; Mark 1:11; Luke 3:22","Augustine, Harmony of the Gospels 2.14 | https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1602214.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"John 1:31","word":"I knew him not","title":"Did John know Jesus?","text":"In the King James Version the Baptist says twice, \"I knew him not\" (1:31, 33). Yet in Matthew he recognizes Jesus at once and says, \"I have need to be baptized of thee\" (Matthew 3:14), and Luke makes their mothers relatives (Luke 1:36). Joseph Smith's translation reads \"and I knew him\" in both places (John 1:30, 32), which removes the conflict.","src":["Inspired Version (1867), John 1:30, 32","Matthew 3:14; Luke 1:36"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Luke 3:20","word":"shut up John in prison","title":"Luke finishes John's story first","text":"Luke tells of John the Baptist's arrest in verse 20 and then goes back to Jesus' baptism in verse 21. Mark and Matthew place the arrest later, after the temptation (Mark 1:14; Matthew 4:12). Luke tends to finish one person's story before turning to the next, so his order here groups events by person rather than by date.","src":["Luke 3:19-22; Mark 1:14; Matthew 4:12"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Matt 4:5","word":"the holy city","title":"Which temptation came second?","text":"Matthew's order is bread, then the temple, then the mountain. Luke's is bread, then the mountain, then the temple (Luke 4:5-9). Matthew links them with \"then\" and \"again\"; Luke only says \"and.\" Augustine said it is uncertain which came first and that it changes nothing as long as all three happened. Luke's order does end the scene at Jerusalem, where his whole Gospel is heading.","src":["Matthew 4:1-11; Luke 4:1-13","Augustine, Harmony of the Gospels 2.16 | https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1602216.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"John 2:15","word":"he drove them all out of the temple","title":"The temple cleansing, early or late?","text":"John puts the cleansing of the temple at the start of Jesus' ministry, at the first Passover he mentions (2:13). Matthew, Mark, and Luke put it in the last week, right after the triumphal entry. Augustine concluded that it happened twice. The other option is one cleansing that John placed at the front of his account.","src":["John 2:13-17; Matthew 21:12-13; Mark 11:15-17; Luke 19:45-46","Augustine, Harmony of the Gospels 2.67 | https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1602267.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Mark 11:20","word":"in the morning","title":"The fig tree over two days","text":"Matthew tells the cursing and the withering together: \"presently the fig tree withered away\" (21:19). Mark spreads it over two mornings. Jesus curses the tree, goes on to cleanse the temple, and the next morning the disciples see it \"dried up from the roots.\" Matthew often gathers an event into one scene, and Mark here keeps the day-by-day order.","src":["Mark 11:12-21; Matthew 21:18-20","Augustine, Harmony of the Gospels 2.68 | https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1602268.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"John 1:41","word":"We have found the Messias","title":"The Jordan first, then the lake","text":"In Matthew and Mark, Jesus calls Peter and Andrew at the Sea of Galilee with \"Follow me.\" John tells an earlier meeting by the Jordan, where Andrew, a follower of John the Baptist, brings his brother Simon to Jesus. The two fit together: they met Jesus first in Judea, went home to their nets, and were later called to leave them.","src":["John 1:35-42; Matthew 4:18-20; Mark 1:16-18","Augustine, Harmony of the Gospels 2.17 | https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1602217.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Luke 4:16","word":"he came to Nazareth","title":"Nazareth at the start or later?","text":"Luke puts Jesus' sermon in the Nazareth synagogue at the very start of his work in Galilee. Matthew and Mark tell of a cold welcome at Nazareth much later (Matthew 13:54-58; Mark 6:1-6). Luke's own scene hints that time had passed: the crowd already knows what was \"done in Capernaum\" (4:23). Either Nazareth turned him away twice, or Luke moved the scene forward to open Jesus' ministry.","src":["Luke 4:16-30; Matthew 13:54-58; Mark 6:1-6"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Luke 6:17","word":"stood in the plain","title":"The mount and the plain","text":"Matthew's Sermon on the Mount and Luke's sermon \"in the plain\" share the Beatitudes, love for enemies, the golden rule, and the two builders. Luke's is shorter and adds four woes. The Greek word behind \"plain\" means a level place, and a hillside can have one, so both may describe one sermon. Jesus may also have taught the same things more than once.","src":["Matthew 5-7; Luke 6:17-49","Strong's Greek Dictionary G3977, pedinos, \"level\""],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Luke 6:20","word":"Blessed be ye poor","title":"\"Poor in spirit\" and \"ye poor\"","text":"Matthew has \"Blessed are the poor in spirit\" (5:3). Luke has \"Blessed be ye poor,\" spoken straight to the crowd, and later \"woe unto you that are rich\" (6:24). Matthew's wording names an attitude; Luke's speaks to the people standing there, many of them poor. Both end with the same promise of the kingdom.","src":["Matthew 5:3; Luke 6:20, 24"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Luke 11:2","word":"Our Father which art in heaven","title":"The Lord's Prayer, twice","text":"In Matthew the Lord's Prayer is part of the Sermon on the Mount (6:9-13). In Luke it comes later, when a disciple asks, \"Lord, teach us to pray.\" Luke's version is shorter. The simplest reading is that Jesus taught it at two times to two groups. Matthew's version, and the one in 3 Nephi 13, end with \"For thine is the kingdom\"; Luke's does not.","src":["Matthew 6:9-13; Luke 11:1-4; 3 Nephi 13:9-13"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Luke 7:3","word":"he sent unto him the elders of the Jews","title":"Did the centurion come himself?","text":"Matthew says the centurion \"came unto him.\" Luke says he sent Jewish elders, then friends. Augustine pointed out that we speak this way all the time: a man is said to do what he does through messengers. Jewish law said the same: \"a person's agent has legal status equivalent to his own.\" Matthew tells it in short form; Luke names the messengers.","src":["Augustine, Harmony of the Gospels 2.20 | https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1602220.htm","Mishnah Berakhot 5:5 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Berakhot.5.5"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Matt 8:28","word":"two possessed with devils","title":"One man or two at the tombs","text":"Matthew says two men met Jesus among the tombs. Mark and Luke tell of one, the man called Legion. Augustine's answer: one of the two was better known, and his healing was the one the region talked about, so Mark and Luke name only him. Mark and Luke never say there was only one.","src":["Augustine, Harmony of the Gospels 2.24 | https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1602224.htm","Matthew 8:28-34; Mark 5:1-20; Luke 8:26-39"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Matt 9:18","word":"is even now dead","title":"Dying, or already dead?","text":"In Matthew, Jairus says, \"My daughter is even now dead.\" In Mark and Luke he says she is dying, and messengers arrive later to say she has died (Mark 5:35). Matthew tells the story in about half the words. Augustine said that for brevity Matthew folds the later news into the father's first request.","src":["Matthew 9:18-26; Mark 5:22-43; Luke 8:41-56","Augustine, Harmony of the Gospels 2.28 | https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1602228.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Mark 6:8","word":"save a staff only","title":"Take a staff, or not?","text":"Mark says the Twelve may take \"a staff only.\" Matthew and Luke say not to take staves. One way to fit them: Matthew's verb is \"provide\" (10:9), so the point is not to get new gear for the trip; go with what you have. Augustine offered another: Mark's staff stands for the right Jesus gave them to be fed by the people they taught, since \"the workman is worthy of his meat.\"","src":["Matthew 10:9-10; Mark 6:8-9; Luke 9:3","Augustine, Harmony of the Gospels 2.30 | https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1602230.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Matt 9:9","word":"named Matthew","title":"Matthew, also called Levi","text":"Matthew's own Gospel calls the tax collector Matthew. Mark and Luke tell the same call, at the same tax booth, with the same \"Follow me,\" and name him Levi, son of Alphaeus. Two names for one man were common, as with Simon called Peter and Thomas called Didymus. Only Matthew's Gospel joins the name to \"the publican\" in the list of the Twelve (10:3).","src":["Matthew 9:9; 10:3; Mark 2:14; Luke 5:27; John 20:24","Augustine, Harmony of the Gospels 2.26 | https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1602226.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Luke 6:16","word":"Judas the brother of James","title":"Thaddaeus, Lebbaeus, or Judas?","text":"Matthew and Mark name an apostle Thaddaeus (Matthew adds \"Lebbaeus\"). Luke and Acts have \"Judas of James\" in that place, and John mentions \"Judas, not Iscariot\" (John 14:22). Most readers take these as one man with more than one name. The KJV prints \"the brother\" in italics because the translators added it; the Greek can also mean \"son of James.\"","src":["Matthew 10:3; Mark 3:18; Luke 6:16; Acts 1:13; John 14:22","KJV italics in Luke 6:16 (gospel-harmony/sources/data/kjv.json)"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"John 1:45","word":"Nathanael","title":"Nathanael and Bartholomew","text":"John's Gospel never mentions Bartholomew, and the other three never mention Nathanael. In Matthew, Mark, and Luke, Bartholomew is listed right beside Philip, and in John it is Philip who brings Nathanael to Jesus. \"Bartholomew\" means \"son of Tolmai,\" a family name, so the same man could carry both. No Gospel says outright that they are one person.","src":["John 1:43-51; Matthew 10:3; Mark 3:18; Luke 6:14","Strong's Greek Dictionary G918, Bartholomaios, \"son of Tolmai\""],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"John 6:9","word":"five barley loaves","title":"The miracle all four tell","text":"Apart from the resurrection, the feeding of the five thousand is the only miracle that all four Gospels tell. They agree on five loaves, two fish, about five thousand men, and twelve baskets left over. Only John adds the boy who brought the food, that the loaves were barley, and the names of Philip and Andrew.","src":["Matthew 14:13-21; Mark 6:30-44; Luke 9:10-17; John 6:1-14"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"John 6:5","word":"he saith unto Philip","title":"Why ask Philip?","text":"John does not say where the feeding happened, and he does not explain why Jesus asked Philip where to buy bread. Luke supplies the place: near \"the city called Bethsaida\" (Luke 9:10). John had said earlier that Philip \"was of Bethsaida\" (John 1:44). Jesus asked the man who knew the town. J. J. Blunt made this point in 1847 as an example of two accounts fitting without either trying to.","src":["John 1:44; John 6:5; Luke 9:10","J. J. Blunt, Undesigned Coincidences in the Writings Both of the Old and New Testament (1847)"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Matt 14:29","word":"he walked on the water","title":"Peter on the water","text":"Matthew, Mark, and John all tell of Jesus walking on the lake at night. Only Matthew tells that Peter walked out to him, began to sink, and was caught (14:28-31). Papias said Mark's Gospel came from Peter's preaching, yet it is Matthew, not Mark, who tells this story about Peter.","src":["Matthew 14:22-33; Mark 6:45-52; John 6:16-21","Papias, quoted in Eusebius, Church History 3.39.15 | https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/250103.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Matt 16:16","word":"the Son of the living God","title":"Three wordings of Peter's answer","text":"Matthew: \"Thou art the Christ, the Son of the living God.\" Mark: \"Thou art the Christ.\" Luke: \"The Christ of God.\" All three agree on the heart of the answer. Only Matthew records what Jesus said next: Peter's blessing, the rock, and the keys of the kingdom (16:17-19).","src":["Matthew 16:13-20; Mark 8:27-30; Luke 9:18-21","Augustine, Harmony of the Gospels 2.53 | https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1602253.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Luke 9:28","word":"about an eight days after","title":"Six days or eight?","text":"Matthew and Mark say the Transfiguration came \"after six days.\" Luke says \"about an eight days after.\" Augustine noted that people sometimes count the first and last days and sometimes only the days in between. Six full days between, plus the day of the saying and the day of the climb, makes eight. Luke also says \"about.\"","src":["Augustine, Harmony of the Gospels 2.56 | https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1602256.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Matt 12:40","word":"three days and three nights","title":"Three days, or the third day?","text":"Here Jesus says \"three days and three nights.\" Elsewhere the Gospels say he would rise \"the third day\" (Matthew 16:21). His enemies treated the two as the same: they quoted \"after three days\" and asked for a guard \"until the third day\" (Matthew 27:63-64). In Jewish counting a part of a day or night could be counted as the whole.","src":["Matthew 27:63-64; Matthew 16:21","Jerusalem Talmud Shabbat 9:3 (\"treats part of a term like the whole\") | https://www.sefaria.org/Jerusalem_Talmud_Shabbat.9.3"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Mark 4:12","word":"That seeing they may see","title":"\"That seeing\" or \"because they seeing\"","text":"Mark says Jesus spoke in parables \"that seeing they may see, and not perceive,\" words taken from Isaiah 6:9-10. Matthew says \"because they seeing see not\" (13:13). Joseph Smith's translation of Matthew adds the reason before it: \"whosoever continueth not to receive, from him shall be taken away even that he hath.\" In that reading the hearers' own refusal comes first.","src":["Inspired Version (1867), Matthew 13:10-12","Isaiah 6:9-10; Matthew 13:13-15; Mark 4:11-12"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Matt 19:20","word":"The young man","title":"The \"rich young ruler\"","text":"No one Gospel calls him a rich young ruler. Matthew says he was young. Luke says he was a ruler (18:18). Mark tells how he came running and knelt, and that Jesus, \"beholding him loved him\" (10:17, 21). All three say he had great possessions. The familiar name comes from putting the three accounts together.","src":["Matthew 19:16-22; Mark 10:17-22; Luke 18:18-23"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Matt 20:20","word":"the mother of Zebedee's children","title":"Who asked for the seats?","text":"In Matthew the mother of James and John makes the request. In Mark the brothers ask for themselves (10:35). Matthew says she came \"with her sons,\" and Jesus answers the sons directly: \"Ye know not what ye ask,\" a plural \"ye.\" Augustine read it as the sons' request spoken through their mother.","src":["Matthew 20:20-23; Mark 10:35-40","Augustine, Harmony of the Gospels 2.64 | https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1602264.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Matt 20:30","word":"two blind men","title":"The blind men at Jericho","text":"Matthew has two blind men as Jesus leaves Jericho. Mark has one, Bartimaeus, as Jesus leaves. Luke has one as Jesus comes near the city. In Jesus' day there were two Jerichos about a mile and a half apart: the old town and Herod's newer one, so \"leaving\" one could be \"nearing\" the other. Augustine thought Mark named only the better-known man.","src":["Augustine, Harmony of the Gospels 2.65 | https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1602265.htm","Gary A. Byers, \"On the Jericho Road,\" Bible and Spade 12.2 | https://www.galaxie.com/article/bspade12-2-03","Encyclopedia of the Bible, \"Jericho\" | https://www.biblegateway.com/resources/encyclopedia-of-the-bible/JERICHO"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Matt 21:2","word":"an ass tied, and a colt with her","title":"One animal or two?","text":"Matthew mentions an ass and her colt; Mark, Luke, and John mention only the colt Jesus rode. Matthew quotes Zechariah 9:9, which names \"an ass, and... a colt the foal of an ass.\" Joseph Smith's translation of Matthew has only \"a colt tied,\" and \"Jesus took the colt and sat thereon\" (21:2, 5 in that translation), so it agrees with the other three.","src":["Inspired Version (1867), Matthew 21:2-5","Zechariah 9:9","Augustine, Harmony of the Gospels 2.66 | https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1602266.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Mark 14:3","word":"Simon the leper","title":"The anointing at Bethany","text":"Matthew, Mark, and John tell one anointing at Bethany, with costly spikenard and a complaint about three hundred pence. Matthew and Mark say the woman poured it on his head; John names her Mary and says she anointed his feet. She may have done both. John dates it six days before Passover; Mark places it in his account where it explains why Judas went to the priests.","src":["Matthew 26:6-16; Mark 14:3-11; John 12:1-8","Augustine, Harmony of the Gospels 2.79 | https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1602279.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Luke 7:38","word":"began to wash his feet with tears","title":"A different anointing","text":"Luke's anointing is a separate event. It happens in Galilee, earlier in the ministry, at the house of Simon the Pharisee, and the woman is known as a sinner, and Jesus answers with a parable about forgiveness. The Bethany anointing comes in the last week, at the house of Simon the leper, and Jesus connects it with his burial (Mark 14:8).","src":["Luke 7:36-50; Mark 14:3-9; John 12:1-8"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Luke 22:17","word":"he took the cup","title":"Two cups in Luke","text":"Matthew and Mark mention one cup at the Last Supper. Luke mentions a cup before the bread (22:17) and \"the cup after supper\" (22:20). The Passover meal had several cups of wine; the Mishnah says even the poorest man should be given four. Luke names two of them.","src":["Mishnah Pesachim 10:1 | https://www.sefaria.org/Mishnah_Pesachim.10.1","Luke 22:14-20; Matthew 26:26-29"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"John 13:4","word":"He riseth from supper","title":"John tells the foot washing","text":"Matthew, Mark, and Luke all tell how Jesus gave the bread and the cup. John, writing after them, does not. Instead he tells the washing of the disciples' feet, which none of the others include. John had already given Jesus' long teaching on his flesh and blood as bread, in chapter 6.","src":["John 13:1-17; John 6:48-58; Matthew 26:26-29"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Mark 14:30","word":"before the cock crow twice","title":"Once or twice?","text":"Matthew, Luke, and John say Peter would deny Jesus three times before the cock crowed. Mark says before it crowed twice, and his account has a first crow after the first denial (14:68) and \"the second time\" after the third (14:72). Augustine called Mark's the more exact version of the same warning. Joseph Smith's translation keeps \"twice\" (Mark 14:34 in that translation).","src":["Augustine, Harmony of the Gospels 3.2 | https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1602302.htm","Inspired Version (1867), Mark 14:34, 80-81"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"John 18:17","word":"the damsel that kept the door","title":"Who asked Peter?","text":"The four accounts name different questioners. The first is a maid in all four. After that, Matthew has \"another maid,\" Mark the same maid again, Luke \"another\" man, and John a group by the fire and then a relative of Malchus. Peter stood in a crowded courtyard at night, and more than one voice was pressing him.","src":["Matthew 26:69-75; Mark 14:66-72; Luke 22:54-62; John 18:15-27","Augustine, Harmony of the Gospels 3.6 | https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1602306.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"John 18:10","word":"The servant's name was Malchus","title":"Names only John gives","text":"All four Gospels tell of the sword and the servant's ear. Matthew, Mark, and Luke say only \"one of them.\" John names both men: Simon Peter struck Malchus. Luke, the physician, alone tells that Jesus \"touched his ear, and healed him\" (22:51). John also later says a relative of Malchus questioned Peter (18:26).","src":["Matthew 26:51; Mark 14:47; Luke 22:50-51; John 18:10, 26","Colossians 4:14"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"John 18:5","word":"I am he","title":"The kiss and \"I am he\"","text":"Matthew and Mark say Judas marked Jesus with a kiss. Luke has Judas come near to kiss him, and Jesus asks, \"betrayest thou the Son of man with a kiss?\" John has no kiss. Jesus steps forward and says \"I am he,\" and the soldiers fall back. Augustine placed the kiss first, then \"Whom seek ye?\" and \"I am he,\" so all of it happened.","src":["Matthew 26:47-50; Mark 14:43-46; Luke 22:47-48; John 18:3-8","Augustine, Harmony of the Gospels 3.5 | https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1602305.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"John 18:13","word":"led him away to Annas first","title":"Three hearings before Pilate","text":"Only John tells of a hearing before Annas, the former high priest and father-in-law of Caiaphas. Matthew and Mark tell of a night trial before Caiaphas. Luke tells of the council meeting \"as soon as it was day\" (22:66), and Mark also mentions a morning meeting (15:1). Put together: Annas, then Caiaphas at night, then the full council at dawn.","src":["John 18:12-24; Matthew 26:57-68; Mark 14:53-65; 15:1; Luke 22:54, 66-71"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Luke 23:7","word":"he sent him to Herod","title":"Only Luke knows about Herod","text":"Only Luke tells that Pilate sent Jesus to Herod Antipas. Luke had sources close to Herod's court. He names Joanna, \"the wife of Chuza Herod's steward,\" among the women who followed Jesus (8:3), and in Acts he names Manaen, who was brought up with Herod (Acts 13:1).","src":["Luke 23:6-12; Luke 8:3; Acts 13:1"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"John 19:17","word":"he bearing his cross","title":"Who carried the cross?","text":"John says Jesus went out \"bearing his cross.\" Matthew, Mark, and Luke say Simon of Cyrene was made to carry it. Both are true in order: Jesus started out carrying it, and on the way the soldiers forced Simon to take it. Luke says Simon carried it \"after Jesus\" (23:26).","src":["Augustine, Harmony of the Gospels 3.10 | https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1602310.htm","John 19:17; Luke 23:26"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Mark 15:21","word":"the father of Alexander and Rufus","title":"Simon's sons","text":"Only Mark names Simon of Cyrene's sons, Alexander and Rufus. He names them as if his readers already knew them. Paul greets a Rufus in Rome (Romans 16:13). Whether it is the same man, no one can say.","src":["Mark 15:21; Romans 16:13"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Matt 27:37","word":"THIS IS JESUS THE KING OF THE JEWS","title":"The sign, worded four ways","text":"Matthew: \"THIS IS JESUS THE KING OF THE JEWS.\" Mark: \"THE KING OF THE JEWS.\" Luke: \"THIS IS THE KING OF THE JEWS.\" John: \"JESUS OF NAZARETH THE KING OF THE JEWS.\" John and Luke say it was written in Hebrew, Greek, and Latin. Three languages could mean three slightly different wordings. All four agree on the charge: \"the King of the Jews.\"","src":["Matthew 27:37; Mark 15:26; Luke 23:38; John 19:19-20"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Matt 27:44","word":"The thieves also","title":"Both thieves, or one?","text":"The King James Version of Matthew and Mark says both thieves mocked Jesus. Luke says one mocked and the other rebuked him and asked to be remembered. Joseph Smith's translation of Matthew reads \"One of the thieves,\" and adds that \"the other rebuked him\" (27:47); his Mark also has \"one of them\" (15:37). With that wording, all three agree.","src":["Inspired Version (1867), Matthew 27:47; Mark 15:37","Augustine, Harmony of the Gospels 3.16 | https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1602316.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Mark 15:23","word":"wine mingled with myrrh","title":"Myrrh or gall?","text":"Mark says they offered Jesus \"wine mingled with myrrh.\" Matthew says \"vinegar to drink mingled with gall\" (27:34). Joseph Smith's translation of Mark reads like Matthew: \"vinegar mingled with gall; and when he had tasted the vinegar, he would not drink\" (15:26 in that translation). Augustine suggested both words describe how bitter the drink was.","src":["Inspired Version (1867), Mark 15:26","Augustine, Harmony of the Gospels 3.11 | https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1602311.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Matt 27:50","word":"cried again with a loud voice","title":"The last words","text":"Matthew and Mark record \"My God, my God, why hast thou forsaken me?\" Luke records \"Father, into thy hands I commend my spirit.\" John records \"It is finished.\" Joseph Smith's translation of Matthew gives the final cry in words: \"Father, it is finished, thy will is done\" (27:54 in that translation). It joins Luke's \"Father\" and John's \"It is finished.\"","src":["Inspired Version (1867), Matthew 27:54","Matthew 27:46; Luke 23:46; John 19:30"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Luke 23:47","word":"a righteous man","title":"\"Son of God\" or \"righteous man\"?","text":"In Matthew and Mark the centurion says, \"Truly this was the Son of God.\" In Luke he says, \"Certainly this was a righteous man.\" Luke's Gospel keeps stressing that Jesus was innocent: Pilate says three times he finds no fault in him (23:4, 14, 22). A soldier at the cross could have said both things.","src":["Luke 23:4, 14, 22, 47; Matthew 27:54; Mark 15:39","Augustine, Harmony of the Gospels 3.20 | https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1602320.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Matt 27:5","word":"hanged himself","title":"How Judas died","text":"Matthew says Judas \"hanged himself.\" Acts says that \"falling headlong, he burst asunder in the midst\" (Acts 1:18). Joseph Smith's translation of Matthew joins the two: he \"hanged himself on a tree. And straightway he fell down, and his bowels gushed out, and he died\" (27:6 in that translation).","src":["Inspired Version (1867), Matthew 27:6","Acts 1:18"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Luke 23:45","word":"the veil of the temple was rent","title":"When the veil tore","text":"Matthew and Mark tell of the temple veil tearing right after Jesus died. Luke tells it just before his last cry, together with the darkness. Each writer lists the signs in the order that suited his account; none of them says \"and then.\" Augustine thought Luke put the torn veil next to the darkened sun so the two signs would be told together.","src":["Matthew 27:50-51; Mark 15:37-38; Luke 23:44-46","Augustine, Harmony of the Gospels 3.19 | https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1602319.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"John 19:25","word":"there stood by the cross","title":"Near the cross or far off?","text":"Matthew, Mark, and Luke say the women watched \"afar off.\" John says his mother and the other women \"stood by the cross,\" close enough for Jesus to speak to her. Augustine suggested the women stood near the cross while Jesus spoke to his mother, then drew back from the crowd. The others mention the women only after his death, when they were watching from farther away.","src":["Augustine, Harmony of the Gospels 3.21 | https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1602321.htm","John 19:25-27; Matthew 27:55-56"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"John 19:35","word":"he that saw it bare record","title":"John stops to say he saw it","text":"Only John tells that the soldiers broke the thieves' legs but not Jesus', and that a spear brought out \"blood and water.\" At this point John stops the story to say that the one who saw it is the one telling it, and that his record is true.","src":["John 19:31-37; John 21:24"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"John 19:39","word":"Nicodemus","title":"Spices on Friday and Sunday","text":"John says Nicodemus brought about a hundred pounds of myrrh and aloes, and the body was wrapped with spices before the Sabbath. Luke and Mark say the women prepared or bought spices to come back after the Sabbath. The burial was rushed on Friday evening, and the women planned to finish the work themselves.","src":["John 19:38-42; Luke 23:55-56; Mark 16:1","Augustine, Harmony of the Gospels 3.23 | https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1602323.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Matt 28:1","word":"Mary Magdalene","title":"Which women came?","text":"Matthew names Mary Magdalene and \"the other Mary.\" Mark adds Salome. Luke adds Joanna and \"other women.\" John names only Mary Magdalene, but she reports \"we know not where they have laid him\" (John 20:2), as if others were with her. Mary Magdalene is in every list.","src":["Matthew 28:1; Mark 16:1; Luke 24:10; John 20:1-2"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Mark 16:2","word":"at the rising of the sun","title":"Dark or sunrise?","text":"John says Mary came \"when it was yet dark.\" Mark says the women came \"at the rising of the sun.\" Matthew says \"as it began to dawn,\" and Luke \"very early in the morning.\" The women likely set out in the dark and reached the tomb as the sun came up.","src":["Matthew 28:1; Mark 16:2; Luke 24:1; John 20:1"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Matt 28:2","word":"the angel of the Lord descended from heaven","title":"One angel or two?","text":"In the King James Version, Matthew and Mark tell of one angel or \"young man,\" while Luke and John tell of two. Joseph Smith's translation has two angels in all four: Matthew \"two angels of the Lord descended\" (28:2), Mark \"two angels sitting thereon\" (16:3), Luke \"two angels standing by it\" (24:2), and John \"two angels sitting thereon\" (20:1).","src":["Inspired Version (1867), Matthew 28:2; Mark 16:3; Luke 24:2; John 20:1"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Luke 24:12","word":"Then arose Peter","title":"Peter alone, or with John?","text":"Luke names only Peter running to the tomb. John says Peter and \"that other disciple\" ran together, and the other got there first (John 20:3-4). Luke himself knows more than one went: the Emmaus disciples say \"certain of them which were with us went to the sepulchre\" (24:24).","src":["Luke 24:12, 24; John 20:3-8"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Matt 28:16","word":"into Galilee","title":"Galilee or Jerusalem?","text":"Matthew and Mark point to Galilee for the meeting with the risen Lord. Luke keeps every appearance in and near Jerusalem and has Jesus tell them to \"tarry ye in the city\" (24:49). John tells of both: appearances in Jerusalem (chapter 20) and by the Sea of Galilee (chapter 21). Paul lists more still (1 Corinthians 15:5-8). There were forty days for all of them (Acts 1:3).","src":["Matthew 28:7, 16; Mark 16:7; Luke 24:33-49; John 20-21; 1 Corinthians 15:5-8; Acts 1:3","Augustine, Harmony of the Gospels 3.25 | https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1602325.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Luke 24:51","word":"carried up into heaven","title":"One day or forty?","text":"Read alone, Luke 24 seems to put the resurrection, the walk to Emmaus, and the ascension all on one day. The same writer says in Acts that Jesus showed himself \"forty days\" first (Acts 1:3). Luke's Gospel compresses the time; his second book fills it in. Matthew and John end without telling of the ascension.","src":["Luke 24:13-51; Acts 1:1-3"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Luke 24:33","word":"the eleven","title":"\"The eleven\" with one missing","text":"Luke says the Emmaus pair found \"the eleven\" gathered. John says Thomas was not with them that night (20:24). \"The eleven\" was the group's name after Judas, whether or not all were in the room. Paul uses \"the twelve\" the same way after Judas was gone (1 Corinthians 15:5).","src":["Luke 24:33; John 20:24; 1 Corinthians 15:5"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Mark 16:12","word":"he appeared in another form unto two of them","title":"Mark's one line about Emmaus","text":"Luke tells the walk to Emmaus at length (24:13-35). Mark gives it one sentence: Jesus \"appeared in another form unto two of them, as they walked,\" and when they told the others, they were not believed. \"Another form\" matches Luke's detail that \"their eyes were holden that they should not know him\" (24:16).","src":["Mark 16:12-13; Luke 24:13-35"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"John 4:2","word":"Though Jesus himself baptized not","title":"Did Jesus baptize?","text":"John 3:22 says Jesus \"tarried with them, and baptized.\" The King James Version of John 4:2 then says \"Jesus himself baptized not, but his disciples.\" Joseph Smith's translation reads that Jesus \"baptized not so many as his disciples; For he suffered them for an example, preferring one another\" (John 4:3-4). Jesus did baptize, and he let his disciples do most of it.","src":["Inspired Version (1867), John 4:1-4","John 3:22"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"John 3:24","word":"For John was not yet cast into prison","title":"John's note on timing","text":"Mark begins Jesus' preaching in Galilee \"after that John was put in prison\" (Mark 1:14). John's Gospel adds a short note here so readers will not be confused: these events in Judea happened before that. John seems to expect his readers already knew the order the other Gospels give.","src":["John 3:22-24; Mark 1:14; Matthew 4:12"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"John 2:13","word":"the Jews' passover was at hand","title":"How long was the ministry?","text":"Matthew, Mark, and Luke mention only one Passover in Jesus' ministry, the last one. John names three (2:13; 6:4; 11:55) and other feasts besides. That is where the common figure of about three years comes from. The first three Gospels never say how long the ministry lasted.","src":["John 2:13; 5:1; 6:4; 7:2; 10:22; 11:55"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"John 11:53","word":"they took counsel together for to put him to death","title":"What set the plot in motion","text":"Only John tells the raising of Lazarus, and in John that miracle is what moves the council to plan Jesus' death; they even plotted to kill Lazarus (12:10). In Mark, the turning point is the cleansing of the temple: \"the scribes and chief priests heard it, and sought how they might destroy him\" (11:18). The two events fall in the same few days.","src":["John 11:45-53; 12:9-11; Mark 11:15-18; Luke 19:45-47"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Luke 10:38","word":"a certain village","title":"Martha's village, named by John","text":"Luke does not name the village where Martha and Mary lived. John does: Bethany, near Jerusalem (11:1, 18). The two writers describe the sisters the same way. In Luke, Martha is busy \"about much serving\" while Mary listens. In John, at the supper in Bethany, \"Martha served,\" and Mary is at Jesus' feet with the ointment (12:2-3).","src":["Luke 10:38-42; John 11:1, 18; 12:1-3"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Luke 7:12","word":"the only son of his mother","title":"Three raisings, three witnesses","text":"The Gospels tell of three people Jesus raised from the dead before his own resurrection. Jairus's daughter is in Matthew, Mark, and Luke. The widow's son at Nain is only in Luke. Lazarus is only in John. No single Gospel tells all three.","src":["Mark 5:35-43; Luke 7:11-17; John 11:1-44"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Luke 5:6","word":"they inclosed a great multitude of fishes","title":"Two great catches","text":"Luke tells of a huge catch of fish when Jesus first called Peter, James, and John (5:1-11). John tells of another after the resurrection (21:1-11). Both follow a night of catching nothing, and in both Peter reacts first. In Luke the net breaks; in John it does not (21:11).","src":["Luke 5:1-11; John 21:1-11"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Matt 15:32","word":"I have compassion on the multitude","title":"Two feedings","text":"Matthew and Mark tell of two feedings: five thousand with five loaves, and four thousand with seven. Some readers have wondered if they are one event told twice. Jesus himself names both, with their own numbers of loaves and baskets: \"the five loaves of the five thousand\" and \"the seven loaves of the four thousand\" (Matthew 16:9-10).","src":["Matthew 15:32-39; 16:9-10; Mark 8:1-9, 19-20","Augustine, Harmony of the Gospels 2.50 | https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1602250.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Matt 24:15","word":"the abomination of desolation","title":"Luke explains the abomination","text":"Matthew and Mark speak of \"the abomination of desolation.\" Luke puts it in plain words: \"Jerusalem compassed with armies\" (21:20). Joseph Smith's translation of Matthew 24 says it twice: once \"concerning the destruction of Jerusalem\" (24:14), and again as something to be \"fulfilled\" in the last days (24:37).","src":["Inspired Version (1867), Matthew 24:14, 37","Luke 21:20; Mark 13:14"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Matt 25:15","word":"five talents","title":"Talents and pounds","text":"Matthew's parable of the talents and Luke's parable of the pounds (Luke 19:12-27) sound alike, but the details differ: three servants or ten, talents or pounds, and in Luke a nobleman whose citizens hate him. Luke says Jesus told his near Jerusalem, because people thought the kingdom would come at once (19:11). Matthew's comes on the Mount of Olives. Jesus likely told two parables.","src":["Matthew 25:14-30; Luke 19:11-27; Matthew 24:3"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Matt 22:2","word":"a certain king, which made a marriage for his son","title":"A wedding feast and a great supper","text":"Matthew's wedding feast and Luke's great supper (Luke 14:16-24) both tell of guests who refuse and strangers brought in from the roads. The settings differ: Luke's is told at a Pharisee's table on a Sabbath (14:1); Matthew's in the temple during the last week. Only Matthew has the man without a wedding garment. Augustine judged them two separate parables.","src":["Matthew 22:1-14; Luke 14:1, 15-24","Augustine, Harmony of the Gospels 2.71 | https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/1602271.htm"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Luke 15:4","word":"an hundred sheep","title":"One lost sheep, two audiences","text":"Luke tells the lost sheep to answer Pharisees who complained that Jesus ate with sinners (15:2). Matthew has it in teaching to the disciples about \"these little ones\" (18:10-14). The story is nearly the same; the listeners differ. A teacher can use the same story twice for different hearers.","src":["Luke 15:1-7; Matthew 18:10-14"],"status":"verified"},{"type":"witnesses","ref":"Mark 14:39","word":"spake the same words","title":"How many prayers in the garden?","text":"Matthew and Mark say Jesus left the three disciples and prayed three times, coming back to find them asleep each time (Mark 14:41, \"the third time\"). Luke tells one prayer, adds the angel who strengthened him, and says they slept \"for sorrow\" (22:43-45). John goes straight to the arrest. Each tells part of the same hour.","src":["Matthew 26:36-46; Mark 14:32-42; Luke 22:39-46; John 18:1-3"],"status":"verified"}];
